/* Pearson Edexcel GCSE Mathematics (1MA1) — room kits built by hand in a Claude Code session: written by
   Opus 5 subagents to the contract in src/kit-validator.js, every question re-solved and the lesson read against
   the specification map by a fresh Opus 5 subagent before shipping. See .claude/skills/course-builder/references/depth.md. */
module.exports = { ID: 'EDX-1MA1', KITS: {"number-structure":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"number-structure","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Every one of the three 1MA1 papers opens on number, and Number carries 22–28% of the Foundation marks. This room holds the machinery the rest of the course runs on: ordering, the four operations, priority of operations, primes and the HCF and LCM, systematic listing, powers and roots, exact answers and standard form. Paper 1 is non-calculator, so the written method and the exact form of the answer are both credited, and a bare answer can lose every method mark. Almost every later topic — ratio, area, probability — is arithmetic wearing a different hat, so a slip here costs marks in rooms that have nothing to do with number.","idea":[{"h":"Ordering numbers and the inequality symbols","t":"Order integers, decimals and fractions by first putting them all in one form, usually decimals, then comparing place value by place value. With negatives, the further a number is from zero the smaller it is, so −7/5 < −1.2. Write the comparison with =, ≠, <, >, ≤ or ≥, reading the symbol as pointing at the smaller number. Examiners ask for a list 'smallest first' or for the right symbol between two values, and a list written in the wrong direction scores nothing.","code":"N1"},{"h":"The four operations and place value","t":"Add, subtract, multiply and divide integers, decimals, proper and improper fractions and mixed numbers, positive and negative, by formal written methods. With decimals, multiply as whole numbers and then count the decimal places: 5.3 × 0.24 has three places altogether, so 1272 becomes 1.272. Subtracting a negative adds. Fractions are added over a common denominator once any mixed number has been written as an improper fraction. On Paper 1 the written method is what carries the method marks.","code":"N2"},{"h":"Inverse operations and priority of operations","t":"Brackets first, then indices (powers and roots), then division and multiplication left to right, then addition and subtraction left to right. A calculator obeys this order too, so the calculation has to be written down correctly in the first place. Each operation is undone by its inverse — + by −, × by ÷, a power by its root — which is how a division is checked and how a calculation is simplified by cancelling before it is worked out.","code":"N3"},{"h":"Primes, factors, multiples, HCF and LCM","t":"A prime has exactly two factors, itself and 1. Every integer above 1 has one prime factorisation and no other — the unique factorisation theorem — written in product notation, for example 720 = 2⁴ × 3² × 5. From two factorisations the HCF is the product of the lowest power of each shared prime and the LCM the product of the highest power of every prime that appears. Exam questions hide these behind contexts: identical bags or rows want the HCF, two events meeting again want the LCM.","code":"N4"},{"h":"Systematic listing strategies","t":"To count every possibility without missing or repeating one, hold one choice fixed and run through the next in a set order, then move the fixed choice on: cheese–tea–flapjack, cheese–tea–brownie, cheese–juice–flapjack, and so on. The marks are for the system, not for the total alone, so the list itself is written out and its order is visible. An unordered scribble that happens to reach the right total is not a complete answer.","code":"N5"},{"h":"Positive integer powers and real roots","t":"A power is repeated multiplication and a root undoes it: ∛64 = 4 because 4³ = 64. Recognise the powers of 2 (2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128), of 3 (3, 9, 27, 81, 243), of 4 (4, 16, 64, 256) and of 5 (5, 25, 125, 625) on sight, because on Paper 1 a question expects them to be written down rather than worked out. The squares up to 15² = 225 and the cubes of 2, 3, 4 and 5 are worth knowing for the same reason.","code":"N6"},{"h":"Calculating with roots and integer indices","t":"Powers of the same base multiply by adding the indices and divide by subtracting them, so 7⁵ × 7³ ÷ 7⁶ = 7⁸ ÷ 7⁶ = 7². A zero index gives 1, and a negative index means the reciprocal, so 5⁻² = 1/5² = 1/25. Roots can be combined within a calculation — √72 ÷ √2 = √36 = 6 — which turns an awkward pair of roots into a whole number. The index form, not a rounded decimal, is usually what the accuracy mark is for.","code":"N7"},{"h":"Exact calculation with fractions and multiples of π","t":"An exact answer is a fraction in its lowest terms or a multiple of π, not a rounded decimal. A circle of radius 6 cm has area π × 6² = 36π cm², and 36π cm² is exact where 113.1 cm² is not. Multiples of π are handled like any other multiple: 3 × 5π = 15π. Paper 1 is non-calculator, so questions there ask for the exact form, and a decimal loses the accuracy mark even when it has been correctly rounded.","code":"N8"},{"h":"Standard form","t":"Standard form is A × 10ⁿ, where A is at least 1 and less than 10 and n is an integer; n is positive for large numbers and negative for small ones, so 0.000 000 065 = 6.5 × 10⁻⁸. To multiply, multiply the A values and add the indices, then adjust the answer back into standard form: 24 × 10¹ is rewritten as 2.4 × 10². To compare two numbers written in standard form, look at the power of 10 first and only then at A.","code":"N9"}],"examples":[{"title":"Comparing signed temperatures","setup":"A weather station logs four overnight temperatures: Monday −2.5 °C, Tuesday −9/4 °C, Wednesday 0.8 °C and Thursday −3/5 °C. Name the warmest night and the coldest night, and then write the correct symbol, < or >, between Monday's reading and Tuesday's reading.","steps":["Change each fraction into a decimal so that all four readings are in one form: −9/4 = −2.25 and −3/5 = −0.6.","Every negative reading is below every positive one, and Wednesday alone is positive, so Wednesday at 0.8 °C was the warmest night.","Of −2.5, −2.25 and −0.6, the reading furthest below zero is −2.5, so Monday was the coldest night.","Monday is −2.5 and Tuesday is −2.25, so Monday's is the smaller; the symbol points at the smaller value, giving −2.5 < −9/4."],"cues":["Are the four readings all written in the same form yet?","How does any negative reading compare with a positive one?","Which position on a thermometer counts as the coldest?","Which value does an inequality symbol point at?"]},{"title":"Checking and undoing with inverse operations","setup":"A stallholder shares a bill of £966 equally between 23 traders and writes down £42 for each of them. Use an inverse operation to decide whether £42 is right, and then find the number that makes 6 × ☐ + 9 = 87 true by undoing the operations in turn.","steps":["The inverse of dividing by 23 is multiplying by 23, so the share is tested with 42 × 23.","42 × 23 = 966, which is the whole bill, so the share of £42 each is right.","In 6 × ☐ + 9 the addition is done last, so it is undone first: 87 − 9 = 78, leaving 6 × ☐ = 78.","Undoing the multiplication as well, 78 ÷ 6 = 13, so the missing number is 13."],"cues":["How can a division be tested without dividing again?","Does the test come back to the whole bill?","Which operation is applied last in 6 × ☐ + 9?","Which operation is still left to undo?"]},{"title":"Prime factorisation, HCF and LCM","setup":"Two numbers are 90 and 126. Without a calculator, write each of them as a product of powers of its prime factors, then use those two factorisations to work out the highest common factor and the lowest common multiple of the pair.","steps":["Divide 90 by primes in turn: 90 = 2 × 45 = 2 × 3 × 15 = 2 × 3 × 3 × 5, so 90 = 2 × 3² × 5.","Do the same for the other: 126 = 2 × 63 = 2 × 3 × 21 = 2 × 3 × 3 × 7, so 126 = 2 × 3² × 7.","HCF: take the lowest power of each prime that is in both lists, 2 and 3², so the HCF = 2 × 9 = 18.","LCM: take the highest power of every prime that appears, 2, 3², 5 and 7, so the LCM = 2 × 9 × 5 × 7 = 630."],"cues":["Which prime is worth trying first on an even number?","Which primes would you expect this second number to share?","For a common factor, is the lower or the higher power needed?","For a common multiple, which primes must be included?"]},{"title":"Listing every possibility in order","setup":"A café meal deal is one sandwich chosen from cheese, ham and tuna, one drink chosen from tea and juice, and one cake chosen from a flapjack and a brownie. By listing the possible deals in a systematic order, work out how many different meal deals there are, and how many of them contain juice.","steps":["Hold cheese and tea, and vary the cake: cheese–tea–flapjack, cheese–tea–brownie.","Keep cheese and move the drink on: cheese–juice–flapjack, cheese–juice–brownie, so cheese gives 4 deals.","Repeat the same four for ham: ham–tea–flapjack, ham–tea–brownie, ham–juice–flapjack, ham–juice–brownie.","Tuna gives four in the same way, so the total is 4 + 4 + 4 = 12 different meal deals.","Juice appears in 2 of the 4 deals for each sandwich, so 2 + 2 + 2 = 6 deals contain juice."],"cues":["Which choice is being held still while another varies?","What has to change next once the cakes are used up?","Why will ham produce the same number as cheese?","How many sandwiches are there to account for?","In each sandwich's list, how many deals had juice?"]},{"title":"Indices and roots without a calculator","setup":"Without a calculator, work out the value of 2⁴ × 2³ ÷ 2⁵ as a whole number, and also the value of √72 ÷ √2.","steps":["The multiplication adds the indices: 2⁴ × 2³ = 2⁷.","The division subtracts the index: 2⁷ ÷ 2⁵ = 2⁷⁻⁵ = 2², which is 4.","For the roots, combine them into a single root: √72 ÷ √2 = √(72 ÷ 2).","Divide inside the root: 72 ÷ 2 = 36, and √36 = 6."],"cues":["What happens to the indices when powers of one base multiply?","And what happens to the indices when they divide?","Can a division of two roots be written as one root?","Is the number now under the root a square number?"]},{"title":"Multiplying in standard form","setup":"A single grain of sand has a mass of 6 × 10⁻⁵ kg. A bucket holds 4 × 10⁶ grains of sand. Without a calculator, work out the total mass of the sand in the bucket, giving your answer in kilograms in standard form.","steps":["Multiply the number parts: 6 × 4 = 24.","Multiply the powers of ten by adding the indices: 10⁻⁵ × 10⁶ = 10¹.","Put the two together: 24 × 10¹, and 24 is not between 1 and 10, so this is not yet standard form.","Rewrite 24 as 2.4 × 10, giving 2.4 × 10 × 10¹ = 2.4 × 10² kg."],"cues":["Which two parts of the numbers can be multiplied separately?","What do you do with indices when powers of ten multiply?","Does the front number obey the rule for standard form?","How does moving the decimal point change the index?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Compare −0.4 and −2/5 using the correct symbol.","a":"−0.4 = −2/5, because −2/5 written as a decimal is −0.4"},{"q":"Work out 20 − 4 × 3 + 2³ without a calculator.","a":"16 (2³ = 8, then 20 − 12 + 8)"},{"q":"Write 48 as a product of powers of its prime factors.","a":"2⁴ × 3"},{"q":"Write 3.6 × 10³ as an ordinary number.","a":"3600"}]},"room":{"facts":["Standard form is A × 10ⁿ with 1 ≤ A < 10 and n an integer; a number smaller than 1 has a negative n.","Laws of indices: aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ, aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ, a⁰ = 1 and a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ.","Priority of operations: brackets, then indices, then division and multiplication left to right, then addition and subtraction left to right.","HCF = the product of the lowest power of each prime the numbers share; LCM = the product of the highest power of every prime that appears in either.","Powers of 2: 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128. Powers of 3: 3, 9, 27, 81, 243. Powers of 4: 4, 16, 64, 256. Powers of 5: 5, 25, 125, 625. Cubes: 2³ = 8, 3³ = 27, 4³ = 64, 5³ = 125.","To multiply in standard form, multiply the A values, add the indices of 10, then adjust so that 1 ≤ A < 10.","Subtracting a negative adds: a − (−b) = a + b; and multiplying two negatives gives a positive."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Write these five numbers in order, starting with the smallest: −1.2, 3/5, −7/5, 0.55, 1/2","a":"−7/5, −1.2, 1/2, 0.55, 3/5","sol":"Write the fractions as decimals: 3/5 = 0.6, −7/5 = −1.4 and 1/2 = 0.5 (M1). The negatives come first and −1.4 < −1.2; then 0.5 < 0.55 < 0.6, so the order is −7/5, −1.2, 1/2, 0.55, 3/5 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Numbers are only easy to compare when they are all written in the same form.","Divide the top of each fraction by the bottom to get a decimal, then compare place value by place value.","Once everything is a decimal, the two negative values are placed first, and the one further from zero is the smaller of them."],"codes":["N1"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out 4.6 × 0.35","a":"1.61","sol":"Multiply as whole numbers by a formal written method: 46 × 35 = 1610 (M1). There is one decimal place in 4.6 and two in 0.35, three altogether, so 1610 becomes 1.61 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The digits of the answer do not depend on where the decimal points are.","Multiply the two numbers as whole numbers first, then decide where the decimal point belongs.","Work out 46 × 35 by a formal written method, then count how many decimal places the two original numbers have between them."],"codes":["N2"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out 30 − 2 × (8 − 3)² ÷ 5","a":"20","sol":"Brackets first: 8 − 3 = 5, then the index: 5² = 25 (M1). Multiplication and division left to right: 2 × 25 = 50 and 50 ÷ 5 = 10; the subtraction is last, 30 − 10 = 20 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["There is a fixed order for these operations, and it is not simply left to right.","Brackets, then the power, then the multiplication and division left to right, and the subtraction last of all.","Evaluate the bracket first, and square the result of that before anything else happens."],"codes":["N3"]},{"q":"Write 84 as a product of powers of its prime factors. A calculator may be used.","a":"2² × 3 × 7","sol":"Divide by primes in turn: 84 = 2 × 42 = 2 × 2 × 21 = 2 × 2 × 3 × 7 (M1). Collecting the repeated prime in product notation gives 84 = 2² × 3 × 7 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Every whole number above 1 is built from primes in exactly one way.","Divide by the smallest prime that goes in exactly, then keep dividing each quotient by a prime until you reach 1.","84 is even, so begin by dividing by 2 and carry on from the quotient; collect any repeated prime as a power at the end."],"codes":["N4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out the value of 2⁵ ÷ 2³ + √81 − ∛27","a":"10","sol":"Divide the powers by subtracting the indices: 2⁵ ÷ 2³ = 2² = 4 (M1). √81 = 9 and ∛27 = 3 (B1). Adding and subtracting left to right, 4 + 9 − 3 = 10 (A1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The two powers share a base, so that division can be done in index form.","Divide the powers by subtracting the indices, work out each root, then add and subtract left to right.","2⁵ ÷ 2³ becomes a single power of 2; √81 asks which number squared gives 81, and ∛27 which number cubed gives 27."],"codes":["N6","N7"]},{"q":"A virus particle measures 0.000 000 12 m across. Write this measurement in standard form. A calculator may be used.","a":"1.2 × 10⁻⁷ m","sol":"The digits give a number between 1 and 10: 1.2 (M1). The decimal point has to move 7 places to the right from 0.000 000 12 to reach 1.2, and the measurement is smaller than 1, so n = −7 and the answer is 1.2 × 10⁻⁷ m (A1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Standard form needs exactly one non-zero digit in front of the decimal point.","Write the digits as a number between 1 and 10, then count how many places the decimal point has to move.","Count the places between the decimal point in the measurement and the position just after its first non-zero digit; a number smaller than 1 takes a negative index."],"codes":["N9"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out 4⁻², giving your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms.","a":"1/16","sol":"A negative index means the reciprocal of the matching positive power: 4⁻² = 1/4² (M1). 4² = 16, so 4⁻² = 1/16 (A1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["A negative index does not make the answer a negative number.","A negative index means take the reciprocal of the matching positive power.","Rewrite the expression as 1 divided by the matching positive power of 4, then evaluate that power."],"codes":["N7"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Ali makes two-digit numbers using two different digits chosen from 3, 5 and 7. By listing them in a systematic order, find (a) how many such two-digit numbers there are, and (b) the largest of them.","a":"(a) 6   (b) 75","sol":"Hold the tens digit and vary the units digit: with 3 in the tens, 35 and 37 (M1). With 5 in the tens, 53 and 57; with 7 in the tens, 73 and 75 (M1). The list is complete with no repeats, so (a) there are 6 numbers and (b) the largest is 75 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Each number uses two different digits, so no digit may be repeated within it.","Hold the tens digit still and run through the possible units digits, then move the tens digit on.","Write down the numbers whose tens digit is the smallest of the three first, then those with the next tens digit, and so on."],"codes":["N5"]},{"q":"Buses to Leeds leave a stop every 12 minutes and buses to York leave the same stop every 18 minutes. Both leave together at 09:00. Work out the time at which they next leave together. A calculator may be used.","a":"09:36","sol":"They leave together again after a number of minutes that is a multiple of both gaps, so the lowest common multiple is needed (M1). 12 = 2² × 3 and 18 = 2 × 3², so the LCM = 2² × 3² = 36 minutes (A1). 09:00 plus 36 minutes is 09:36 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["They meet again at a time that is a whole number of gaps along both routes.","Find the lowest common multiple of the two gaps in minutes, then add it to the starting time.","Write each gap as a product of prime powers, then take the highest power of every prime that appears."],"codes":["N4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A circle has a radius of 7 cm. Work out its area, giving your answer as a multiple of π. The formulae sheet printed with the paper gives area of a circle = πr².","a":"49π cm²","sol":"Substitute r = 7 into the printed formula: area = π × 7² (M1). 7² = 49, so the exact area is 49π cm² (A1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The formulae sheet printed with the paper already gives the area of a circle.","Substitute the radius into πr² and leave π in the answer instead of multiplying it out.","Square the radius first, then write that result in front of π with the correct square unit."],"codes":["N8"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out 2/5 + 1 1/4, giving your answer as a mixed number in its lowest terms.","a":"1 13/20","sol":"Write the mixed number as an improper fraction: 1 1/4 = 5/4 (B1). Use the common denominator 20: 2/5 = 8/20 and 5/4 = 25/20 (M1). 8/20 + 25/20 = 33/20 = 1 13/20 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A mixed number is much easier to add once it is written as a single fraction.","Change the mixed number to an improper fraction, then rewrite both fractions over a common denominator.","The denominators are 5 and 4, so use their lowest common multiple as the common denominator."],"codes":["N2","N8"]},{"q":"A gardener has 60 tulip bulbs and 84 daffodil bulbs. She plants them all in identical rows, every row holding the same number of tulips and the same number of daffodils, with none left over, and she wants as many rows as possible. Work out (a) the number of rows she makes and (b) the number of tulip bulbs in each row. A calculator may be used.","a":"(a) 12 rows   (b) 5 tulip bulbs","sol":"The number of rows must divide both 60 and 84 exactly, so the greatest possible number of rows is their highest common factor (M1). 60 = 2² × 3 × 5 and 84 = 2² × 3 × 7 (M1). The lowest power of each shared prime gives HCF = 2² × 3 = 12, so (a) she makes 12 rows (A1). Each row then holds 60 ÷ 12 = 5 tulip bulbs (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Every row is identical and no bulbs at all are left over.","The number of rows has to divide both totals exactly, and the greatest number that does is the highest common factor.","Write both totals as products of prime powers, then take the lowest power of each prime they share."],"codes":["N4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Ellie has a £2 coin, a 50p coin, a 20p coin and a 10p coin. She chooses exactly two of the four coins. By listing the possible pairs in a systematic order, work out (a) how many different pairs she can choose and (b) the total value of the most valuable pair.","a":"(a) 6 pairs   (b) £2.50","sol":"Hold the £2 coin and pair it with each of the others: £2 and 50p, £2 and 20p, £2 and 10p (M1). Then hold the 50p: 50p and 20p, 50p and 10p; then the 20p: 20p and 10p (M1). The list has no repeats, so (a) 3 + 2 + 1 = 6 different pairs (A1). The most valuable pair is the two largest coins, £2 + 50p = £2.50 (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Two coins are chosen from four, and the order in which she picks them up does not matter.","Hold the most valuable coin and pair it with each of the others in turn, then move on to the next coin down.","List the pairs that contain the £2 coin first, then the pairs that contain the 50p coin but not the £2 coin, and so on."],"codes":["N5","N2"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Three stars are 4.1 × 10¹³ km, 9.5 × 10¹² km and 3.8 × 10¹³ km from Earth. Work out (a) the three distances in order, nearest first, and (b) how much further away the furthest star is than the nearest, in km in standard form.","a":"(a) 9.5 × 10¹² km, 3.8 × 10¹³ km, 4.1 × 10¹³ km   (b) 3.15 × 10¹³ km","sol":"Compare the powers of 10 first: 10¹² is smaller than 10¹³, so 9.5 × 10¹² is the nearest; among the other two, 3.8 < 4.1 (M1). So (a) the order is 9.5 × 10¹² km, 3.8 × 10¹³ km, 4.1 × 10¹³ km (A1). To subtract, write both with the same power of 10: 9.5 × 10¹² = 0.95 × 10¹³ (M1). Then 4.1 × 10¹³ − 0.95 × 10¹³ = 3.15 × 10¹³ km (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["In standard form the power of 10 decides the size before the number in front of it does.","Compare the indices first, and only compare the front numbers when the indices are equal.","For the subtraction, rewrite the smaller distance so that it has the same power of 10 as the larger one."],"codes":["N9","N1"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A number n is given by n = 2³ × 3² × 5. Work out (a) the value of n and (b) the smallest whole number that n must be multiplied by so that the product is a square number.","a":"(a) 360   (b) 10","sol":"Evaluate the powers: 2³ = 8 and 3² = 9, so (a) n = 8 × 9 × 5 = 360 (B1). A square number has an even index for every prime in its factorisation, and here the indices of 2 and of 5 are odd (M1). Multiplying by 2 × 5 gives 2⁴ × 3² × 5² = 3600 = 60², so (b) the smallest multiplier is 10 (A1).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["A square number is one whose square root is a whole number.","Evaluate the powers to get n, then look at whether each index in the factorisation is even or odd.","In 2³ × 3² × 5 the indices are 3, 2 and 1, and a square number needs every index to be even."],"codes":["N4","N6"]},{"q":"A shop sells pens in boxes of 48. A school orders 25 boxes at £6.40 for each box and pays £20 for delivery. Work out the cost of one pen, in pence. A calculator may be used.","a":"15p","sol":"Cost of the boxes: 25 × £6.40 = £160 (M1). Total cost including delivery: £160 + £20 = £180 (A1). Number of pens: 25 × 48 = 1200 (M1). Cost of one pen: £180 ÷ 1200 = £0.15 (M1), which is 15p (A1).","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["The delivery charge is paid once for the whole order, not once for each box.","Work out the cost of the boxes, add the delivery charge, then divide by the total number of pens.","Multiply the number of boxes by the price of one box; separately, multiply the number of boxes by the number of pens in a box."],"codes":["N2"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write 4.05 × 10⁴ as an ordinary number.","a":"40 500"},{"q":"Work out 6 + 4 × 5²","a":"106"},{"q":"Write down the lowest common multiple of 6 and 8.","a":"24"},{"q":"Work out 2⁻³, giving your answer as a fraction.","a":"1/8"}]},"cards":[{"front":"The inequality symbols =, ≠, <, >, ≤, ≥","back":"= equals; ≠ is not equal to; < is less than; > is greater than; ≤ is less than or equal to; ≥ is greater than or equal to. The symbol always points at the smaller value.","code":"N1","open":false},{"front":"Ordering negative numbers","back":"The further a negative number lies from zero, the smaller it is, so −7 < −3 < −0.5 < 0 < 0.5. Every negative number is smaller than every positive one.","code":"N1","open":false},{"front":"Name two ways of showing that 0.6 is greater than 4/7","back":"Write both as decimals, since 4/7 = 0.571… and 0.571… < 0.6; write both over the common denominator 35, since 0.6 = 21/35 and 4/7 = 20/35; multiply out, since 0.6 × 7 = 4.2 and 4.2 > 4.","code":"N1","open":true},{"front":"Place value when multiplying decimals","back":"Multiply the digits as whole numbers, then give the answer as many decimal places as the two numbers have between them: 0.3 × 0.04 = 0.012.","code":"N2","open":false},{"front":"Subtracting a negative number","back":"Subtracting a negative is the same as adding: −7 − (−12) = −7 + 12 = 5. Two negative signs next to each other become a plus.","code":"N2","open":false},{"front":"Priority of operations (BIDMAS)","back":"Brackets, then indices (powers and roots), then division and multiplication left to right, then addition and subtraction left to right.","code":"N3","open":false},{"front":"Inverse operations","back":"Each operation is undone by its inverse: + and −, × and ÷, a power and its root. Use the inverse to check: 372 ÷ 12 = 31 because 31 × 12 = 372.","code":"N3","open":false},{"front":"Prime number","back":"A whole number greater than 1 with exactly two factors, itself and 1. The primes begin 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19. 1 is not prime.","code":"N4","open":false},{"front":"Prime factorisation and the unique factorisation theorem","back":"Every integer greater than 1 is a product of primes in exactly one way, written in product notation: 720 = 2⁴ × 3² × 5.","code":"N4","open":false},{"front":"Reading the HCF and the LCM from two prime factorisations","back":"HCF: multiply the lowest power of each prime that appears in both. LCM: multiply the highest power of every prime that appears in either.","code":"N4","open":false},{"front":"A systematic listing strategy","back":"Hold one choice fixed and vary the next in a set order, then move the fixed choice on. The list is then complete, with no gaps and no repeats.","code":"N5","open":false},{"front":"The powers and roots to recognise on sight","back":"2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128; 3, 9, 27, 81, 243; 4, 16, 64, 256; 5, 25, 125, 625. A root undoes its power: ∛64 = 4 because 4³ = 64.","code":"N6","open":false},{"front":"The laws of indices for integer indices","back":"aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ; aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ; a⁰ = 1; a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ. The base must be the same before indices may be added or subtracted.","code":"N7","open":false},{"front":"An exact answer","back":"A fraction in its lowest terms or a multiple of π, never a rounded decimal: a circle of radius 3 cm has area 9π cm², not 28.3 cm².","code":"N8","open":false},{"front":"Standard form A × 10ⁿ","back":"A is at least 1 and less than 10, and n is an integer: positive for large numbers, negative for small ones. 0.000 45 = 4.5 × 10⁻⁴.","code":"N9","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: area of a circle = πr², where r is the radius — so an exact area such as 49π cm² can be written straight from the sheet.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd, where r is the radius and d is the diameter.","Those two circle results are the only things on this room's syllabus that the Foundation Exam Aid prints; everything below has to be remembered.","Remember: the laws of indices — aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ, aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ, a⁰ = 1, a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ (N7). The Exam Aid does not print them at either tier.","Remember: standard form is A × 10ⁿ with 1 ≤ A < 10 and n an integer, and to multiply you multiply the A values, add the indices of 10, then adjust A back into range (N9).","Remember: HCF = the product of the lowest power of each prime shared by the two factorisations; LCM = the product of the highest power of every prime that appears (N4).","Remember: the priority of operations — brackets, indices, division and multiplication left to right, then addition and subtraction left to right (N3).","Remember: the squares up to 15² = 225, the cubes 2³ = 8, 3³ = 27, 4³ = 64 and 5³ = 125, and the powers of 2 up to 2⁷ = 128 (N6).","Remember: when multiplying decimals the answer carries as many decimal places as the two numbers have between them, and a − (−b) = a + b (N2).","For reference, the rest of the Foundation Exam Aid — none of it needed in this room — is the area of a trapezium, the volume of a prism, Pythagoras' theorem, the three trigonometric ratios, the compound interest formula and P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B)."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T11:42:40Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.82,"notes":"Every answer key is correct — all 16 bank questions, the 4 exit questions, the 4 lesson checks and all 6 worked examples re-solved independently in Python, with mark tallies matching each tariff and the Formulae sheet matching the 2026 Foundation Exam Aid word for word — so the only work before publishing is to stop lesson.idea from printing the answers to questions 1, 5, 6 and 9."}}},"number-fdp":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"number-fdp","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Fractions, decimals and percentages are the working language of the rest of this course. A sale price, a share of a ratio, a rate of pay, a probability and a share of a survey are all the same move: one quantity acted on by a multiplier. This room gives you the three conversions that unlock that move — a terminating decimal into its fraction and back, a ratio into the fraction of the whole, and a fraction or percentage used as an operator. Paper 1 is taken without a calculator, so these conversions have to be done by hand and answers left exact, as a fraction in its lowest terms; on Papers 2 and 3 the same multipliers are what you key in. Get them wrong here and every later percentage, proportion and rate question goes wrong with them.","idea":[{"h":"Terminating decimals and their fractions","t":"A terminating decimal stops, so its last digit names a denominator: tenths, hundredths, thousandths. Write the digits over that place value and cancel by the highest common factor — 0.28 = 28/100 = 7/25, and 1.6 = 16/10 = 8/5. To go back, divide numerator by denominator: 9/40 = 9 ÷ 40 = 0.225. Examiners ask for this in three shapes: convert and give the fraction in its lowest terms, convert a fraction to a decimal, or order a mixed list of fractions and decimals by putting them all into the same form first. Knowing 1/2, 1/4, 3/4, 1/5, 1/8 and 1/20 by sight saves the division every time.","code":"N10"},{"h":"Fractions hiding inside a ratio","t":"A ratio counts equal parts, so a ratio a : b describes a + b parts altogether and each quantity is a fraction of that whole: 2 : 3 makes 2/5 and 3/5. This is why a ratio question and a fraction question can be answered by the same working — find the total number of parts, then take that many parts of the amount. The move also runs backwards: if 3/7 of a class walk to school, the rest are 4/7 and the ratio walk : bus is 3 : 4. Watch for part : part against part : whole, because 3 : 4 and 3/7 describe the same class.","code":"N11"},{"h":"Fractions and percentages as operators","t":"An operator is something that acts on a quantity and scales it. The word 'of' means multiply: 3/4 of 60 kg = 3/4 × 60 = 45 kg, and 15% of 240 = 0.15 × 240 = 36. A change is the same idea in one step: an increase of p% leaves you with 100% + p%, so the multiplier is 1 + p/100 (15% gives × 1.15), and a decrease leaves 100% − p%, so the multiplier is 1 − p/100 (35% gives × 0.65). One multiplication, not two steps, is what the mark scheme looks for, and by hand it is often easier as a fraction: × 0.75 is × 3/4.","code":"N12"}],"examples":[{"title":"A terminating decimal written as a fraction","setup":"A bolt is 0.28 m long. Write 0.28 as a fraction in its lowest terms.","steps":["The last digit 8 sits in the hundredths column, so 0.28 = 28/100.","Both parts divide by 4: 28 ÷ 4 = 7 and 100 ÷ 4 = 25.","So 0.28 = 7/25, and 7 and 25 share no common factor, so this is lowest terms."],"cues":["Which place value does the last digit sit in?","What is the highest common factor of 28 and 100?","Can the cancelled fraction be reduced any further?"]},{"title":"Comparing a fraction with a decimal","setup":"Two ribbons are measured in different ways. Ribbon A is 7/8 of a metre long. Ribbon B is 0.87 m long. Which ribbon is longer, and by how many metres?","steps":["Change Ribbon A into a decimal by short division: 7 ÷ 8 = 0.875.","Pad Ribbon B to the same number of decimal places: 0.87 becomes 0.870.","Digit by digit the tenths and hundredths match, but in the thousandths 5 is bigger than 0, so 0.875 is the larger value.","0.875 − 0.870 = 0.005, so Ribbon A is longer, by 0.005 m."],"cues":["Which form should both lengths be in before they are compared?","Why is it safer to give both lengths the same number of decimal places?","Which column decides the comparison here?","How is the gap between two decimals found?"]},{"title":"From a ratio to a fraction of the whole","setup":"A fruit bowl holds only apples and pears, in the ratio 4 : 5. There are 36 pieces of fruit in the bowl altogether. What fraction of the fruit are apples, and how many apples are there?","steps":["Add the parts: 4 + 5 = 9, so the fruit is shared into 9 equal parts.","Apples take 4 of those 9 parts, so the fraction that are apples is 4/9, and that fraction acts on the 36 pieces of fruit.","One part is 36 ÷ 9 = 4 pieces of fruit, so 4/9 of the bowl is 4 × 4 = 16 apples."],"cues":["How many equal parts does this ratio describe?","How many of those parts are apples, and what does that fraction act on?","How many pieces of fruit make up one part?"]},{"title":"A percentage increase in one multiplication","setup":"A train ticket costs £48. The price is to be increased by 15%. Use a multiplier to work out the new price.","steps":["An increase of 15% keeps the whole 100% and adds 15% on top, so the multiplier is 1 + 15/100 = 1.15.","New price = old price × multiplier = 48 × 1.15.","48 × 1.15 = 48 + 4.80 + 2.40 = 55.20, so the new price is £55.20."],"cues":["What percentage of the old price is the new price?","Which single calculation now gives the new price?","What are 10% and 5% of £48?"]},{"title":"A percentage decrease done as a fraction","setup":"A coat costs £84. In a sale, every price is reduced by 25%. Work out the sale price, using a multiplier written as a fraction in its lowest terms.","steps":["Taking 25% off leaves 100% − 25% = 75% of the price, so the multiplier is 0.75.","As a fraction 0.75 = 75/100 = 3/4 after dividing both parts by 25, so the sale price is 84 × 3/4.","84 ÷ 4 = 21 and 21 × 3 = 63, so the sale price is £63."],"cues":["What percentage of the price does the shopper actually pay?","How could this multiplier be written so the arithmetic is easy by hand?","What is one quarter of £84?"]},{"title":"From a fraction back to a ratio","setup":"In a class of 28 students, 3/7 walk to school and the rest travel by bus. Write the number who walk to the number who travel by bus as a ratio in its simplest form, and work out how many students are in each group.","steps":["Subtract from the whole: 7/7 − 3/7 = 4/7 do not walk, giving walk : bus = 3 : 4.","One seventh of the class is 28 ÷ 7 = 4 students, so 3/7 of 28 = 4 × 3 = 12 walk.","28 − 12 = 16 travel by bus, and 12 : 16 divided through by 4 is 3 : 4, which agrees with the ratio."],"cues":["What fraction of the class does not walk?","How many students make up one seventh of the class?","How can the two group sizes be used to check the ratio?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Write 0.4 as a fraction in its lowest terms.","a":"2/5"},{"q":"Write 7/20 as a decimal.","a":"0.35"},{"q":"Counters are red : blue in the ratio 3 : 5. What fraction of the counters are blue?","a":"5/8"},{"q":"Work out 2/3 of 54 m.","a":"36 m"},{"q":"Write down the single multiplier that increases an amount by 22%.","a":"1.22"}]},"room":{"facts":["Decimal to fraction: put the digits over the place value of the last digit, then cancel — 0.28 = 28/100 = 7/25.","Fraction to decimal: divide the numerator by the denominator — 9/40 = 9 ÷ 40 = 0.225.","A ratio a : b has a + b equal parts, so the first quantity is a/(a + b) of the whole and the second is b/(a + b).","A fraction of the whole gives a ratio: if a/b have the property, property : rest = a : (b − a).","'Of' means multiply: a/b of Q = a/b × Q, and p% of Q = (p/100) × Q.","Multiplier for an increase of p% is 1 + p/100; for a decrease of p% it is 1 − p/100 (a 35% decrease is × 0.65).","Equivalents to know by sight: 1/2 = 0.5, 1/4 = 0.25, 3/4 = 0.75, 1/5 = 0.2, 1/8 = 0.125, 3/8 = 0.375, 1/20 = 0.05, 1/25 = 0.04."],"questions":[{"q":"Write 1.6 as an improper fraction in its lowest terms. (non-calculator)","a":"8/5","sol":"The last digit sits in the tenths column, so 1.6 = 16/10. M1 for 16/10 (or for 1 whole and 6/10). Both parts divide by 2: 16 ÷ 2 = 8 and 10 ÷ 2 = 5, giving 8/5. A1 for 8/5 (16/10 uncancelled scores only the method mark).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The digit after the point tells you which place value names the denominator.","Write all the digits over that place value, then cancel by the highest common factor.","The first line is 1.6 = 16/10; the cancelling comes after it."],"codes":["N10"]},{"q":"Write 9/40 as a decimal. (non-calculator)","a":"0.225","sol":"9/40 means 9 ÷ 40. Short division gives 9.000 ÷ 40 = 0.225 (or multiply both parts by 25: 9/40 = 225/1000 = 0.225). B1 for 0.225.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The line in a fraction is a division sign.","Divide the numerator by the denominator, or scale the denominator up to a power of 10.","The first line is 9 ÷ 40, worked as 9.000 ÷ 40."],"codes":["N10"]},{"q":"Work out 3/5 of 85 kg. (non-calculator)","a":"51 kg","sol":"'Of' means multiply, so 3/5 of 85 = 3/5 × 85. One fifth is 85 ÷ 5 = 17, so three fifths is 17 × 3. M1 for 85 ÷ 5 = 17 or for 3/5 × 85 seen. A1 for 51 kg, with the unit.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A fraction in front of a quantity is acting on it.","Divide by the denominator to get one part, then multiply by the numerator.","The first line is 85 ÷ 5, and the numerator is used after that."],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"Write down the single number an amount is multiplied by to increase it by 7%. (non-calculator)","a":"1.07","sol":"After a 7% increase the amount is 100% + 7% = 107% of what it was, and 107% = 107/100. B1 for 1.07.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["After the increase, what percentage of the original amount is left standing?","A percentage only works as a multiplier once it is written as a decimal.","The same route on different numbers: a 9% increase gives 100% + 9% = 109%, so the multiplier is 1.09."],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"A shelf holds fiction and non-fiction books in the ratio 6 : 5. What fraction of the books on the shelf are non-fiction? Give your answer in its simplest form. (non-calculator)","a":"5/11","sol":"The ratio splits the books into 6 + 5 = 11 equal parts. M1 for 11 parts seen. Non-fiction takes 5 of those 11 parts, so the fraction is 5/11. A1 for 5/11.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A ratio counts equal parts, and the whole shelf is all of them.","Add the parts to get the whole, then count the parts you want.","The first line is 6 + 5, and the non-fiction parts go on top."],"codes":["N11"]},{"q":"Priya is paid £9.60 per hour. Her rate of pay is increased by 5%. Work out her new rate of pay per hour. (a calculator may be used)","a":"£10.08","sol":"An increase of 5% gives 100% + 5% = 105%, so the multiplier is 1.05. New rate = 9.60 × 1.05 = 10.08. M1 for 1.05 or for 9.60 × 1.05 (or for 5% of 9.60 = 0.48). A1 for £10.08.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["One multiplication can do the whole increase.","Work out the multiplier for a 5% increase, then apply it to the old rate.","The first line is old rate × multiplier, with the multiplier found from 100% + 5%."],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"In a car park, 0.45 of the vehicles are red. Write the ratio of red vehicles to vehicles that are not red, in its simplest form. (non-calculator)","a":"9 : 11","sol":"0.45 = 45/100 = 9/20 of the vehicles are red. M1 for 45/100 or 9/20. The rest are 20/20 − 9/20 = 11/20 (or 1 − 0.45 = 0.55 = 11/20). M1 for 11/20 or 0.55. In twentieths the vehicles split 9 parts to 11 parts, so red : not red = 9 : 11. A1 for 9 : 11.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A decimal of the whole can be read as a fraction of the whole.","Turn the decimal into a fraction in its lowest terms, then find the fraction that is left over.","The first line is 0.45 = 45/100, and the two fractions over the same denominator give the ratio."],"codes":["N10","N11"]},{"q":"A laptop costs £540. In a sale the price is reduced by 35%. Work out the sale price. (a calculator may be used)","a":"£351","sol":"A reduction of 35% leaves 100% − 35% = 65%, so the multiplier is 0.65. M1 for 0.65 (or for 35% of 540 = 189). Sale price = 540 × 0.65. M1 for 540 × 0.65 (or 540 − 189). This gives 351. A1 for £351.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The shopper still pays for most of the laptop — how much of it?","Find the percentage that is left and use it as a multiplier on the old price.","The first line is 100% − 35%, written as a decimal multiplier."],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"A jar contains only 20p coins and 50p coins, in the ratio 3 : 2. There are 45 coins in the jar. How many 50p coins are in the jar? (non-calculator)","a":"18","sol":"The ratio gives 3 + 2 = 5 equal parts, so the 50p coins are 2/5 of the coins. M1 for 5 parts or for the fraction 2/5. One fifth of the jar is 45 ÷ 5 = 9 coins. M1 for 45 ÷ 5 = 9. So 2/5 of 45 = 9 × 2 = 18. A1 for 18.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The 50p coins are a fraction of all the coins, not of the 20p coins.","Turn the ratio into the fraction that is 50p coins, then take that fraction of 45.","The first line is 3 + 2 = 5 parts, and 45 is then divided by the number of parts."],"codes":["N11","N12"]},{"q":"A café bakes 120 cakes. It sells 5/8 of them in the morning, then sells 40% of the cakes that are left during the afternoon. How many cakes are unsold at the end of the day? (a calculator may be used)","a":"27","sol":"Morning: 5/8 of 120 = (120 ÷ 8) × 5 = 75 cakes sold. M1 for 5/8 × 120 = 75. Left at midday: 120 − 75 = 45. M1 for 45. Afternoon: 40% are sold, so 60% of the 45 are left, giving 0.6 × 45. M1 for 0.6 × 45 (or 0.4 × 45 = 18 followed by 45 − 18). This gives 27. A1 for 27 cakes.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The afternoon percentage acts on what is left, not on the whole batch.","Work out the morning sales first, subtract them, then apply a multiplier to what remains.","The first line is 5/8 × 120, and the remainder is what the afternoon multiplier acts on."],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"Put these four numbers in order, smallest first: 0.7, 5/8, 0.66, 17/25. (non-calculator)","a":"5/8, 0.66, 17/25, 0.7","sol":"Put everything in one form. 5/8 = 5 ÷ 8 = 0.625. M1 for 0.625. 17/25 = 68/100 = 0.68. M1 for 0.68. Comparing 0.625, 0.66, 0.68 and 0.7 gives 0.625 < 0.66 < 0.68 < 0.7, so in the forms given the order is 5/8, 0.66, 17/25, 0.7. A1 for the correct order, written in the original forms.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Numbers can only be compared when they are written the same way.","Convert both fractions to decimals, then compare place value by place value.","The first line is 5 ÷ 8, and the second fraction is scaled to hundredths."],"codes":["N10"]},{"q":"Ali and Ben share a piece of work. Ali does 0.375 of the work and Ben does the rest. They are paid £560 for the job, shared between them in the same proportions as the work. How much is Ben paid? (non-calculator)","a":"£350","sol":"Ali: 0.375 = 375/1000 = 3/8 of the work. M1 for 3/8. Ben does the rest, 1 − 3/8 = 5/8 (so Ali : Ben = 3 : 5). M1 for 5/8 or for the ratio 3 : 5. Ben's pay = 5/8 of 560, and 560 ÷ 8 = 70. M1 for 560 ÷ 8 = 70. So 70 × 5 = 350. A1 for £350.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The decimal tells you Ali's share of one whole job.","Turn the decimal into a fraction, take it from the whole to get Ben's fraction, then apply that fraction to the money.","The first line is 0.375 = 375/1000, cancelled, and the whole is 1."],"codes":["N10","N11","N12"]},{"q":"A shop buys 240 bottles of juice for 90p each. It sells 3/4 of the bottles at £1.50 each, and sells the rest at a price that is 30% less than £1.50. Work out the shop's total profit on the 240 bottles. (a calculator may be used)","a":"£117","sol":"Cost = 240 × 0.90 = £216. M1 for 240 × 0.90. Bottles at full price = 3/4 × 240 = 180, so 240 − 180 = 60 are left. M1 for 180 and 60. Full-price takings = 180 × 1.50 = £270. M1 for 270. The reduced price is 1.50 × 0.7 = £1.05, so the last bottles take 60 × 1.05 = £63. M1 for 63. Total takings = 270 + 63 = £333, and profit = 333 − 216 = £117. A1 for £117.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Profit compares what the shop takes in with what it paid out.","Work out the cost, then the takings from each group of bottles separately, using a multiplier for the reduced price.","The first line is 240 × 0.90 for the cost, and the bottles then split 3/4 to the rest."],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"A youth club has 96 members. The ratio of members aged under 16 to members aged 16 or over is 5 : 3. A quarter of the under-16 members are girls, and 1/3 of the members aged 16 or over are girls. How many members of the club are girls? (non-calculator)","a":"27","sol":"The ratio gives 5 + 3 = 8 equal parts, so the under-16 members are 5/8 of the club and the rest are 3/8. M1 for 5/8 (or 8 parts with 96 ÷ 8 = 12). Under 16: 5/8 of 96 = (96 ÷ 8) × 5 = 60 members; 16 or over: 96 − 60 = 36 members. M1 for 60 and 36. Girls under 16 = 1/4 of 60 = 15. M1 for 15. Girls aged 16 or over = 1/3 of 36 = 12. M1 for 12. Total girls = 15 + 12 = 27. A1 for 27.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["The ratio splits the club before either fraction is used.","Find the size of each age group first, then apply each group's own fraction to that group.","The first line is 5 + 3 parts, giving the fraction of the club that is under 16."],"codes":["N11","N12"]},{"q":"The same pair of trainers is on offer in two shops. In shop A they cost £80 and the price is reduced by 1/4. In shop B they cost £76 and the price is reduced by 20%. Which shop sells the trainers more cheaply, and by how much? (non-calculator)","a":"Shop A, by £0.80","sol":"Shop A: a reduction of 1/4 leaves 3/4, so the price is 80 × 3/4. M1 for 80 × 3/4 (or 1/4 of 80 = 20 taken off). Shop B: a reduction of 20% leaves 80%, so the price is 76 × 0.8. M1 for 76 × 0.8 (or 20% of 76 = 15.20 taken off). The prices are £60 and £60.80. A1 for both £60 and £60.80. 60.80 − 60 = 0.80, so shop A is cheaper by £0.80. A1 for the comparison with the difference.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A fraction off and a percentage off are the same kind of instruction.","Turn each reduction into the fraction or decimal of the price that is still paid, then compare the two sale prices.","The first line is 80 × 3/4 for shop A, with shop B needing its own multiplier."],"codes":["N10","N12"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write 0.24 as a fraction in its lowest terms.","a":"6/25"},{"q":"Write 3/16 as a decimal.","a":"0.1875"},{"q":"Sweets are red : green in the ratio 2 : 7. What fraction of the sweets are green?","a":"7/9"},{"q":"Write down the single multiplier that decreases an amount by 18%.","a":"0.82"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Terminating decimal","back":"A decimal that stops after a fixed number of decimal places, such as 0.375. Written over a power of 10 it cancels, so in lowest terms its denominator has no prime factors other than 2 and 5: 0.28 = 7/25.","code":"N10","open":false},{"front":"Decimal to fraction: the method","back":"Write the digits over the place value of the last digit (tenths, hundredths, thousandths), then cancel by the highest common factor: 0.28 = 28/100 = 7/25.","code":"N10","open":false},{"front":"Fraction to decimal: the method","back":"Divide the numerator by the denominator: 3/8 = 3 ÷ 8 = 0.375. Scaling to a power of 10 also works: 9/40 = 225/1000 = 0.225.","code":"N10","open":false},{"front":"3.5 written as an improper fraction","back":"3.5 = 35/10 = 7/2. The 3 is 30/10 and the 0.5 is 5/10, and 35/10 cancels by 5.","code":"N10","open":false},{"front":"The decimal equivalents worth knowing by sight","back":"1/2 = 0.5, 1/4 = 0.25, 3/4 = 0.75, 1/5 = 0.2, 1/8 = 0.125, 3/8 = 0.375, 1/10 = 0.1, 1/20 = 0.05, 1/25 = 0.04, 1/40 = 0.025.","code":"N10","open":false},{"front":"Give one example of a terminating decimal written as a fraction in its lowest terms","back":"0.45 = 9/20; 0.6 = 3/5; 0.125 = 1/8. Any decimal correctly converted and fully cancelled is acceptable.","code":"N10","open":true},{"front":"Number of parts in the ratio a : b","back":"a + b equal parts. One part is the whole amount divided by a + b, which is the step most ratio questions turn on.","code":"N11","open":false},{"front":"Fraction of the whole from a ratio","back":"In the ratio a : b the first quantity is a/(a + b) of the whole and the second is b/(a + b). So 2 : 3 gives 2/5 and 3/5.","code":"N11","open":false},{"front":"Ratio from a fraction of the whole","back":"If a/b of a set has a property, the rest is (b − a)/b, so property : rest = a : (b − a). 3/7 of a class gives 3 : 4.","code":"N11","open":false},{"front":"Part : part against part : whole","back":"3 : 4 counts one group against the other; 3/7 counts one group against everybody. Check which the question gives before using it.","code":"N11","open":false},{"front":"Fraction as an operator","back":"'Of' means multiply: a/b of Q = a/b × Q. Divide by the denominator for one part, then multiply by the numerator: 3/4 of 60 kg = 45 kg.","code":"N12","open":false},{"front":"Percentage as an operator","back":"p% of Q = (p/100) × Q. So 15% of 240 = 0.15 × 240 = 36.","code":"N12","open":false},{"front":"Multiplier for an increase of p%","back":"1 + p/100. An increase of 15% is × 1.15, because the new amount is 115% of the old one.","code":"N12","open":false},{"front":"Multiplier for a decrease of p%","back":"1 − p/100. A decrease of 35% is × 0.65, because 65% of the amount is left.","code":"N12","open":false},{"front":"Multiplier written as a fraction","back":"Any multiplier can be a fraction: × 0.75 is × 3/4 and × 1.2 is × 6/5. Without a calculator the fraction is usually the quicker route.","code":"N12","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Decimal to fraction: digits over the place value of the last digit, then cancel — 0.28 = 28/100 = 7/25. Not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","Fraction to decimal: numerator ÷ denominator — 9/40 = 9 ÷ 40 = 0.225. Not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","Decimal to improper fraction and back: 3.5 = 35/10 = 7/2. Not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","Fraction of the whole from a ratio a : b — first quantity = a/(a + b), second = b/(a + b), and one part = whole ÷ (a + b). Not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","Ratio from a fraction — if a/b of a set has a property, property : rest = a : (b − a). Not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","'Of' as an operator — a/b of Q = a/b × Q and p% of Q = (p/100) × Q. Not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","Multiplier for a p% increase = 1 + p/100 (15% gives × 1.15); for a p% decrease = 1 − p/100 (35% gives × 0.65). Not on the Exam Aid: remember both.","Decimal equivalents that save a division: 1/2 = 0.5, 1/4 = 0.25, 3/4 = 0.75, 1/5 = 0.2, 1/8 = 0.125, 3/8 = 0.375, 1/10 = 0.1, 1/20 = 0.05, 1/25 = 0.04, 1/40 = 0.025. Nowhere on the Exam Aid: remember them.","The one multiplier result the Foundation Exam Aid (W87990A) does print is Total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, for interest compounded over n periods — the repeated-multiplier case, which belongs to the growth and decay room, not to the single-step multipliers here.","Nothing else in this room is printed: the Foundation sheet gives only the area of a trapezium, the volume of a prism, the circumference and area of a circle, a² + b² = c², sin A = a/c, cos A = b/c, tan A = a/b, the compound interest formula and P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B), all of which belong to other rooms."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T11:41:22Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.93,"notes":"Answer keys, mark tallies and solution lines are all correct and the Foundation formulae-sheet claims match Exam Aid W87990A exactly — publish as it is, and only trim the hints on the 7% multiplier question if you want students to do any of it themselves."}}},"number-measures":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"number-measures","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Measures and accuracy marks are spread right across all three papers of 1MA1, and they are rarely the whole question: a speed, a density or a price per unit sits inside a ratio or proportion problem, a rounding instruction sits at the end of a calculator question, and an error interval sits inside a reasoning question about whether something will fit. Every answer in the exam has to be given to the accuracy asked for and with its unit, so the marks in this room protect marks everywhere else. Paper 1 is non-calculator, so estimation by rounding to 1 significant figure is also how you check your own arithmetic, and on Papers 2 and 3 it is how you catch a mistyped calculator key before it costs you the accuracy mark.","idea":[{"h":"Standard units and compound measures","t":"Use the standard units of mass (g, kg, tonnes), length (mm, cm, m, km), capacity (ml, litres), time (seconds, minutes, hours) and money (£ and pence), and name the unit with every answer — an answer with no unit loses the accuracy mark. A compound measure is one quantity divided by another: average speed = distance ÷ time, density = mass ÷ volume, price per unit = total price ÷ number of units. Its unit is written the same way: km/h, m/s, g/cm³, £ per litre. The two quantities must be in matching units before you divide, so 45 minutes becomes 0.75 hours and 1.79 kg becomes 1790 g. Decimal quantities are expected: 2.5 hours, not 2 hours 30 minutes, inside a division.","code":"N13"},{"h":"Estimating and checking by approximation","t":"To estimate, round every number in the calculation to 1 significant figure, then work out the easy calculation that is left, writing ≈ rather than =. The method mark is for the rounded calculation being written down, so a bare estimate scores nothing. Dividing by a number below 1 makes the answer bigger: ÷ 0.5 doubles, ÷ 0.2 multiplies by 5. Estimation is also how a calculator answer is checked — if the display reads 42.4 and the estimate is 400, a key has been mistyped. Questions often ask you to say whether an answer is sensible, so finish with a sentence comparing the estimate with the value given, not just the estimate itself.","code":"N14"},{"h":"Rounding and error intervals","t":"Rounding to a number of decimal places counts places after the point; rounding to significant figures starts counting at the first non-zero digit, so 0.04862 to 2 significant figures is 0.049. Look at the next digit along: 5 or more rounds up, 4 or less leaves the digit alone. An appropriate degree of accuracy usually means money to the nearest penny, or the same accuracy as the values you started with. A rounded measurement does not give the exact value, so the error interval says what it could have been: to the nearest unit u the interval is half a unit either side, written with ≤ at the lower end and < at the upper end because a value on the upper limit would round up. Truncation chops the digits off instead of rounding, so a value truncated to 8.3 lies in 8.3 ≤ n < 8.4.","code":"N15"},{"h":"Limits of accuracy","t":"A rounded or truncated measurement stands for every value in its interval, and the limits of accuracy are the two ends of that interval: a mass of 25 kg to the nearest kilogram has a lower limit of 24.5 kg and an upper limit of 25.5 kg, and the true mass is at least 24.5 kg but never quite 25.5 kg. Interpreting the limits means saying what they mean for the situation: the shortest a shelf could be, the most a lift could be carrying, whether a claimed value is possible at all. In a reasoning question the useful limit is usually the one that works against you — the shortest length when something has to fit, the largest load when something has a safe limit — and the conclusion has to be written down in words, with the comparison that justifies it.","code":"N16"}],"examples":[{"title":"Average speed as a compound measure","setup":"A car travels 150 km in 2.5 hours. Work out its average speed in km/h, giving the answer as a decimal quantity with its unit.","steps":["Average speed = distance ÷ time, so the calculation is 150 ÷ 2.5.","Doubling both parts clears the decimal: 150 ÷ 2.5 = 300 ÷ 5, which is the line the method mark is awarded for.","300 ÷ 5 = 60, so the average speed is 60 km/h."],"cues":["Which quantity is being shared out over which?","How could the divisor be made a whole number?","What unit belongs on a distance in km over a time in hours?"]},{"title":"Estimating to check a calculator answer","setup":"A calculator shows 39.7 × 5.13 ÷ 0.48 = 424.29 (to 2 decimal places). Estimate the value of 39.7 × 5.13 ÷ 0.48 by rounding each number to 1 significant figure, and decide whether the calculator answer is sensible.","steps":["Round each value to 1 significant figure: 39.7 ≈ 40, 5.13 ≈ 5 and 0.48 ≈ 0.5, choosing the nearest easy numbers.","The estimate is 40 × 5 ÷ 0.5; do the multiplication first, 40 × 5 = 200.","Dividing by 0.5 doubles a number, so 200 ÷ 0.5 = 400.","400 is close to the value the calculator displayed, so that answer is sensible."],"cues":["What is the first significant figure of each number?","Which part of the rounded calculation is easiest to do first?","What does dividing by a half do to a number?","How does the estimate compare with the display?"]},{"title":"Writing an error interval","setup":"A length is measured as 8 cm to the nearest centimetre. Write down the error interval for the length x, using inequality notation.","steps":["To the nearest centimetre means the value was rounded, so it lies within half a centimetre either side: the gap is 1 ÷ 2 = 0.5 cm.","Smallest value: 8 − 0.5 = 7.5 cm, and 7.5 cm itself rounds up to 8 cm, so 7.5 is included.","Largest value: 8 + 0.5 = 8.5 cm, but 8.5 cm would round up to 9 cm, so 8.5 is not included.","So the error interval is 7.5 ≤ x < 8.5, in cm."],"cues":["How far either side of the reading can the true value be?","What is the smallest length that still rounds to this reading?","Does the value at the top of the range round up or down?","Which end of the interval needs the strict inequality?"]},{"title":"Interpreting limits of accuracy","setup":"A time is measured as 12.5 seconds, correct to the nearest 0.1 second. State the limits of accuracy of the measurement, and say whether the true time could have been 12.44 seconds.","steps":["Nearest 0.1 second means the reading was rounded, so the true value lies within 0.1 ÷ 2 = 0.05 s of it.","Lower limit: 12.5 − 0.05 = 12.45 s. Upper limit: 12.5 + 0.05 = 12.55 s.","As an interval, 12.45 ≤ t < 12.55, so the true time is at least 12.45 s and below 12.55 s.","The value suggested in the question is below 12.45 s, so no — the true time could not have been that small."],"cues":["Half of which unit gives the gap either side?","What are the two ends of the range?","How is that range written in inequality notation?","Does the suggested value lie inside the range?"]},{"title":"Price per litre and best value","setup":"A 750 ml bottle of juice costs £2.19. A 2 litre carton of the same juice costs £5.60. Work out the price per litre of each, to the nearest penny, and say which is the better value.","steps":["750 ml = 750 ÷ 1000 = 0.75 litres, so the bottle gives 0.75 litres for £2.19.","Bottle: price per litre = 2.19 ÷ 0.75 = £2.92.","Carton: price per litre = 5.60 ÷ 2 = £2.80.","£2.80 per litre is less than £2.92 per litre, so the carton is the better value."],"cues":["What has to happen before the two sizes can be compared?","Which quantity is divided by which for a price per litre?","What is the same calculation for the larger container?","Which price per litre is the smaller?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A width is 4.7 cm to the nearest 0.1 cm. Write down the error interval for the width w.","a":"4.65 ≤ w < 4.75 (cm)"},{"q":"Estimate 396 ÷ 1.96 by rounding each number to 1 significant figure.","a":"200 (from 400 ÷ 2)"},{"q":"A runner covers 250 m in 50 seconds. Work out the average speed in m/s.","a":"5 m/s"},{"q":"Round 2.0649 to 2 decimal places.","a":"2.06"}]},"room":{"facts":["Average speed = distance ÷ time, so distance = speed × time and time = distance ÷ speed. Distance in km with time in hours gives km/h; metres with seconds gives m/s.","Density = mass ÷ volume (g/cm³ or kg/m³), and price per unit = total price ÷ number of units. Both quantities must be in matching units before dividing.","To estimate: round every number to 1 significant figure, then do the easy calculation, writing ≈ not =. Dividing by 0.5 doubles a number; dividing by 0.2 multiplies it by 5.","Metric units: 1 km = 1000 m, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 cm = 10 mm, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 tonne = 1000 kg, 1 litre = 1000 ml, 1 ml = 1 cm³, 1 hour = 60 minutes.","Rounding: look at the next digit along — 5 or more rounds up, 4 or less leaves the digit alone. Significant figures are counted from the first non-zero digit.","A measurement given to the nearest u has error interval (value − u/2) ≤ x < (value + u/2); a measurement truncated at that place has value ≤ x < value + u."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Round 0.04862 correct to 2 significant figures.","a":"0.049","sol":"Significant figures are counted from the first non-zero digit, so the first is 4 and the second is 8. The next digit is 6, which is 5 or more, so the 8 rounds up to 9 and the zeros holding the place value are kept: 0.049 (B1). Writing 0.05 (1 significant figure) or 0.048 scores nothing.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The zeros in front do not count as significant figures.","Find the first non-zero digit, count two digits from there, then look at the digit after them.","Your first line is to mark which digit is the second significant figure."],"codes":["N15"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A water tank holds 47 000 litres, correct to the nearest thousand litres. Write down the error interval for the volume V litres.","a":"46 500 ≤ V < 47 500","sol":"To the nearest thousand the true volume lies within half a thousand of the reading, and half of 1000 is 500. 47 000 − 500 = 46 500 and 47 000 + 500 = 47 500 (B1 for both values seen). A volume of 46 500 litres rounds up to 47 000 so it is included, while 47 500 litres rounds up to 48 000 so it is not: 46 500 ≤ V < 47 500 (B1 for the fully correct inequality).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The figure given is not exact — it stands for a whole range of volumes.","Work out half of the unit the value was rounded to, then go that far either side.","Your first line is half of the rounding unit; then take it away from, and add it to, the value."],"codes":["N15"]},{"q":"A part-time worker is paid £58.50 for 4.5 hours of work. Work out her rate of pay in £ per hour. A calculator may be used.","a":"£13 per hour","sol":"Rate of pay is a compound measure: pay ÷ time worked = 58.50 ÷ 4.5 (M1). This gives 13, so the rate of pay is £13 per hour (A1; the unit is needed for the accuracy mark).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A rate of pay measures money against the time worked.","Divide the total pay by the number of hours worked.","Your first line is total pay ÷ number of hours."],"codes":["N13"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Estimate the value of 4.8 × 19.7 by rounding each number to 1 significant figure.","a":"100","sol":"Rounding each number to 1 significant figure gives 4.8 ≈ 5 and 19.7 ≈ 20, so the calculation becomes 5 × 20 (M1 for both roundings and the product written down). 5 × 20 = 100 (A1). The exact value is 94.56, so the estimate is a sensible check.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["You are not meant to work out the exact product here.","Replace each number by the nearest number with one non-zero digit.","Your first line writes the rounded calculation, with ≈ instead of =."],"codes":["N14"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A bag of sand has a mass of 25 kg, correct to the nearest kilogram. Write down the smallest possible mass of the bag.","a":"24.5 kg","sol":"To the nearest kilogram the true mass lies within 0.5 kg either side of 25 kg, so the lower limit is 25 − 0.5 = 24.5 kg (B1, unit needed). A mass of exactly 24.5 kg rounds up to 25 kg, so it is the smallest mass possible.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The mass on the label is a rounded value, not the exact mass.","For a value rounded to the nearest kilogram, the true mass can be up to half a kilogram either side.","Your first line is half of the unit the mass was rounded to."],"codes":["N16"]},{"q":"A solid block of copper has a mass of 1.79 kg and a volume of 200 cm³. Work out the density of the copper in g/cm³. A calculator may be used.","a":"8.95 g/cm³","sol":"The unit asked for uses grams, so convert the mass first: 1.79 kg = 1.79 × 1000 = 1790 g (M1). Density = mass ÷ volume = 1790 ÷ 200 (M1). This gives 8.95, so the density is 8.95 g/cm³ (A1, unit needed).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Check the units the answer has to be in before you divide anything.","Convert the mass into grams, then divide mass by volume.","Your first line converts kilograms into grams by multiplying by 1000."],"codes":["N13"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Estimate the value of 29.3² ÷ 5.8.","a":"150","sol":"Round each number to 1 significant figure: 29.3 ≈ 30 and 5.8 ≈ 6 (M1). Square first: 30² = 900 (M1). Then 900 ÷ 6 = 150 (A1). The exact value is 148.0 to 1 decimal place, so the estimate is a good check.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Pick numbers that make both the squaring and the dividing easy.","Round both values to 1 significant figure, then follow the order of operations.","Your first line is the rounded calculation written with the square still to be worked out."],"codes":["N14"]},{"q":"A train travels 84 km in 45 minutes. Work out its average speed in km/h. A calculator may be used.","a":"112 km/h","sol":"The unit is km/h, so the time must be in hours: 45 minutes = 45 ÷ 60 = 0.75 hours (M1). Average speed = distance ÷ time = 84 ÷ 0.75 (M1). This gives 112, so the average speed is 112 km/h (A1, unit needed).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The two quantities have to be in units that match the answer.","Turn the time into a decimal number of hours, then divide distance by time.","Your first line divides the number of minutes by 60."],"codes":["N13"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A number n is truncated to 1 decimal place, giving 8.3. Write down the error interval for n.","a":"8.3 ≤ n < 8.4","sol":"Truncating chops off the digits after the first decimal place instead of rounding, so every value from 8.3 up to (but not including) 8.4 is truncated to 8.3 — for example 8.39 truncates to 8.3 (B1 for 8.3 and 8.4 identified as the limits). The lower limit is included and the upper limit is not: 8.3 ≤ n < 8.4 (B1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Truncating and rounding do not give the same interval.","Ask what the largest and smallest numbers are whose digits start this way.","Your first line writes down the two values the interval runs between."],"codes":["N15"]},{"q":"A car uses 41.6 litres of fuel to travel 508 km. Work out the fuel used per 100 km, correct to 3 significant figures. A calculator may be used.","a":"8.19 litres per 100 km","sol":"Fuel per kilometre = 41.6 ÷ 508 = 0.0818897… litres (M1). Fuel per 100 km = 0.0818897… × 100 = 8.18897… (M1). The fourth significant figure is 8, which is 5 or more, so this rounds to 8.19 litres per 100 km (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["This is a compound measure: an amount of fuel measured against a distance.","Find the fuel used for one kilometre first, then scale it up.","Your first line divides the volume of fuel by the distance travelled."],"codes":["N13","N15"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A bottle is labelled as containing 500 ml, correct to the nearest 10 ml. Dan says the bottle could contain 506 ml. Is Dan right? You must give a reason.","a":"No — the volume must be less than 505 ml","sol":"To the nearest 10 ml the true volume lies within 10 ÷ 2 = 5 ml either side, so the error interval is 495 ≤ v < 505 in ml (B1). 506 ml is above the upper limit of 505 ml, so Dan is wrong (B1 for the conclusion with the comparison).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The label is a rounded value, so the contents lie in a range.","Work out the two limits the rounding allows, then test Dan's value against them.","Your first line is half of the unit the volume was rounded to."],"codes":["N16"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A shelf is 2.4 m long, correct to the nearest 0.1 m. Ben wants to cut 8 pieces, each exactly 0.3 m long, from the shelf. Can Ben be certain that he has enough shelf? You must show your working.","a":"No — the shelf could be only 2.35 m, which is less than the 2.4 m needed","sol":"Length of shelf needed: 8 × 0.3 = 2.4 m (M1). The shelf is rounded to the nearest 0.1 m, so the lower limit of its length is 2.4 − 0.05 = 2.35 m (M1). Since 2.35 m is less than the 2.4 m needed, the shelf might be too short, so Ben cannot be certain (A1 for the comparison and the conclusion).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Two lengths have to be compared: what is needed and what is certainly there.","Work out the total length of the cuts, then the shortest the shelf could possibly be.","Your first line multiplies the number of pieces by the length of one piece."],"codes":["N16"]},{"q":"A lorry travels 210 km at an average speed of 60 km/h, and then 90 km at an average speed of 45 km/h. Work out the average speed for the whole journey, correct to 3 significant figures. A calculator may be used.","a":"54.5 km/h","sol":"First stage: time = distance ÷ speed = 210 ÷ 60 = 3.5 hours (M1). Second stage: 90 ÷ 45 = 2 hours (M1). Total distance = 210 + 90 = 300 km and total time = 3.5 + 2 = 5.5 hours, so average speed = 300 ÷ 5.5 (M1). This gives 54.545…, which is 54.5 km/h to 3 significant figures (A1). Averaging the two speeds instead gives 52.5 km/h and scores no accuracy mark.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["An average speed for a whole journey is never the average of the two speeds.","Find the time for each stage, then use the total distance and the total time.","Your first line rearranges the speed formula to time = distance ÷ speed."],"codes":["N13","N15"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A club has 312 members. Each member pays £48.50 a year. The treasurer says the yearly income will be about £20 000. Use estimation to decide whether the treasurer is right. You must show your working.","a":"No — the income is about £15 000","sol":"Round each value to 1 significant figure: 312 ≈ 300 and £48.50 ≈ £50 (M1). Estimated income = 300 × 50 (M1). 3 × 5 = 15 and there are three zeros to replace, so the estimate is £15 000 (A1 for £15 000 together with the conclusion that £20 000 is too high). The exact value is £15 132, so the estimate is a fair one.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["An estimate is enough here — the exact total is not needed.","Round the number of members and the yearly payment to 1 significant figure, then multiply.","Your first line writes the two rounded values as a product, using ≈."],"codes":["N14"]},{"q":"A gold bar is a cuboid measuring 12 cm by 6 cm by 4 cm. The density of the gold is 19.3 g/cm³. Work out the mass of the bar in kilograms, correct to 3 significant figures. A calculator may be used.","a":"5.56 kg","sol":"Volume of the cuboid = 12 × 6 × 4 = 288 cm³ (M1). Rearranging density = mass ÷ volume gives mass = density × volume = 19.3 × 288 (M1), which is 5558.4 g (A1). Converting, 5558.4 ÷ 1000 = 5.5584 kg, which is 5.56 kg to 3 significant figures (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The density tells you the mass of each cubic centimetre of gold.","Find the volume of the cuboid first, then use the density formula rearranged for mass.","Your first line multiplies the three dimensions of the cuboid together."],"codes":["N13","N15"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Round 0.008361 correct to 2 significant figures.","a":"0.0084"},{"q":"A length L is 18 cm to the nearest centimetre. Write down the error interval for L.","a":"17.5 ≤ L < 18.5"},{"q":"A cyclist covers 400 m in 50 seconds. Work out the average speed in m/s.","a":"8 m/s"},{"q":"Estimate 8.13 × 29.6 by rounding each number to 1 significant figure.","a":"240 (from 8 × 30)"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Compound measure","back":"A measure made by dividing one quantity by another, such as speed, density or price per litre. Its unit is written the same way: km/h, g/cm³, £ per litre.","code":"N13","open":false},{"front":"Average speed","back":"Average speed = distance ÷ time. Rearranged: distance = speed × time, time = distance ÷ speed. Units must match — km with hours gives km/h, metres with seconds gives m/s.","code":"N13","open":false},{"front":"Density","back":"Density = mass ÷ volume, so mass = density × volume. Measured in g/cm³ or kg/m³. Convert the mass and volume into the units the answer needs before dividing.","code":"N13","open":false},{"front":"Price per unit (unit pricing)","back":"Price per unit = total price ÷ number of units, for example £ per litre or pence per gram. The better value is the one with the smaller price per unit.","code":"N13","open":false},{"front":"Standard metric conversions","back":"1 km = 1000 m, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 cm = 10 mm, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 tonne = 1000 kg, 1 litre = 1000 ml, 1 ml = 1 cm³, £1 = 100 p.","code":"N13","open":false},{"front":"Writing a time as a decimal number of hours","back":"Divide the minutes by 60: 45 minutes = 45 ÷ 60 = 0.75 hours, 15 minutes = 0.25 hours, 20 minutes = 1/3 hour. Needed before dividing to get a speed in km/h.","code":"N13","open":false},{"front":"Estimating a calculation","back":"Round every number to 1 significant figure, then work out the easy calculation, writing ≈ not =. The rounded calculation must be written down: a bare estimate earns no method mark.","code":"N14","open":false},{"front":"Worst case of a rounded measurement","back":"The value at the wrong end of one measurement's error interval: a rope given as 5 m to the nearest metre could be only 4.5 m, so it may not reach; a 5 kg load could be nearly 5.5 kg.","code":"N16","open":false},{"front":"Give one way to check that a calculator answer is about right","back":"Estimate by rounding each value to 1 significant figure and compare; check the size of the answer against the situation (a person's mass is not 700 kg); redo the calculation with the inverse operation.","code":"N14","open":true},{"front":"Significant figures","back":"Counted from the first non-zero digit. In 0.04862 the first significant figure is 4; to 2 significant figures it is 0.049. Zeros in front only hold place value and are not significant.","code":"N15","open":false},{"front":"The rounding rule","back":"Look at the digit after the place you are rounding to: 5 or more rounds the digit up, 4 or less leaves it as it is. Digits after the rounding place are dropped, place-holding zeros kept.","code":"N15","open":false},{"front":"Error interval for a rounded measurement","back":"A value x given as a to the nearest u lies in (a − u/2) ≤ x < (a + u/2). A length of 8 cm to the nearest cm gives 7.5 ≤ x < 8.5.","code":"N15","open":false},{"front":"Truncation","back":"Chopping off the later digits instead of rounding. A number truncated to 8.3 lies in 8.3 ≤ n < 8.4, so the stated value is always the lower limit of the interval.","code":"N15","open":false},{"front":"Appropriate degree of accuracy","back":"Money to the nearest penny (2 decimal places), and other answers to about the accuracy of the values given, usually 3 significant figures from a calculator. Never round part-way through a calculation.","code":"N15","open":false},{"front":"Limits of accuracy","back":"The values a single rounded measurement lies between, read off its error interval: a mass of 25 kg to the nearest kilogram lies in 24.5 ≤ m < 25.5, so it is at least 24.5 kg and under 25.5 kg.","code":"N16","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Preamble: the Exam Aid formulae sheet printed as an insert with every Foundation tier 1MA1 paper gives only the eight results listed next, and none of them belongs to this room, so every result marked Remember must be written from memory.","Given on the Exam Aid: area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h, where a and b are the parallel sides and h their perpendicular separation.","Given on the Exam Aid: volume of a prism = area of cross section × length.","Given on the Exam Aid: circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd, where r is the radius and d the diameter.","Given on the Exam Aid: area of a circle = πr².","Given on the Exam Aid: Pythagoras' theorem a² + b² = c², in a right-angled triangle whose hypotenuse is c.","Given on the Exam Aid: sin A = a/c, cos A = b/c, tan A = a/b, in a right-angled triangle ABC whose hypotenuse is c.","Given on the Exam Aid: compound interest total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, where P is the principal, r the interest rate per period and n the number of periods.","Given on the Exam Aid: P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B).","Remember: average speed = distance ÷ time — not given on the Exam Aid, which prints no compound measure.","Remember: distance = speed × time and time = distance ÷ speed, the two rearrangements of the speed formula.","Remember: density = mass ÷ volume, so mass = density × volume and volume = mass ÷ density.","Remember: price per unit = total price ÷ number of units; the better value is the smaller price per unit.","Remember: to estimate, round every number to 1 significant figure and work out the easy calculation, writing ≈ not =.","Remember: a value a given to the nearest u has error interval (a − u/2) ≤ x < (a + u/2), with ≤ at the lower end and < at the upper end.","Remember: a value a truncated at a place value of u has interval a ≤ x < a + u, so the value given is the lower limit.","Remember: 1 km = 1000 m, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 cm = 10 mm, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 tonne = 1000 kg, 1 litre = 1000 ml, 1 ml = 1 cm³.","Remember: minutes become a decimal number of hours by dividing by 60, so 45 minutes = 0.75 hours.","Remember: every answer in this room carries its unit — km/h, g/cm³, £ per litre, cm — because the accuracy mark depends on it."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T11:40:27Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"Answer keys, solutions, marks and units are sound throughout — all 15 bank questions, both four-item checklists and all five worked examples re-solved and confirmed, nine questions correctly flagged non-calculator with hand-workable numbers, and the Formulae sheet matches the 2026 Foundation Exam Aid exactly — so it is publishable as it stands; only the discrete-population interval in question 1 and the packed first formulae item are worth a teacher's pencil."}}},"algebra-notation":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"algebra-notation","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Every other piece of algebra you meet is written in the notation of this room. Solving an equation, using a formula in a mensuration or ratio question, reading a straight-line graph or testing a sequence rule all assume you can write a × b as ab, put a negative value in brackets before squaring it, tell an expression from an equation, an identity from a formula, collect like terms, expand and factorise, and turn a formula round to give a different letter. Marks here are lost far more often to careless notation — 0.25x written where x/4 is wanted, −4² worked out as though it were (−4)², a minus sign dropped after a bracket — than to any hard idea, and much of it is examined without a calculator, where the written method is what earns the method mark.","idea":[{"h":"The conventions of algebraic notation","t":"Algebra has a shorthand and examiners expect it. A multiplication sign is left out, so a × b is written ab and 3 × y is written 3y; repeated addition of the same letter is written as a multiple, so y + y + y is 3y; repeated multiplication is written as a power, so a × a is a², a × a × a is a³ and a × a × b is a²b. A division is written as a fraction: a ÷ b is a/b. The number multiplying a letter is its coefficient, and it is written as a fraction rather than a decimal, so x/4, never 0.25x. Brackets mean that everything inside is treated as one quantity first, so 3(x + 2) is three lots of the whole of x + 2. Questions ask you to write an expression in this form, and every later answer is marked in it.","code":"A1"},{"h":"Substituting numerical values","t":"To find a value from a formula or an expression, replace each letter by the number it stands for and then work the arithmetic out in the usual priority: brackets, then powers, then × and ÷, then + and −. The trap is negative values: write each one in brackets as you substitute, because (−4)² = 16 while −4² means −(4²) = −16. Scientific formulae such as v = u + at are substituted the same way, and the answer is given with its unit and to the accuracy asked for. The method mark is for the substitution written down, so put the line with the numbers in it on the page before you evaluate anything.","code":"A2"},{"h":"Expression, equation, formula, identity, inequality","t":"These five words are examined directly. An expression, such as 5y − 2, has no equals sign; it can be simplified or evaluated but not solved. An equation, such as 5y − 2 = 13, has an equals sign and is true only for particular values. An identity, written with ≡, is true for every value: 2(x + 3) ≡ 2x + 6. A formula, such as A = πr² or v = u + at, gives a rule for working out one quantity, its subject, from others. An inequality, such as y > 2, compares two expressions with <, ≤, > or ≥. Within an expression, a term is a part separated by + or −, and a factor is something the expression divides by exactly.","code":"A3"},{"h":"Simplifying and manipulating expressions","t":"Four techniques do most of the work. Collect like terms: only terms with exactly the same letters and powers can be joined, and each term keeps the sign in front of it. Multiply a single term over a bracket: every term inside is multiplied, signs included. Take out common factors: divide each term by the highest common factor and write it outside the bracket, and check by expanding. Expand products of two binomials: every term in the first bracket multiplies every term in the second, giving four products to collect. The same statement covers factorising x² + bx + c, where the two numbers multiply to c and add to b, the difference of two squares x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a), and the laws of indices: xᵃ × xᵇ = xᵃ⁺ᵇ, xᵃ ÷ xᵇ = xᵃ⁻ᵇ, (xᵃ)ᵇ = xᵃᵇ and x⁰ = 1. 'Simplify fully' and 'factorise fully' mean a partly finished answer scores no accuracy mark.","code":"A4"},{"h":"Standard formulae and changing the subject","t":"A formula has a subject — the letter standing alone on one side — and you are often asked for a different one. Work out what has been done to the letter you want, then undo those operations in reverse order, doing the same thing to both sides each time, until that letter stands alone and appears only once. To make t the subject of v = u + at, take u from both sides and then divide by a; to make r the subject of A = πr², divide by π and then take the square root. Standard formulae such as the area of a circle or a trapezium are printed on the Exam Aid, so the marks are for the rearranging and the substituting, not for the recall.","code":"A5"},{"h":"Showing two expressions are always equal","t":"An equation is true for particular values of the letter; an identity is true for all of them. To show two expressions are equivalent, take one side, expand every bracket and collect every like term, and show the result is exactly the other side — then say so. That single sentence is what turns working into an argument, and it is where the reasoning mark sits. Testing one value is not enough: it shows the two sides agree there, not everywhere. Once shown, the statement may be written with ≡ instead of =. The same expand-and-collect chain proves results about numbers, such as showing an expression is always even or always a multiple of 5.","code":"A6"},{"h":"An expression as a function: input and output","t":"An expression such as 3x − 1 can be read as a machine: an input x goes in, it is multiplied by 3 and then 1 is taken away, and an output comes out. Substituting an input gives the output. Running the machine backwards — undoing each operation in reverse order — takes an output back to the input that produced it, which is the same inverse-operation thinking used to change the subject of a formula. Questions give a function machine, a mapping written x → 3x − 1, or a plain expression, and ask for an output from a given input or the input that gives a stated output.","code":"A7"}],"examples":[{"title":"Substituting a negative value into a scientific formula","setup":"The kinetic energy of a moving object is E = ½mv², where m is its mass in kilograms and v is its velocity in m/s. A trolley of mass m = 6 kg is rolling backwards, so its velocity is v = −4 m/s. Work out the value of E, in joules.","steps":["Replace each letter by its value, keeping the negative one in brackets: E = ½ × 6 × (−4)²","Powers are worked out before multiplying, and (−4)² = −4 × −4 = 16","Now multiply from the left: ½ × 6 = 3, so E = 3 × 16","E = 48 J"],"cues":["Why must the velocity be written inside brackets here?","Which part of this calculation is done first?","What is left to work out once the square is known?","What unit must be attached to the value?"]},{"title":"Expanding and simplifying a product of two brackets","setup":"Expand and simplify the product (2x + 5)(x − 4), writing the answer as a quadratic expression with its terms in descending powers of x.","steps":["Every term in the left bracket multiplies every term in the right: 2x × x = 2x², 2x × (−4) = −8x, 5 × x = 5x, 5 × (−4) = −20","Written in a row, the four products give 2x² − 8x + 5x − 20","The x terms are like terms and combine: −8x + 5x = −3x","So (2x + 5)(x − 4) = 2x² − 3x − 20"],"cues":["How many separate products will multiplying out give?","Which of the four products are negative?","Which two of the four terms can be joined together?","Will the coefficient of x end up positive or negative?"]},{"title":"Changing the subject of a perimeter formula","setup":"The perimeter P of a rectangle of length l and width w is given by the formula P = 2(l + w). Rearrange this formula to make w the subject.","steps":["The bracket is multiplied by 2 last, so undo that first: divide both sides by 2 to get P/2 = l + w","The length is added to w, so subtract l from both sides: P/2 − l = w","Turn the formula round so the subject is written first: w = P/2 − l"],"cues":["Which operation is applied to the bracket last?","What is still attached to w after the first move?","On which side should the new subject be written?"]},{"title":"Deciding whether a statement is an identity","setup":"Priya says that 5(x + 2) − 3(x − 1) is the same as 2x + 13 for every value of x. Show whether Priya is right, and say whether her statement is an equation or an identity.","steps":["Multiply out the left bracket: 5(x + 2) = 5x + 10","Multiply out the right bracket, remembering the sign in front of it: −3(x − 1) = −3x + 3","Collect like terms: 5x − 3x = 2x and 10 + 3 = 13, so the whole expression is 2x + 13","That is exactly the expression Priya gave, and it came from simplifying, not from choosing a value, so she is right for every value of x","A statement true for every value is an identity, written 5(x + 2) − 3(x − 1) ≡ 2x + 13"],"cues":["What must happen to both brackets before anything can be compared?","What sign will the number term from the second bracket have?","Which terms in the four can be joined together?","Why is testing x = 1 not enough to settle this?","Which word describes a statement true for all values?"]},{"title":"Factorising fully by taking out the common factor","setup":"Factorise fully 6a²b + 15ab², giving your answer as a product of a single term and a bracket.","steps":["The highest common factor of 6 and 15 is 3, and both terms contain at least one a and one b, so the common factor is 3ab","Divide each term by that factor to find what goes inside the bracket: 6a²b ÷ 3ab = 2a and 15ab² ÷ 3ab = 5b","Write the common factor outside and the results inside: 6a²b + 15ab² = 3ab(2a + 5b)","Expanding checks it: 3ab × 2a = 6a²b and 3ab × 5b = 15ab², so the factorisation is right and nothing is left to take out"],"cues":["What number and what letters divide into both terms?","How do you find what belongs inside the bracket?","Where does the common factor go in the answer?","How can a factorisation be checked in one line?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Write 7 × y × y × y in conventional algebraic notation.","a":"7y³"},{"q":"Work out the value of 3a − b² when a = 5 and b = −3.","a":"6"},{"q":"Is 4(x + 1) = 4x + 4 an equation or an identity? Give the reason.","a":"An identity — both sides simplify to 4x + 4, so it is true for every value of x"},{"q":"Make h the subject of the formula A = ½bh.","a":"h = 2A/b"},{"q":"Simplify 8c + 5d − 3c − 9d.","a":"5c − 4d"}]},"room":{"facts":["The notation conventions: ab means a × b; 3y means y + y + y and 3 × y; a² means a × a; a²b means a × a × b; a/b means a ÷ b; brackets group a whole quantity.","A coefficient is written as a fraction, not a decimal: x/4, never 0.25x.","Substitution order: brackets, then powers, then × and ÷, then + and −, with every negative value put in brackets, so (−4)² = 16 but −4² = −16.","An expression has no equals sign; an equation is true for particular values; an identity (≡) is true for all values; a formula gives a rule for its subject; an inequality compares with <, ≤, > or ≥.","Laws of indices: xᵃ × xᵇ = xᵃ⁺ᵇ; xᵃ ÷ xᵇ = xᵃ⁻ᵇ; (xᵃ)ᵇ = xᵃᵇ; x⁰ = 1.","x² + bx + c = (x + p)(x + q) where p × q = c and p + q = b; the difference of two squares is x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a).","To change the subject, apply the inverse operation to both sides in turn until the wanted letter stands alone on one side and appears only once."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Write each of these in conventional algebraic notation: (i) t + t + t + t, (ii) m × m × n.","a":"(i) 4t (ii) m²n","sol":"(i) Four lots of t is 4 × t, and the multiplication sign is left out with the number written in front: 4t (B1). (ii) m × m is written as a power, m², and the remaining letter is written next to it with no sign: m²n (B1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Repeated addition of one letter, and repeated multiplication of one letter, each have a shorter standard form.","Count how many lots of the letter the first one has, and how many times the letter multiplies itself in the second.","Write the first as a number in front of t with the sign left out, and the second as a power of m written next to n."],"codes":["A1"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Write a ÷ b in conventional algebraic notation, and rewrite the expression 0.25x so that its coefficient is written as a fraction.","a":"a/b and x/4","sol":"A division is written as a fraction with the number being divided on top: a ÷ b = a/b (B1). A coefficient is written as a fraction rather than a decimal, and 0.25 = 25/100 = 1/4, so 0.25x is written x/4 (B1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Divisions and decimal coefficients each have a preferred written form in algebra.","A fraction bar does the same job as ÷, and a decimal coefficient is the same as a fraction of the letter.","Write the division with a horizontal bar, and convert the decimal to a fraction in its lowest terms before attaching the letter."],"codes":["A1"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Here are four statements: 5y − 2, 5y − 2 = 13, C = 5y, y > 2. Write down which one is a formula, and write down what kind of statement 5y − 2 = 13 is.","a":"C = 5y is the formula; 5y − 2 = 13 is an equation","sol":"A formula gives a rule for working out one named quantity from another, so it has a subject standing alone on one side: C = 5y (B1). 5y − 2 = 13 has an equals sign but is true only for the particular value y = 3, so it is an equation, not an identity (B1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Look at the signs first: two of the four have an equals sign, one has an inequality sign and one has neither.","A formula works out one named quantity from others; an equation with an equals sign holds only for particular values.","Ask of each statement in turn whether it has an equals sign, and if it does, whether it tells you how to work out a named quantity."],"codes":["A3"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Simplify 7a + 4b − 3a + 2b.","a":"4a + 6b","sol":"Collect the a terms, keeping the sign in front of each: 7a − 3a = 4a (B1). Collect the b terms: 4b + 2b = 6b (B1). The two results are unlike terms, so the answer is left as 4a + 6b. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Only terms with exactly the same letter can be joined together.","Deal with the a terms and the b terms separately, and keep the sign that sits in front of each term with it.","Rewrite the expression with the a terms next to each other and the b terms next to each other before combining anything."],"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"(calculator allowed) The formula v = u + at gives the velocity v, in m/s, of an object t seconds after it starts. Work out v when u = 3.2 m/s, a = 1.5 m/s² and t = 4 s.","a":"9.2 m/s","sol":"Replace each letter by its value: v = 3.2 + 1.5 × 4 (M1). Multiplication comes before addition, so 1.5 × 4 = 6 and v = 3.2 + 6 = 9.2 m/s (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Each letter in the formula stands for one of the values listed in the question.","Once the values are in place, the priority of operations decides which part is worked out first.","Write the formula out with each letter replaced by its value, then evaluate the product before the addition."],"codes":["A2"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A function takes an input x and gives the output 4x − 5. Write down the output when the input is 6, and the output when the input is −1.","a":"19 and −9","sol":"Substitute x = 6: 4 × 6 − 5 = 24 − 5 = 19 (B1). Substitute x = −1, keeping the negative input in brackets: 4 × (−1) − 5 = −4 − 5 = −9 (B1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The expression works like a machine: whatever goes in is multiplied, then something is taken away.","Put each input in place of x and carry out the two operations in the order the expression sets.","Write 4 × (the input) − 5 for each of the two inputs, putting a negative input in brackets."],"codes":["A7","A2"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A rectangle has length (x + 7) cm and width (x − 2) cm. Write an expression, in its simplest form, for the area of the rectangle in cm².","a":"x² + 5x − 14","sol":"Area = length × width = (x + 7)(x − 2). Every term in the first bracket multiplies every term in the second: x² − 2x + 7x − 14 (M1 for at least three of the four products correct). Collect the like terms, −2x + 7x = 5x, giving an area of x² + 5x − 14 cm² (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The area of a rectangle is worked out the same way whether its sides are numbers or expressions.","Multiplying two brackets means each term of one multiplies each term of the other, giving four products to collect.","Write the area as the product of the two bracketed sides, then multiply them out term by term before collecting."],"codes":["A4","A1"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Factorise x² + 7x + 12.","a":"(x + 3)(x + 4)","sol":"The expression factorises as (x + p)(x + q) where p × q = 12 and p + q = 7. The pairs of factors of 12 are 1 and 12, 2 and 6, 3 and 4, and only the last pair adds to 7 (M1). So x² + 7x + 12 = (x + 3)(x + 4) (A1), which expanding confirms. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["A quadratic in this form comes from multiplying two brackets, each beginning with x.","The two numbers in the brackets multiply to give the number term and add to give the coefficient of x.","List the pairs of whole numbers whose product is the number term, then test which pair has the right sum."],"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Simplify (p⁵ × p⁴) ÷ p³.","a":"p⁶","sol":"Multiplying powers of the same letter adds the indices: p⁵ × p⁴ = p⁵⁺⁴ = p⁹ (M1). Dividing subtracts them: p⁹ ÷ p³ = p⁹⁻³ = p⁶ (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["All three powers are powers of the same letter, so the laws of indices apply throughout.","One law handles the multiplication and a second handles the division; work the bracket out first.","Add the indices of the two powers being multiplied, then subtract the index of the power being divided by."],"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out the value of 5x² − 3y when x = −3 and y = −2.","a":"51","sol":"Substitute, keeping each negative value in brackets: 5 × (−3)² − 3 × (−2). Powers first: (−3)² = 9, so the first term is 5 × 9 = 45, and the second is −3 × (−2) = +6 (M1 for either term correct). Adding them: 45 + 6 = 51 (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["A squared negative number and a subtracted negative number both need care in this one.","Replace the letters first, with brackets round each negative value, then follow the priority of operations.","Write 5 × (the x value in brackets)² − 3 × (the y value in brackets) before working anything out."],"codes":["A2"]},{"q":"(calculator allowed) Make t the subject of the formula v = u + at.","a":"t = (v − u)/a","sol":"The term at is added to u, so subtract u from both sides: v − u = at (M1). t is multiplied by a, so divide both sides by a: (v − u)/a = t (A1). Written with the subject first, t = (v − u)/a. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The letter you want is inside a product that is being added to another letter.","Undo the operations in the reverse of the order they were applied, doing the same to both sides each time.","Take the letter that is added to the at term away from both sides first, then deal with what multiplies t."],"codes":["A5"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Show that 3(2y − 1) + 5 is equal to 6y + 2 for every value of y, and name the kind of statement this makes.","a":"3(2y − 1) + 5 = 6y − 3 + 5 = 6y + 2, so the statement is an identity","sol":"Expand the bracket: 3 × 2y = 6y and 3 × (−1) = −3, giving 6y − 3 + 5 (M1). Collect the number terms, −3 + 5 = 2, so the left-hand side simplifies to 6y + 2, which is exactly the right-hand side (A1). The two sides agree for every value of y, not just particular ones, so the statement is an identity and may be written 3(2y − 1) + 5 ≡ 6y + 2 (B1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Showing two expressions are always equal means simplifying one of them until it matches the other.","Multiply the bracket out, collect the number terms, and compare the result with the expression on the right.","Multiply each term inside the bracket by the number in front of it, leaving the + 5 where it stands."],"codes":["A6","A4","A3"]},{"q":"(calculator allowed) The formula C = 5/9 (F − 32) changes a temperature F, in degrees Fahrenheit, into the temperature C, in degrees Celsius. Rearrange the formula to make F the subject, and use it to work out F when C = 25.","a":"F = 9C/5 + 32, and F = 77","sol":"The bracket is multiplied by 5/9, so multiply both sides by its reciprocal 9/5: 9C/5 = F − 32 (M1). Add 32 to both sides: F = 9C/5 + 32 (A1). Substitute C = 25: F = 9 × 25 ÷ 5 + 32 = 45 + 32 (M1). F = 77 (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["F sits inside a bracket that is being multiplied by a fraction, so that fraction has to come off first.","Undo the multiplication by the fraction by multiplying both sides by its reciprocal, then undo the subtraction inside the bracket.","Multiply both sides by 9/5 to clear the fraction, then deal with the number that is being subtracted from F."],"codes":["A5","A2"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The sides of a triangle are (3x + 1) cm, (2x − 3) cm and (x + 5) cm. Write an expression in its simplest form for the perimeter of the triangle, and work out the perimeter when x = 4.","a":"Perimeter = 6x + 3; when x = 4 the perimeter is 27 cm","sol":"The perimeter is the sum of the three sides: (3x + 1) + (2x − 3) + (x + 5) (M1). Collect the x terms, 3x + 2x + x = 6x, and the number terms, 1 − 3 + 5 = 3, giving 6x + 3 (A1). Substitute x = 4 into the simplified expression: 6 × 4 + 3 (M1). This gives 24 + 3 = 27 cm (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A perimeter is found in the same way whether the sides are numbers or expressions.","Add the three side expressions and collect like terms, then substitute the given value into the simplified expression.","Write the three side expressions in a row joined by + signs, then group the x terms and the number terms."],"codes":["A4","A2","A1"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Priya adds 3(2n + 1) to 4(n − 3), where n is a whole number. Show that her result is always an odd number.","a":"The sum is 10n − 9, which is always odd because 10n is always even and an even number minus an odd number is odd","sol":"Expand both brackets: 3(2n + 1) = 6n + 3 and 4(n − 3) = 4n − 12 (M1). Add them and collect like terms: 6n + 4n = 10n and 3 − 12 = −9, so the sum is 10n − 9 (A1). 10n is a multiple of 10, so it is even for every whole number n (B1). An even number take away an odd number is odd, so 10n − 9 is odd whatever whole number n is (B1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Nothing can be decided while the brackets are still there, so the two expressions must first become one.","Expand and collect, then look at whether each part of the result is always even or always odd.","Multiply out each bracket by the number in front of it and write the four terms in a row before collecting."],"codes":["A6","A4"]},{"q":"(calculator allowed) The area of a circle is given by A = πr², where r is the radius. Make r the subject of this formula, and use it to find the radius of a circle of area 120 cm², giving your answer to 1 decimal place.","a":"r = √(A/π), and r = 6.2 cm","sol":"Divide both sides by π to leave the square on its own: A/π = r² (M1). Take the positive square root of both sides, since a radius is a length: r = √(A/π) (A1). Substitute A = 120: r = √(120 ÷ π) = √38.197… (M1). r = 6.180… = 6.2 cm to 1 decimal place (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The area formula itself is given to you, so the work is in turning it round and then substituting.","Undo the multiplication by π first, and then undo the squaring by the inverse operation.","Divide both sides by π so that r² stands alone, then apply the inverse of squaring to both sides."],"codes":["A5","A2"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write 4 × p × p × q in conventional algebraic notation.","a":"4p²q"},{"q":"Work out the value of 2x² + y when x = −5 and y = 3.","a":"53"},{"q":"Factorise x² − 36.","a":"(x + 6)(x − 6)"},{"q":"Make b the subject of the formula P = a + 2b.","a":"b = (P − a)/2"}]},"cards":[{"front":"The conventions of algebraic notation","back":"ab means a × b; 3y means y + y + y and 3 × y; a² means a × a; a²b means a × a × b; a/b means a ÷ b.","code":"A1","open":false},{"front":"Coefficient","back":"The number multiplying a letter in a term: in 7x it is 7, in −3x² it is −3. A coefficient is written as a fraction, x/4, not as a decimal, 0.25x.","code":"A1","open":false},{"front":"Term (in an algebraic expression)","back":"A part of an expression separated from the next by + or −. In 5x² − 3x + 7 the terms are 5x², −3x and 7.","code":"A3","open":false},{"front":"Factor of an expression","back":"Something the expression divides by exactly. 3 and (x + 2) are factors of 3x + 6, because 3x + 6 = 3(x + 2).","code":"A3","open":false},{"front":"Expression","back":"A collection of terms with no equals sign, such as 4x − 9. It can be simplified or evaluated, but it cannot be solved.","code":"A3","open":false},{"front":"Equation","back":"A statement with an equals sign that is true only for particular values, such as 3x + 1 = 7, which holds only when x = 2.","code":"A3","open":false},{"front":"Identity","back":"A statement true for every value of the letter, written with ≡, such as 2(x + 3) ≡ 2x + 6. Both sides simplify to the same expression.","code":"A6","open":false},{"front":"Formula","back":"A rule connecting quantities, with its subject standing alone on one side, such as A = πr² or v = u + at. Substituting the other letters gives the subject's value.","code":"A5","open":false},{"front":"Inequality","back":"A statement comparing two expressions with <, ≤, > or ≥, such as y > 2, which is true for a range of values rather than for one.","code":"A3","open":false},{"front":"Priority of operations when substituting","back":"Brackets, then powers, then × and ÷, then + and −. Write a negative value in brackets first: (−4)² = 16, but −4² = −16.","code":"A2","open":false},{"front":"Laws of indices","back":"xᵃ × xᵇ = xᵃ⁺ᵇ; xᵃ ÷ xᵇ = xᵃ⁻ᵇ; (xᵃ)ᵇ = xᵃᵇ; x⁰ = 1.","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Difference of two squares","back":"x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a). So x² − 25 factorises as (x + 5)(x − 5).","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Factorising x² + bx + c","back":"Find two numbers with product c and sum b, then write (x + p)(x + q). For x² + 9x + 20 the numbers are 4 and 5.","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Changing the subject of a formula","back":"Apply the inverse operation to both sides in turn until the wanted letter stands alone on one side and appears only once.","code":"A5","open":false},{"front":"An expression read as a function","back":"3x − 1 acts as a machine: an input x gives an output. Substituting finds the output; undoing the operations in reverse traces an output back to its input.","code":"A7","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Preamble: none of this room's own notation, index and factorising results is printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid (W87990A), the insert in every Foundation paper, so every item below marked Remember must be written from memory; the items marked Printed are given to you on that sheet, so a question that substitutes into one of them, or rearranges it, prints it for you.","Remember: ab means a × b — the multiplication sign is left out. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: 3y means y + y + y, and also 3 × y. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: a² means a × a. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: a³ means a × a × a. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: a²b means a × a × b. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: a/b means a ÷ b — a division is written as a fraction. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: a coefficient is written as a fraction, x/4, never as a decimal, 0.25x. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: brackets hold a whole quantity together, so 3(x + 2) means three lots of the whole of x + 2. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: the priority of operations when you substitute — brackets, then powers, then × and ÷, then + and −. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: put a negative value in brackets when you substitute it, so that (−4)² = 16 while −4² = −16. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: xᵃ × xᵇ = xᵃ⁺ᵇ. The Foundation sheet says nothing about the laws of indices, so remember it.","Remember: xᵃ ÷ xᵇ = xᵃ⁻ᵇ. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: (xᵃ)ᵇ = xᵃᵇ. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: x⁰ = 1. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: x² + bx + c = (x + p)(x + q), where p × q = c and p + q = b. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: the difference of two squares, x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a). Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: to change the subject, apply the inverse operation to both sides in turn until the wanted letter stands alone and appears only once. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: area of a triangle = ½ × base × height, the formula behind A = ½bh. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h, where a and b are the parallel sides and h their perpendicular separation.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: volume of a prism = area of cross section × length.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd, where r is the radius and d the diameter.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: area of a circle = πr², the formula this room's stretch question asks you to rearrange.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: Pythagoras' theorem a² + b² = c², in a right-angled triangle whose hypotenuse is c.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: sin A = a/c, in a right-angled triangle ABC whose hypotenuse is c and whose side a is opposite angle A.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: cos A = b/c, where b is the side adjacent to angle A and c the hypotenuse.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: tan A = a/b, where a is opposite angle A and b adjacent to it.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, where P is the principal, r the interest rate per period and n the number of periods.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B)."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T11:43:19.036509+00:00","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.86,"notes":"Every answer key, solution line and mark tally checks out (all 16 bank questions, the 4 exit questions, the 5 lesson checks and all 5 worked examples re-solved in sympy), A1–A7 are covered in order with nothing beyond Foundation — the only repairs needed are to the wording of the Formulae sheet extra."}}},"algebra-equations":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"algebra-equations","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Solving is where algebra earns its keep: once a situation has been turned into an equation, the answer follows from a method you can learn, write down and check. This room holds the solving methods the Foundation papers return to again and again — inverse operations for a linear equation, including one with the unknown on both sides, factorising for a quadratic, elimination or substitution for a pair of simultaneous equations, and the same moves once more for an inequality, where multiplying or dividing by a negative turns the sign round. It also holds the step that decides most problem-solving questions: naming the unknown and writing down the relation the words describe. Prices, perimeters, ages, numbers of pupils and consecutive numbers are all set as equations, and a graph gives the same answers, because the solution of one equation is where two graphs cross and the roots of a quadratic are where its curve cuts the x axis. Every answer here can be checked by substituting it back into the original statement, so a mark you have earned need never be lost.","idea":[{"h":"Solving a linear equation by inverse operations","t":"An equation says that two expressions have the same value, and solving it means finding the value of the unknown that makes that true. Keep the equation balanced: whatever you do to one side you do to the other. Undo the operations in reverse order — take the number away before dividing by the coefficient, and clear any bracket or fraction first, by expanding or by multiplying every term by the denominator. When the unknown is on both sides, as in 9x + 1 = 5x + 13, subtract the smaller x term from both sides so the unknown is on one side only, then collect the numbers on the other. The same equation can be solved approximately from a graph: draw y = each side on the same axes and read off the x coordinate of the point where the two graphs cross. Always substitute your value back into the original equation to check.","code":"A17"},{"h":"Solving a quadratic equation by factorising","t":"A quadratic equation must first be written with zero on one side, in the form x² + bx + c = 0; if it reads x² + 4x = 21 you subtract 21 from both sides before doing anything else. Then factorise: find two numbers that multiply to give the constant term and add to give the coefficient of x, so that the left-hand side becomes a product of two brackets. Now use the fact that a product can only be zero when one of the things multiplied is zero, so set each bracket equal to zero in turn. That gives two one-step equations and therefore two roots, and both must be written down unless the situation rules one out. On a graph the same roots are the x coordinates of the points where the curve y = x² + bx + c crosses the x axis, which is how approximate solutions are found when the quadratic will not factorise.","code":"A18"},{"h":"Solving two simultaneous linear equations","t":"Two equations in two unknowns are solved together, because only one pair of values satisfies both. To eliminate, multiply one or both equations until one unknown has coefficients of the same size, then add the equations if the signs of those terms are opposite and subtract if they are the same. That leaves one equation in one unknown; solve it, then back-substitute into either original equation to get the second value, and check the pair in the equation you did not use. To substitute instead, rearrange one equation to give one unknown in terms of the other and put that expression into the second equation. The pair of values is the point (x, y) where the graphs of the two lines cross, so a drawn pair of lines gives an approximate solution and a useful check on the algebra.","code":"A19"},{"h":"Turning a situation into an equation and back again","t":"Most marks in a problem-solving question are for the translation, not the arithmetic. Name the unknown and say what it stands for, with its unit — let x be the cost of one pen in pence, let n be the number of pupils. Write every other quantity in terms of that letter: a stamp costing 30p more than another is (x + 30) pence, the length of a rectangle 3 cm longer than its width is (w + 3) cm. Then write the equals sign the situation gives you: a total paid, a perimeter, two things that must be equal, or a product. Solve the equation, then interpret the solution: answer the question that was actually asked, in context and with its unit, and reject any root the situation makes impossible, such as a negative length or a negative number of people. Where two quantities are unknown, two separate statements give two simultaneous equations.","code":"A21"},{"h":"Solving a linear inequality and showing the solution set","t":"An inequality is solved with exactly the same moves as an equation — add, subtract, multiply or divide both sides — with one extra rule: multiplying or dividing both sides by a negative number reverses the inequality sign, so from −3x > 9 you get x < −3. Adding and subtracting never change the sign. The answer is not a single value but a solution set, and it must be written with the inequality sign, as x ≤ 4, not as x = 4. On a number line the end value is marked with a closed (filled) circle for ≤ or ≥, because that value is included, and with an open circle for < or >, because it is not; a line with an arrow then runs towards the values that satisfy the inequality. If a question asks for integer solutions, list the whole numbers inside the set, taking care at the end value.","code":"A22"}],"examples":[{"title":"A linear equation with the unknown on both sides","setup":"Solve the equation 5x − 3 = 2x + 9, showing your working. No calculator is allowed.","steps":["Subtract 2x from both sides so the unknown appears on one side only: 3x − 3 = 9","Add 3 to both sides so that the x term stands alone: 3x = 12","Divide both sides by 3, the line the mark scheme credits as the method: x = 4","Check by substituting back: 5 × 4 − 3 = 17 and 2 × 4 + 9 = 17, so the solution x = 4 is correct"],"cues":["Which x term should be removed, and from which side?","What is still attached to the x term on the left?","What single operation is left to do?","How can you be certain the value is right?"]},{"title":"A quadratic equation solved by factorising","setup":"Solve the equation x² + 5x + 6 = 0 by factorising, giving both solutions. No calculator is allowed.","steps":["Two numbers are needed that multiply to give 6 and add to give 5: they are 2 and 3","Write the left-hand side as a product of two brackets, the line that earns the method mark: (x + 2)(x + 3) = 0","A product is zero only when one of the factors is zero, so x + 2 = 0 or x + 3 = 0","Solving each in turn: x = −2 or x = −3"],"cues":["What pair of conditions must the two numbers satisfy?","How are those two numbers used to rewrite the expression?","What does a product of zero tell you about the factors?","What values come out of the two small equations?"]},{"title":"Simultaneous equations by elimination, read on the graph","setup":"Solve the simultaneous equations 3x + 2y = 12 and x − y = 1. Give the values of x and y, and say what the pair of values means on the graphs of the two lines. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Multiply the second equation through by 2 so that the y terms are the same size: 2x − 2y = 2","The y terms now have opposite signs, so adding the two equations removes y: 5x = 14","Divide both sides by 5: x = 2.8","Substitute into x − y = 1: 2.8 − y = 1, so y = 1.8","The solution x = 2.8, y = 1.8 is the point (2.8, 1.8) at which the graphs of the two lines cross"],"cues":["Which unknown is easier to match here, and by what multiplier?","Should the two equations now be added or subtracted?","What is left to do to the equation in x alone?","Which original equation is quickest for finding the second value?","What does the pair of values look like on a pair of axes?"]},{"title":"Deriving an equation from a described shape","setup":"A rectangle measures 3 cm more in length than in width, and the distance all the way round it is 26 cm. Form an equation for the width and solve it, then state the width and the length in cm. No calculator is allowed.","steps":["Let the width be w cm, so the length is (w + 3) cm and the four sides add to w + (w + 3) + w + (w + 3)","Collect the terms and set the total equal to the distance round the shape, the line that earns the method mark: 4w + 6 = 26","Subtract 6 from both sides: 4w = 20","Divide both sides by 4: w = 5","The width is 5 cm and the length is 5 + 3 = 8 cm"],"cues":["Which side should be called w, and how is the other written?","What does the sum of the four sides have to equal?","Which of the two operations on the left should be undone first?","What is still being done to w, and what undoes it?","What was actually asked for, and in what unit?"]},{"title":"An inequality and its solution set on a number line","setup":"Solve the inequality 4x − 5 ≤ 11, and describe exactly how the solution set is drawn on a number line. No calculator is allowed.","steps":["Add 5 to both sides, exactly as you would for an equation: 4x ≤ 16","Divide both sides by 4; that divisor is positive, so the inequality sign does not change: x ≤ 4","On the number line, put a closed (filled) circle at 4, because that value itself satisfies x ≤ 4","Draw a line with an arrow from the circle towards the smaller numbers, so the solution set x ≤ 4 covers every value from 4 downwards"],"cues":["What is the first move, and does the sign change?","Is the divisor positive or negative, and why does that matter?","Open circle or closed circle here, and what decides it?","Which way should the arrow point?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Solve 3x + 8 = 29.","a":"x = 7"},{"q":"Solve (x + 4)(x − 7) = 0.","a":"x = −4 or x = 7"},{"q":"Solve x² − 2x − 15 = 0 by factorising.","a":"x = 5 or x = −3"},{"q":"Solve the simultaneous equations x + y = 9 and x − y = 3.","a":"x = 6, y = 3"},{"q":"Solve 2x − 1 > 7 and write down the smallest integer that satisfies it.","a":"x > 4, and the smallest integer is 5"},{"q":"A number is doubled and then 5 is added; the result is 31. Form an equation and find the number.","a":"2n + 5 = 31, so n = 13"}]},"room":{"facts":["An equation stays true if the same thing is done to both sides; solve by inverse operations, undoing in reverse order (+ undoes −, × undoes ÷).","When the unknown is on both sides, subtract the smaller x term from both sides first, then collect the numbers on the other side.","A quadratic must be rearranged into the form x² + bx + c = 0 before it is factorised: x² + bx + c = (x + p)(x + q), where p × q = c and p + q = b.","If a product is zero then one of the factors is zero: (x + p)(x + q) = 0 gives x = −p or x = −q, so a quadratic has two roots.","Elimination: scale one or both equations until an unknown has coefficients of the same size, add when their signs are opposite and subtract when they are the same, then back-substitute for the other unknown.","A graph gives the same solutions: the solution of one equation is the x coordinate where the two graphs cross, two simultaneous linear equations are solved at the point where the lines cross, and the roots of a quadratic are where its curve cuts the x axis.","An inequality is solved like an equation, but multiplying or dividing both sides by a negative number reverses the sign; on a number line use a closed circle for ≤ or ≥ and an open circle for < or >."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve 4x + 7 = 23.","a":"x = 4","sol":"Subtract 7 from both sides so that only the x term is left: 4x = 16 (M1). Divide both sides by 4: x = 4 (A1). Check: 4 × 4 + 7 = 23. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Something has been done to x twice; solving means undoing both, in the opposite order.","Get the term in x by itself first, then remove the number it is multiplied by.","Subtract 7 from both sides, then divide both sides by the coefficient of x."],"codes":["A17"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve 7x + 2 = 4x + 17.","a":"x = 5","sol":"Subtract 4x from both sides so the unknown is on one side only: 3x + 2 = 17 (M1). Subtract 2 from both sides: 3x = 15 (M1). Divide both sides by 3: x = 5 (A1). Check: 7 × 5 + 2 = 37 and 4 × 5 + 17 = 37. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The unknown appears on both sides, so the first job is to get it onto one side only.","Take the smaller number of x from both sides, then deal with the numbers in the same way.","Subtract 4x from both sides, then move the constant across before dividing."],"codes":["A17"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve (x − 3)(x + 8) = 0.","a":"x = 3 or x = −8","sol":"A product is zero only when one of its factors is zero, so x − 3 = 0 or x + 8 = 0 (M1). Solving each in turn gives x = 3 or x = −8 (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Two quantities have been multiplied together and the result is zero.","That can only happen if at least one of the two quantities is itself zero.","Set each bracket in turn equal to zero and solve the two one-step equations that gives."],"codes":["A18"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve the inequality 3x + 4 < 19.","a":"x < 5","sol":"Subtract 4 from both sides: 3x < 15 (M1). Divide both sides by 3; the divisor is positive, so the inequality sign is unchanged: x < 5 (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["An inequality is solved with the same moves as an equation; only the sign in the middle is different.","Undo the addition first and then the multiplication, watching whether the sign has to turn round.","Subtract 4 from both sides, then divide by the coefficient of x and keep the inequality sign in your answer."],"codes":["A22"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve the simultaneous equations x + y = 15 and x − y = 3.","a":"x = 9, y = 6","sol":"The y terms are + y and − y, so adding the two equations eliminates y: 2x = 18 (M1). Divide by 2: x = 9 (A1). Substitute into x + y = 15: 9 + y = 15, so y = 6 (A1). Check in x − y = 3: 9 − 6 = 3. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Look at the y terms: one is added and the other is subtracted.","Combining the two equations in the right way will make one of the unknowns disappear.","Add the two left-hand sides and the two right-hand sides, then use either original equation for the second value."],"codes":["A19"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve 5(2x − 3) = 3x + 13.","a":"x = 4","sol":"Expand the bracket: 10x − 15 = 3x + 13 (M1). Subtract 3x from both sides and add 15 to both sides: 7x = 28 (M1). Divide by 7: x = 4 (A1). Check: 5 × (8 − 3) = 25 and 3 × 4 + 13 = 25. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The bracket has to go before any terms can be collected.","Multiply everything inside the bracket by the number outside, then gather the x terms on one side and the numbers on the other.","Multiply both terms inside the bracket by 5, then subtract the smaller x term from both sides."],"codes":["A17"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve x² + 3x − 28 = 0.","a":"x = −7 or x = 4","sol":"Two numbers that multiply to −28 and add to 3 are 7 and −4, so the equation factorises as (x + 7)(x − 4) = 0 (M1). Set each bracket equal to zero: x + 7 = 0 or x − 4 = 0 (M1). Solving gives x = −7 or x = 4 (A1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The equation already has zero on one side, so it is ready to be written as a product.","Look for two numbers that multiply to give the constant term and add to give the coefficient of x.","Write out the factor pairs of the constant term and look for the pair whose difference is the coefficient of x, remembering that one of the two numbers must be negative."],"codes":["A18"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve x² + 6x = 16.","a":"x = −8 or x = 2","sol":"Subtract 16 from both sides so that one side is zero: x² + 6x − 16 = 0 (M1). Two numbers that multiply to −16 and add to 6 are 8 and −2, so (x + 8)(x − 2) = 0 (M1). Each bracket in turn is zero, giving x = −8 or x = 2 (A1). Check: (−8)² + 6 × (−8) = 64 − 48 = 16 and 2² + 6 × 2 = 16. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A quadratic cannot be factorised for solving while there is a number on the right-hand side.","Move that number across first, then factorise the quadratic that is left.","Subtract 16 from both sides to make the right-hand side zero, then look for two numbers multiplying to the new constant term and adding to 6."],"codes":["A18"]},{"q":"(a calculator may be used) Solve the simultaneous equations 3x + 4y = 18 and 5x − 2y = 4.","a":"x = 2, y = 3","sol":"Multiply the second equation through by 2 so that the y terms are the same size: 10x − 4y = 8 (M1). The y terms now have opposite signs, so add this to 3x + 4y = 18: 13x = 26 (M1). Divide by 13: x = 2 (A1). Substitute into 3x + 4y = 18: 6 + 4y = 18, so 4y = 12 and y = 3 (A1). Check in 5x − 2y = 4: 10 − 6 = 4. Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Neither unknown has matching coefficients yet, so one of the equations has to be scaled first.","Double the second equation so the y terms are the same size, then combine the two equations.","Multiply every term of 5x − 2y = 4 by 2, then decide whether to add or subtract by looking at the signs of the y terms."],"codes":["A19"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A pen costs x pence. A notebook costs 40 pence more than a pen. Ellie buys 3 pens and 2 notebooks and pays £3.55 altogether. Form an equation in x and solve it to find the cost of one pen.","a":"55p (x = 55)","sol":"Work in pence throughout. A notebook costs (x + 40) pence, so 3 pens and 2 notebooks give 3x + 2(x + 40) = 355 (M1). Expand the bracket and collect: 5x + 80 = 355 (M1). Subtract 80 from both sides: 5x = 275 (M1). Divide by 5: x = 55, so one pen costs 55p (A1). Check: 3 × 55 + 2 × 95 = 165 + 190 = 355 pence = £3.55. Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Work in pence throughout so that every number in the equation is a whole number.","Write the cost of one notebook in terms of x, then write the cost of three pens and two notebooks added together.","Add three lots of x to two lots of (x + 40), set that total equal to the amount paid in pence, and expand the bracket."],"codes":["A21","A17"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve the inequality 5 − 2x < 13 and write down the smallest integer that satisfies it.","a":"x > −4, and the smallest integer is −3","sol":"Subtract 5 from both sides: −2x < 8 (M1). Divide both sides by −2; dividing by a negative number reverses the inequality sign: x > −4 (A1). The integers greater than −4 begin at −3, so the smallest integer in the solution set is −3 (B1). Check: x = −3 gives 5 + 6 = 11, which is less than 13, while x = −4 gives exactly 13. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The term in x here is negative, and that changes one thing before the answer is written down.","Solve it as you would an equation, but watch what happens to the sign when you divide by a negative number.","Subtract 5 from both sides, then divide by the coefficient of x and turn the inequality sign round."],"codes":["A22"]},{"q":"(a calculator may be used) In a café, 2 teas and 3 coffees cost £9.60 altogether, and 4 teas and 1 coffee cost £8.20 altogether. Work out the cost of one tea and the cost of one coffee.","a":"A tea costs £1.50 and a coffee costs £2.20","sol":"Let £t be the cost of a tea and £c the cost of a coffee, so 2t + 3c = 9.60 and 4t + c = 8.20 (M1). Multiply the second equation by 3 so that the c terms match: 12t + 3c = 24.60 (M1). Subtract the first equation from this: 10t = 15 (M1). Divide by 10: t = 1.50, so a tea costs £1.50 (A1). Substitute into 4t + c = 8.20: 6.00 + c = 8.20, so c = 2.20 and a coffee costs £2.20 (A1). Check: 2 × 1.50 + 3 × 2.20 = 3.00 + 6.60 = £9.60. Total 5 marks.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["There are two unknown prices here, so two separate statements are needed before anything can be solved.","Let t and c stand for the two prices in pounds, write one equation for each order, then eliminate one of them.","Write an equation for each order in pounds, then multiply the second one through so that its c term is the same size as the c term in the first."],"codes":["A21","A19"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The product of two consecutive positive whole numbers is 72. By forming and solving a quadratic equation, find the two numbers.","a":"8 and 9","sol":"Let the smaller number be n, so the next one is (n + 1) and n(n + 1) = 72 (M1). Expand and subtract 72 from both sides: n² + n − 72 = 0 (M1). Two numbers that multiply to −72 and add to 1 are 9 and −8, so (n + 9)(n − 8) = 0 (M1). This gives n = −9 or n = 8, and the numbers are positive, so n = 8 (A1). The two numbers are therefore 8 and 9 (A1). Check: 8 × 9 = 72. Total 5 marks.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Consecutive whole numbers differ by 1, so one letter is enough to describe both.","Write the product of the two numbers in terms of that letter, set it equal to the given product, and rearrange into a quadratic.","Call the smaller number n, multiply n by (n + 1), then move every term to one side so that the other side is zero."],"codes":["A21","A18"]},{"q":"(a calculator may be used) The lines y = 2x − 3 and y = 12 − x are drawn on the same axes. (a) Use algebra to find the coordinates of the point where the two lines cross. (b) Hence write down the solution of the equation 2x − 3 = 12 − x.","a":"(a) (5, 7)  (b) x = 5","sol":"(a) At the point where the lines cross, the two expressions for y are equal: 2x − 3 = 12 − x (M1). Add x to both sides and add 3 to both sides: 3x = 15, so x = 5 (M1). Substituting into y = 2x − 3 gives y = 7, and 12 − 5 = 7 confirms it, so the lines cross at (5, 7) (A1). (b) At that crossing point both expressions take the same value, so its x coordinate is the value of x that makes 2x − 3 = 12 − x true, and the solution is therefore x = 5 (B1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["At a crossing point, a value of x gives the same y on both lines.","Put the two expressions for y equal to each other, solve for x, and then use either line to find y.","Write 2x − 3 = 12 − x, then collect the x terms on one side and the numbers on the other."],"codes":["A17","A19"]},{"q":"(a calculator may be used) A school trip costs £45 for each pupil plus a fixed coach charge of £320. The school can spend at most £2000 on the trip. By forming and solving an inequality, work out the greatest number of pupils who can go.","a":"37 pupils","sol":"Let n be the number of pupils. The total cost is (45n + 320) pounds and it can be at most £2000, so 45n + 320 ≤ 2000 (M1). Subtract 320 from both sides: 45n ≤ 1680 (M1). Divide both sides by 45: n ≤ 37.33… (A1). The number of pupils must be a whole number, so the greatest number who can go is 37 (A1). Check: 45 × 37 + 320 = £1985, which is within budget, while 38 pupils would cost £2030. Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The budget is a limit rather than an exact amount, so the statement you write will not be an equation.","Write the total cost for n pupils, say that it is at most the budget, and solve for n.","Form the inequality with the cost per pupil times n plus the fixed charge on the left, then subtract the fixed charge from both sides."],"codes":["A21","A22"]},{"q":"(a calculator may be used) In a sale a shop reduces the price of a coat by 20%. Jack pays £68 for the coat in the sale. Let £x be the price of the coat before the sale. Form an equation and solve it to find the price before the sale, and work out how much Jack saved.","a":"£85 before the sale, a saving of £17","sol":"A reduction of 20% leaves 80% of the original price, so 0.8x = 68 (M1). Divide both sides by 0.8: x = 68 ÷ 0.8 (M1). This gives x = 85, so the coat cost £85 before the sale (A1). The saving is 85 − 68 = £17 (B1). Check: 20% of £85 = £17 and £85 − £17 = £68. Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The £68 is what is left after the reduction, not the size of the reduction.","Write the sale price as a multiplier of the original price, form an equation and solve it.","Express the sale price as a decimal multiplier of x, set that expression equal to the price paid, then divide both sides by the multiplier."],"codes":["A21","A17"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Solve 6x − 5 = 3x + 13.","a":"x = 6"},{"q":"Solve x² + 2x − 15 = 0.","a":"x = 3 or x = −5"},{"q":"Solve the simultaneous equations 2x + y = 11 and x − y = 1.","a":"x = 4, y = 3"},{"q":"Solve the inequality 3 − 4x ≥ 15.","a":"x ≤ −3"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Solving an equation","back":"Finding the value of the unknown that makes the two sides equal. Every step does the same thing to both sides, so the equation stays balanced and stays true.","code":"A17","open":false},{"front":"Inverse operations","back":"The operation that undoes another: + undoes −, × undoes ÷, and a square is undone by a square root, but x² = k has two roots, x = ±√k. Undo a linear equation's operations in reverse order.","code":"A17","open":false},{"front":"First move when the unknown is on both sides","back":"Subtract the smaller x term from both sides, so the unknown is left on one side only: from 9x + 1 = 5x + 13, subtracting 5x leaves 4x + 1 = 13.","code":"A17","open":false},{"front":"Approximate solution of an equation from a graph","back":"Draw y = each side of the equation on one pair of axes; the x coordinate of the point where the two graphs cross is the solution, read off to the accuracy of the scale.","code":"A17","open":false},{"front":"Standard form of a quadratic equation","back":"ax² + bx + c = 0, with every term on one side and 0 on the other. A quadratic must be rearranged into this form before it is factorised and solved.","code":"A18","open":false},{"front":"The zero product property","back":"If two quantities multiply to give 0, one of them must be 0. So (x + p)(x + q) = 0 gives x = −p or x = −q.","code":"A18","open":false},{"front":"Factorising x² + bx + c","back":"x² + bx + c = (x + p)(x + q), where p × q = c and p + q = b: two numbers multiplying to the constant term and adding to the coefficient of x.","code":"A18","open":false},{"front":"Roots of a quadratic from its graph","back":"The solutions of x² + bx + c = 0 are the x coordinates of the points where the curve y = x² + bx + c crosses the x axis, which is how approximate roots are read off.","code":"A18","open":false},{"front":"Elimination method for simultaneous equations","back":"Scale one or both equations until an unknown has coefficients of the same size, then add if their signs are opposite and subtract if they are the same; solve, then back-substitute.","code":"A19","open":false},{"front":"Substitution method for simultaneous equations","back":"Rearrange one equation to give one unknown in terms of the other, put that expression into the second equation, solve for one unknown, then back-substitute for the other.","code":"A19","open":false},{"front":"Simultaneous equations on a graph","back":"The solution of two simultaneous linear equations is the point (x, y) where the graphs of the two lines cross; parallel lines never cross, so such a pair has no solution.","code":"A19","open":false},{"front":"Deriving an equation from a situation","back":"Name the unknown and its unit, write every other quantity in terms of that letter, then write down the equals sign the situation gives: a total, a perimeter or a product.","code":"A21","open":false},{"front":"Interpreting the solution in context","back":"Turn the value of the unknown back into the answer asked for, with its unit, and reject any solution the situation makes impossible, such as a negative length or a negative number of people.","code":"A21","open":false},{"front":"Multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number","back":"The inequality sign reverses: from −3x > 9, dividing both sides by −3 gives x < −3. Adding, subtracting, and multiplying or dividing by a positive number leave the sign unchanged.","code":"A22","open":false},{"front":"Number line convention for a solution set","back":"A closed (filled) circle at the end value for ≤ or ≥, an open circle for < or >, with a line and arrow drawn towards the values that satisfy the inequality.","code":"A22","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Nothing this room needs is printed on the Foundation Exam Aid (W87990A). Its only sections are area of a trapezium, volume of a prism, circumference and area of a circle, Pythagoras' theorem and the three trigonometric ratios, compound interest and P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B). Every rule below has to be remembered.","Recall: an equation stays true if the same operation is done to both sides; solve by inverse operations, clearing brackets and fractions first.","Recall: when the unknown is on both sides, subtract the smaller x term from both sides before collecting the numbers on the other side.","Recall: a quadratic is solved from the form x² + bx + c = 0 — rearrange until one side is zero before factorising.","Recall: x² + bx + c = (x + p)(x + q), where p × q = c and p + q = b.","Recall: if a product is zero then a factor is zero — (x + p)(x + q) = 0 gives x = −p or x = −q, so there are two roots.","Recall: elimination — scale one or both equations so an unknown has coefficients of the same size, add when the signs are opposite and subtract when they are the same, then back-substitute.","Recall: substitution — rearrange one equation for one unknown and put that expression into the other equation.","Recall: the graphical readings — the solution of one equation is the x coordinate where the two graphs cross, two simultaneous linear equations are solved where the lines cross, and the roots of a quadratic are where its curve cuts the x axis.","Recall: an inequality is solved like an equation, but the sign reverses when both sides are multiplied or divided by a negative number.","Recall: the number line convention — closed (filled) circle for ≤ or ≥, open circle for < or >, with the arrow drawn towards the solution set.","The quadratic formula x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/2a is printed on the Higher Exam Aid (W87991A) only; it is not on the Foundation sheet, and it is not needed in this room, where quadratic equations are solved by factorising.","If a question in this room sets its equation inside a context that uses one of the printed results — the area of a circle, say, or the area of a trapezium — that formula is given to you on the sheet, but the equation you build from it and the method you solve it by are not."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T11:52:32Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"Answer keys, solutions and mark tallies are all sound and the Formulae sheet matches the Foundation Exam Aid (W87990A) exactly, so this is publishable as it stands; the only things worth a teacher's pen are the duplicated x² + 6x = 16 and the \"square root undoes a square\" card."}}},"algebra-sequences":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"algebra-sequences","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Sequences are set on all three papers at both tiers, and they are the cheapest algebra marks on the paper if you know what to look for. A question may hand you a rule and ask for terms, hand you terms and ask for the rule, or hide either inside a pattern of tiles, matchsticks or weekly savings. The same three moves cover them all: apply a term-to-term rule repeatedly, substitute a position into an nth-term expression, or read the common difference off a linear sequence and build dn + (a - d). Because an nth-term expression tests any value in one line, it also settles the reasoning questions examiners like — is 200 in this sequence, which term is the first to go negative, which numbers belong to both sequences — and those carry the AO2 and AO3 marks.","idea":[{"h":"Generating terms: term-to-term and position-to-term rules","t":"A term-to-term rule tells you how to get from one term to the next, so it is useless without a starting term and you must work through every term in order: 'first term 9, term-to-term rule subtract 5' gives 9, 4, −1, −6, −11. A position-to-term rule, usually written as 'the nth term is …', gives any term directly from its position: with nth term 9n − 4 the 8th term is 9 × 8 − 4 = 68 and the 20th is 9 × 20 − 4 = 176, with no need for the 19 terms in between. Exam questions name which rule you have been given, so read the wording before you start; the marks for generating terms are usually B marks, but a single arithmetic slip early in a term-to-term chain spoils every term after it, so write each step down rather than doing it in your head.","code":"A23"},{"h":"The sequences you are expected to recognise","t":"Five families come up by name. Triangular numbers 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, 21, 28, … go up by 1 more each time; square numbers 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, … are n × n; cube numbers 1, 8, 27, 64, 125, … are n × n × n. A Fibonacci-type sequence adds the two previous terms, so it needs two starting values and can be run backwards by subtracting. A geometric progression multiplies by a constant common ratio r, found by dividing any term by the one before it, and the sequence rⁿ is r, r², r³, … . A quadratic sequence is the one whose differences are not constant: work out the first differences, and if those go up or down by the same amount each time the second difference is constant and you continue by extending the differences first. Checking the differences is the move that tells you which family you are in.","code":"A24"},{"h":"Deducing the nth term of a linear sequence","t":"A linear (arithmetic) sequence has a constant difference d between consecutive terms, and that difference is the coefficient of n: the sequence behaves like the multiples of d, shifted. Compare 4, 10, 16, 22 with the multiples of 6 — 6, 12, 18, 24 — and every term is 2 less, so the nth term is 6n − 2. In general the nth term is dn + (a − d), where a is the first term, and a − d is the term that would sit at position zero. A decreasing sequence has a negative d: 30, 25, 20, 15 gives 35 − 5n. Always check your expression on a term you have not used. The expression is what answers the harder questions: substitute a position to get a term, or set the expression equal to a value and solve for n — a positive whole number means the value is in the sequence, anything else means it is not.","code":"A25"}],"examples":[{"title":"Generating terms from a two-step term-to-term rule","setup":"A sequence has first term 5. Its term-to-term rule is 'multiply by 2, then subtract 3'. Work out the 4th term of the sequence.","steps":["Apply the rule to the first term: 5 × 2 = 10, then 10 − 3 = 7, so the 2nd term is 7","The rule now acts on 7: 7 × 2 = 14, then 14 − 3 = 11, so the 3rd term is 11","The rule acts on 11: 11 × 2 = 22, then 22 − 3 = 19, so the 4th term is 19","Check by listing the terms in order: 5, 7, 11, 19 — the rule has been applied three times, so 19 does sit at position 4"],"cues":["Which order are the two operations done in?","Which number does the rule act on next?","How many more times must the rule be applied?","How can you be sure the rule has been applied the right number of times?"]},{"title":"Finding a later term of a geometric progression","setup":"A sequence begins 2, 6, 18, 54, … . Show that it is a geometric progression, and work out its 7th term.","steps":["Divide each term by the one before it: 6 ÷ 2 = 3, 18 ÷ 6 = 3, 54 ÷ 18 = 3, so the common ratio is 3 and the sequence is geometric","Multiply on to the 5th term: 54 × 3 = 162","The 6th term is 162 × 3 = 486","One more multiplication gives 486 × 3 = 1458, so the term asked for is 1458"],"cues":["What is the same about each pair of neighbouring terms?","What must be done to the last term given to move on one place?","Which position has been reached now?","How many more multiplications are needed?"]},{"title":"Continuing a quadratic sequence","setup":"Here are the first four terms of a quadratic sequence: 3, 8, 15, 24. Work out the next two terms of the sequence.","steps":["Work out the first differences: 8 − 3 = 5, 15 − 8 = 7, 24 − 15 = 9","The differences themselves go up by 2 each time, so the next two first differences are 11 and 13","Add the next difference to the last term: 24 + 11 = 35","Add the difference after that: 35 + 13 = 48, so the next two terms are 35 and 48"],"cues":["What should be worked out first when the gaps are not equal?","What pattern do those gaps themselves follow?","Which term does the next gap get added to?","What remains to be done to reach a second new term?"]},{"title":"Deducing the nth term of a linear sequence","setup":"Here are the first four terms of a linear sequence: 9, 14, 19, 24. Find an expression, in terms of n, for the nth term of this sequence.","steps":["The gap between terms is constant: 14 − 9 = 5, so the coefficient of n is 5","The multiples of 5 are 5, 10, 15, 20, while the sequence reads 9, 14, 19, 24","Each term of the sequence is 4 more than the matching multiple of 5","nth term = 5n + 4"],"cues":["What is the same about every gap between neighbouring terms?","What should the sequence be compared with once the gap is known?","How does each term compare with the multiple above it?","How is that comparison written as an expression in n?"]},{"title":"Testing whether a number is a term of a sequence","setup":"The nth term of a sequence is 6n − 5. Alfie says that 121 is a term of this sequence. Decide whether Alfie is right, showing your working.","steps":["A number is a term only if some whole-number position gives it, so set 6n − 5 = 121","Add 5 to both sides: 6n = 126","Divide both sides by 6: n = 21","n = 21 is a positive whole number, so 121 is the 21st term and Alfie is right"],"cues":["What has to be true about the position for a number to be in the sequence?","What is the first move towards getting n on its own?","What undoes the multiplication by 6?","What does a whole-number position tell you about Alfie's claim?"]},{"title":"The nth term from two terms that are not next to each other","setup":"In a linear sequence the 3rd term is 20 and the 7th term is 44. Find an expression, in terms of n, for the nth term of the sequence.","steps":["From the 3rd to the 7th term is 4 steps, and the terms rise by 44 − 20 = 24 altogether","So each step adds 24 ÷ 4 = 6, and the coefficient of n is 6","Step back from the 3rd term to the 1st term: 20 − 6 − 6 = 8, so the first term is a = 8","The constant is the first term minus the common difference, so nth term = 6n + (8 − 6), that is 6n + 2"],"cues":["How many steps separate the two terms given?","What does the total rise divided by the number of steps give?","Which term of the sequence is still missing before an expression can be written?","How are the first term and the common difference combined into the nth term?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A sequence has first term 5 and term-to-term rule 'add 6'. Write down the first four terms.","a":"5, 11, 17, 23"},{"q":"Write down the first five square numbers and the first five triangular numbers.","a":"Square: 1, 4, 9, 16, 25. Triangular: 1, 3, 6, 10, 15"},{"q":"Find an expression for the nth term of the linear sequence 7, 10, 13, 16, …","a":"3n + 4"},{"q":"Is 84 a term of the sequence whose nth term is 5n + 4?","a":"Yes: 5n + 4 = 84 gives n = 16, so 84 is the 16th term"},{"q":"The first three terms of a geometric progression are 4, 12, 36. Write down the common ratio and work out the 5th term.","a":"Common ratio 3; 5th term 324"}]},"room":{"facts":["A term-to-term rule needs the previous term and is applied over and over from a given first term; a position-to-term (nth term) rule gives any term straight from its position n.","For a linear sequence the nth term is dn + (a − d), where d is the common difference and a is the first term: the difference is the coefficient of n and the constant is the term that would sit at position zero.","Triangular numbers: 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, 21, 28, 36, 45, 55. Square numbers: 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81, 100. Cube numbers: 1, 8, 27, 64, 125, 216, 343, 512, 729, 1000.","In a Fibonacci-type sequence each term is the sum of the two terms before it, so two starting terms are needed; running it backwards means subtracting.","In a geometric progression each term is the previous term multiplied by the common ratio r, and r = any term ÷ the term before it; the sequence rⁿ reads r, r², r³, r⁴, …","A linear sequence has a constant first difference; a quadratic sequence has a constant second difference, so its first differences go up or down by the same amount each time.","To test whether a value V is in a sequence, solve nth term = V: a positive whole-number n means V is the nth term, and any other value of n means V is not in the sequence."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) A sequence has first term 7 and term-to-term rule 'subtract 4'. Write down the first five terms of the sequence.","a":"7, 3, −1, −5, −9","sol":"The rule is applied to each term in turn, starting from 7: 7 − 4 = 3, 3 − 4 = −1, −1 − 4 = −5, −5 − 4 = −9 (M1 for repeatedly subtracting 4, shown by at least three correct terms). The first five terms are 7, 3, −1, −5, −9 (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A term-to-term rule only ever acts on the term you have just written.","Apply the rule again and again, using each new term as the starting point for the next.","Take 4 away from the first term, then take 4 away from whatever that gives, and keep going until five terms are written."],"codes":["A23"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The nth term of a sequence is 5n − 2. Work out the 8th term and the 20th term of this sequence.","a":"8th term 38; 20th term 98","sol":"A position-to-term rule gives a term directly, so substitute the position for n. For the 8th term, 5 × 8 − 2 = 40 − 2 = 38 (B1). For the 20th term, 5 × 20 − 2 = 100 − 2 = 98 (B1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The letter n stands for the position of the term in the sequence.","There is no need to write out the earlier terms — an nth term rule reaches any position in one step.","Replace n by 8 in 5n − 2, then replace n by 20 in the same expression."],"codes":["A23"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Here are the first five triangular numbers: 1, 3, 6, 10, 15. Write down the next two triangular numbers.","a":"21 and 28","sol":"The gaps between triangular numbers are 2, 3, 4, 5, going up by 1 each time, so the next gaps are 6 and 7 (M1 for using gaps that increase by 1, or for adding the next counting number). 15 + 6 = 21 and 21 + 7 = 28 (A1 for both). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Triangular numbers are built by adding one more dot in each new row.","Work out the gap between each pair of terms and look at how the gaps themselves change.","The gaps so far are 2, 3, 4 and 5, so work out what has to be added to 15 next."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Find an expression, in terms of n, for the nth term of the linear sequence 6, 13, 20, 27, …","a":"7n − 1","sol":"The common difference is 13 − 6 = 7, so the coefficient of n is 7 and the sequence is compared with the multiples of 7: 7, 14, 21, 28 (M1 for a common difference of 7 or for 7n seen). Each term of the sequence is 1 less than the matching multiple of 7, so the nth term is 7n − 1 (A1). Check with n = 3: 7 × 3 − 1 = 20. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["In a sequence that goes up by the same amount each time, that amount is built into the rule.","Compare the sequence term by term with the multiples of the common difference.","Work out 13 − 6 to get the common difference, then write the multiples of that number under the sequence and compare them term by term."],"codes":["A25"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Write down the first four cube numbers.","a":"1, 8, 27, 64","sol":"A cube number is a whole number multiplied by itself and then by itself again: 1 × 1 × 1 = 1, 2 × 2 × 2 = 8, 3 × 3 × 3 = 27, 4 × 4 × 4 = 64, giving 1, 8, 27, 64 (B1 for all four in order). Total 1 mark.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["A cube number counts the small cubes in a solid cube.","Take each of the counting numbers 1, 2, 3, 4 in turn and use it three times as a factor.","Work out 1 × 1 × 1, then 2 × 2 × 2, and carry on."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"The sequence 2, 5, 12.5, 31.25, … is a geometric progression. Write down the common ratio and work out the next term. A calculator may be used.","a":"Common ratio 2.5; next term 78.125","sol":"The common ratio is any term divided by the term before it: 5 ÷ 2 = 2.5, and 12.5 ÷ 5 = 2.5 confirms it (B1). The next term is the last one multiplied by the ratio: 31.25 × 2.5 = 78.125 (B1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["In a geometric progression the same thing is done to every term to reach the next one.","Dividing a term by the term before it gives the number that is being multiplied by each time.","Work out 5 ÷ 2, check it against 12.5 ÷ 5, then use that ratio on the last term given."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The nth term of a sequence is 4n + 3. Explain why 200 is not a term of this sequence.","a":"4n + 3 = 200 gives n = 49.25, which is not a whole number, so 200 is not a term","sol":"If 200 were a term there would be a whole-number position n with 4n + 3 = 200, so 4n = 200 − 3 = 197 (M1 for forming the equation or for 200 − 3 followed by ÷ 4). Then n = 197 ÷ 4 = 49.25, which is not a whole number, so no position gives 200 and 200 is not a term of the sequence (A1 for 49.25 with the conclusion). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Every term of the sequence sits at a whole-number position.","Assume 200 is a term and see what position it would have to have.","Set the nth term expression equal to 200 and take 3 from both sides."],"codes":["A25"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Here are the first four terms of a quadratic sequence: 2, 9, 20, 35. Work out the next two terms.","a":"54 and 77","sol":"The first differences are 9 − 2 = 7, 20 − 9 = 11 and 35 − 20 = 15, which go up by 4 each time, so the second difference is 4 and the next first differences are 19 and 23 (M1 for the first differences with a constant second difference of 4). Then 35 + 19 = 54 (A1) and 54 + 23 = 77 (A1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The gaps between these terms are not equal, so look at how the gaps change.","Work out the first differences, then the differences of those differences.","Write 7, 11, 15 under the sequence and continue that row before returning to the terms."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"The nth term of a sequence is 60 − 7n. Work out the first term of the sequence that is negative, and write down its position. A calculator may be used.","a":"−3, which is the 9th term","sol":"Substitute increasing positions: n = 8 gives 60 − 56 = 4, which is still positive, and n = 9 gives 60 − 63 = −3 (M1 for testing successive positions, or for solving 60 − 7n < 0 to get n > 8.57…). So the first negative term is at position 9 (A1) and its value is −3 (A1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Each step up in position takes 7 away from the term, so the terms fall steadily.","Find the position at which 7n first overtakes 60, then work out the term there.","Substitute n = 8 into 60 − 7n and see whether the answer has passed zero yet."],"codes":["A23","A25"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) In a Fibonacci-type sequence each term is the sum of the two terms before it. The 3rd term is 11 and the 4th term is 18. Work out the 1st term.","a":"4","sol":"The 4th term is the 2nd term plus the 3rd term, so the 2nd term is 18 − 11 (M1 for subtracting to run the sequence backwards). The 2nd term is 7 (A1). The 3rd term is the 1st term plus the 2nd term, so the 1st term is 11 − 7 = 4 (A1). Check: 4, 7, 11, 18. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The rule that builds the sequence forwards can be undone to work backwards.","Adding gives a later term, so the opposite operation recovers an earlier one.","The 2nd term and the 3rd term add to give the 4th term, so work out 18 − 11 first."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Find an expression, in terms of n, for the nth term of the sequence 27, 23, 19, 15, … and use it to work out the 12th term.","a":"31 − 4n; 12th term −17","sol":"The sequence falls by 4 each time, so the common difference is −4 and the coefficient of n is −4 (M1). The term at position zero is 27 + 4 = 31, so the nth term is 31 − 4n (A1). Substituting n = 12 gives 31 − 4 × 12 = 31 − 48 = −17 (B1, follow through on a correct expression). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The sequence is decreasing, so think about what that does to the number in front of n.","The common difference is the coefficient of n, and the constant is the term one step before the first.","Work out 23 − 27 to get the common difference, then compare the sequence term by term with the multiples of that number."],"codes":["A25"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Patterns are made from matchsticks. Pattern 1 uses 4 matchsticks, pattern 2 uses 7 matchsticks and pattern 3 uses 10 matchsticks. Priya has 150 matchsticks. Find an expression for the number of matchsticks in pattern n, then work out the largest pattern Priya can make and how many matchsticks she has left over.","a":"3n + 1; pattern 49, with 2 matchsticks left over","sol":"Each new pattern uses 3 more matchsticks than the one before, so the common difference is 3 (M1) and, since pattern 1 uses 4, the expression is 3n + 1 (A1). Priya needs 3n + 1 to be at most 150, so 3n ≤ 149 and n ≤ 49.66… , or by trial 3 × 49 + 1 = 148 while 3 × 50 + 1 = 151 is too many (M1). The largest pattern is pattern 49, which uses 148 matchsticks, leaving 150 − 148 = 2 matchsticks (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The numbers of matchsticks form a linear sequence, so it has an nth term rule.","Find the rule first, then ask which position it can reach without going past 150.","Work out 7 − 4 to get the step between one pattern and the next, then compare the multiples of that step with 4, 7, 10."],"codes":["A25","A23"]},{"q":"The first three terms of a geometric progression are 5, 15, 45. Work out the first term of this progression that is greater than 10 000, and say which position it has. A calculator may be used.","a":"10 935, the 8th term","sol":"The common ratio is 15 ÷ 5 = 3 (M1). Multiplying on by 3 each time gives 135, 405, 1215, 3645, 10935 as the 4th to 8th terms (M1 for continuing until 10 000 is passed). The 7th term, 3645, is still below 10 000, so the first term greater than 10 000 is 10 935, the 8th term (A1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Each term of a geometric progression is a fixed multiple of the one before it.","Find that multiplier, then keep multiplying and count the positions as you go.","Work out 15 ÷ 5 and check it with 45 ÷ 15, then multiply 45 by that ratio."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Sequence A has nth term 2n + 1. Sequence B is the sequence of triangular numbers. Write down all the numbers less than 30 that appear in both sequence A and sequence B.","a":"3, 15 and 21","sol":"The triangular numbers below 30 are 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, 21, 28 (M1). Sequence A is 3, 5, 7, 9, …, the odd numbers from 3 upwards, so a triangular number is in A only if it is odd and at least 3 (M1); this rules out 6, 10 and 28 for being even, and 1 because 2n + 1 = 1 would need position n = 0. The numbers in both sequences are 3, 15 and 21 (A1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Two sequences share a number when it can be found in each of them separately.","List one sequence up to the limit given, then test each of its numbers against the other rule.","Write out the triangular numbers as far as 30 before you look at the rule for sequence A at all."],"codes":["A24","A23"]},{"q":"Sequence P has nth term 4n + 1 and sequence Q has nth term 46 − 5n. There is one position at which the two sequences have the same term. Work out that position and the value of the term. A calculator may be used.","a":"Position 5; the term is 21","sol":"The terms are equal when 4n + 1 = 46 − 5n (M1). Adding 5n to both sides and subtracting 1 gives 9n = 45 (M1). So n = 5 (A1). Substituting back, P gives 4 × 5 + 1 = 21 and Q gives 46 − 25 = 21, so the common term is 21 (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["'The same term' means the two expressions have the same value at that position.","Set the two nth term expressions equal to each other and solve for n.","Write 4n + 1 = 46 − 5n and collect the n terms on one side."],"codes":["A25"]},{"q":"Ella runs 2.5 km in week 1 of a training plan, and each week after that she runs 0.4 km further than in the week before. Find an expression, in terms of n, for the distance she runs in week n, and work out the first week in which she runs more than 10 km. A calculator may be used.","a":"0.4n + 2.1 km; week 20","sol":"The distances form a linear sequence with common difference 0.4 and first term 2.5, so the week-n distance is 2.5 + 0.4(n − 1) (M1), which simplifies to 0.4n + 2.1 km (A1). Setting 0.4n + 2.1 greater than 10 gives 0.4n greater than 7.9 and n greater than 19.75, or by trial week 19 gives 0.4 × 19 + 2.1 = 9.7 km, which is not enough (M1). The first week over 10 km is week 20, when she runs 0.4 × 20 + 2.1 = 10.1 km (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The weekly distances go up by the same amount each week, so they form a linear sequence.","Build the nth term from the common difference, then find the first position where it passes 10.","Count how many weekly increases have happened by week n, then add that many of them to the 2.5 km of week 1."],"codes":["A25","A23"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write down the next two terms of the sequence 5, 9, 13, 17, …","a":"21 and 25"},{"q":"The nth term of a sequence is 4n − 1. Which term of the sequence is equal to 59?","a":"The 15th term"},{"q":"Write down the next two terms of the Fibonacci-type sequence 2, 5, 7, 12, …","a":"19 and 31"},{"q":"Find an expression, in terms of n, for the nth term of the sequence 11, 8, 5, 2, …","a":"14 − 3n"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Sequence","back":"An ordered list of numbers, called terms, that follows a rule. The position of a term is counted n = 1, 2, 3, … from the first term.","code":"A23","open":false},{"front":"Term-to-term rule","back":"A rule that produces the next term from the previous one, such as 'add 3' or 'multiply by 2 then subtract 1'. A first term must be given, and the rule is applied repeatedly.","code":"A23","open":false},{"front":"Position-to-term rule (the nth term)","back":"A rule that gives any term directly from its position n. With nth term 4n + 1 the 10th term is 4 × 10 + 1 = 41, with no need for the earlier terms.","code":"A23","open":false},{"front":"Linear (arithmetic) sequence","back":"A sequence with a constant difference between consecutive terms, so its nth term has the form dn + c and its terms lie on a straight line.","code":"A25","open":false},{"front":"The nth term of a linear sequence","back":"nth term = dn + (a − d), where d is the common difference and a is the first term. The difference is the coefficient of n; the constant is the term at position zero.","code":"A25","open":false},{"front":"Common difference","back":"The fixed amount added to each term of a linear sequence to reach the next. It is negative when the sequence decreases, as in 40, 33, 26, … where d = −7.","code":"A25","open":false},{"front":"Testing whether a value is a term of a linear sequence","back":"Set the nth term expression equal to the value and solve for n. A positive whole number means the value is the nth term; any other value of n means it is not in the sequence.","code":"A25","open":false},{"front":"Triangular numbers","back":"1, 3, 6, 10, 15, 21, 28, 36, 45, 55 — the dots that build a triangle. The gaps go up by 1 each time: +2, +3, +4, +5, …","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"Square numbers","back":"1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81, 100 — each is n × n, the number of dots in a square array.","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"Cube numbers","back":"1, 8, 27, 64, 125, 216, 343, 512, 729, 1000 — each is n × n × n, the number of unit cubes in a cube.","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"Fibonacci-type sequence","back":"A sequence in which each term is the sum of the two terms before it, so two starting terms are needed: 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21, … Subtracting runs it backwards.","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"Geometric progression","back":"A sequence in which each term is the previous term multiplied by a constant common ratio r. The sequence rⁿ reads r, r², r³, r⁴, … for n = 1, 2, 3, 4, …","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"Common ratio of a geometric progression","back":"r = any term ÷ the term before it, and the value is the same for every such pair. For 2, 6, 18, 54 the common ratio is 6 ÷ 2 = 3.","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"Quadratic sequence","back":"A sequence whose first differences are not constant but change by the same amount each time, so the second difference is constant. In 4, 7, 12, 19 the differences 3, 5, 7 rise by 2.","code":"A24","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Nothing in this room is printed on either tier's Exam Aid formulae sheet (W87990A Foundation, W87991A Higher). Every rule below must be written from memory.","Recall: nth term of a linear sequence = dn + (a − d), where d is the common difference and a is the first term.","Recall: the triangular numbers 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, 21, 28, 36, 45, 55; the square numbers 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81, 100; the cube numbers 1, 8, 27, 64, 125, 216, 343, 512, 729, 1000.","Recall: in a Fibonacci-type sequence each term is the sum of the two terms before it; in a geometric progression each term is the previous term × r, and the sequence rⁿ reads r, r², r³, r⁴, …","Recall: common ratio r = any term ÷ the term before it; common difference d = any term − the term before it.","Recall: a linear sequence has a constant first difference and a quadratic sequence has a constant second difference — the test that tells you which method to use.","Recall: a value V is a term of a sequence only if solving nth term = V gives a positive whole number n, and that value of n is its position.","What the Foundation Exam Aid does print, none of it needed in this room: area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h; volume of a prism = area of cross section × length; circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd; area of a circle = πr²; a² + b² = c²; sin A = a/c, cos A = b/c, tan A = a/b; total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ; P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B).","The Higher Exam Aid prints all of those and, in addition, the quadratic formula x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/2a, the sine rule, the cosine rule, area of a triangle = ½ab sin C and P(A and B) = P(A given B) P(B). No sequence rule appears on either sheet."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T11:57:01Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.95,"notes":"Every answer key, solution line, mark split, card and formulae-sheet claim checks out — the Foundation and Higher Exam Aid lists match W87990A/W87991A exactly and the room correctly never asks for the nth term of a quadratic sequence (Higher-only A25) — so it is publishable as it stands, with only the difficulty label on the geometric-progression question worth a second look."}}},"ratio":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"ratio","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Ratio, proportion and rates of change is the largest single block of Foundation content — between 22% and 28% of the course at this tier — and it is where the exam meets real life: sharing money, mixing concrete, map scales, best buys, speed and density, percentage change, interest and depreciation. Almost every AO3 problem-solving question on a Foundation paper sits in this room or passes through it, because a ratio, a multiplier or a rate is nearly always the step that turns a wordy situation into a calculation. The techniques are short, so what goes wrong is units, which quantity divides which, and leaving out the intermediate value the method mark is waiting for.","idea":[{"code":"R1","h":"Converting standard and compound units","t":"Changing units is multiplying or dividing by the conversion factor: 1 m = 100 cm, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 hour = 60 minutes. Area squares that factor (1 m² = 100² = 10 000 cm²) and volume cubes it (1 m³ = 100³ = 1 000 000 cm³), which is the single most common slip in this room. A compound unit is converted one part at a time: to turn 25 m/s into km/h, multiply by 3600 for the seconds and divide by 1000 for the metres. Examiners give the method mark for the conversion line, so write it down rather than doing it in your head."},{"code":"R2","h":"Scale factors, scale diagrams and maps","t":"A scale written as 1 : n means one unit on the drawing stands for n of the same units in real life, so a measured length × n gives the real length and a real length ÷ n gives the drawing length. A scale written as a statement — 1 cm to 2.5 m — is used the same way. Work in a single unit first (change metres to centimetres, or centimetres to metres) and only convert to the unit asked for at the end. Questions ask for a real distance in kilometres from a map, or for the length to draw on a plan, and the unit conversion carries its own mark."},{"code":"R3","h":"One quantity as a fraction of another","t":"To write one quantity as a fraction of another, put the first over the second with both in the same unit, then cancel: 40 cm as a fraction of 3 m is 40/300 = 2/15. The fraction is allowed to be greater than 1 — 7 as a fraction of 4 is 7/4 — and an improper fraction is a perfectly good answer unless the question asks for a mixed number. The examined slip is comparing unlike units, so convert before you divide; the conversion line is the method mark and the cancelled fraction is the accuracy mark."},{"code":"R4","h":"Ratio notation and simplest form","t":"A ratio compares parts using a colon, with both parts in the same unit and no units written in the ratio itself. Simplest form means dividing every part by their highest common factor so the parts are whole numbers with no common factor: 20 : 35 = 4 : 7. Where a question asks for the form 1 : n, divide both parts by the first part, and n may then be a decimal. A ratio containing a decimal or a fraction is cleared by multiplying every part by the same number, so 0.75 : 1 becomes 3 : 4."},{"code":"R5","h":"Dividing a quantity in a given ratio","t":"Sharing in the ratio a : b means cutting the quantity into a + b equal parts. Find one part by dividing the whole by the total number of parts, then multiply up for each share; the division line is where the method mark falls, so write the value of one part down. A part:whole ratio works the same way once you read off how many parts each side holds. Adding your two shares back to the original total is a free check, and mixing, concentration and scaling problems are the same calculation dressed in context."},{"code":"R6","h":"Multiplicative relationships as ratios or fractions","t":"'One quantity is so many times another' is a ratio in disguise: if A is 1.5 times B then A : B = 1.5 : 1 = 3 : 2, and as a fraction A/B = 3/2. Either form can be fed into the next step of a calculation, and the direction matters — A : B is not B : A. Multiplying both parts by the same number clears a decimal or a fraction from the ratio. Questions state the relationship in words and ask for it as a ratio in its simplest form, or give the ratio and ask how many times bigger one quantity is."},{"code":"R7","h":"Proportion as equality of ratios","t":"Two pairs of quantities are in proportion when their ratios are equal, so 3 : 5 and 12 : 20 are in proportion because both simplify to 3 : 5. That equality is the tool for a missing value: scale one ratio up or down until one part matches, and the other part follows — in 3 : 5 = x : 35 the second parts scale by 7, so x scales by 7 too. In problems the equality is often hidden in a sentence about parts of a group, and the step examiners look for is the scaling factor written down."},{"code":"R8","h":"Ratios, fractions and linear functions","t":"A ratio, a fraction and a straight-line graph through the origin all say the same thing. If x : y = a : b then y/x = b/a and y = (b/a)x, whose graph is a straight line through the origin with gradient b/a. Reading the gradient off such a graph gives the ratio, and a table of values that is in a fixed ratio must plot as a straight line through the origin. This link is what lets a proportion question be answered from a graph, from a table or from algebra, and the exam moves between the three."},{"code":"R9","h":"Percentages and percentage change","t":"Per cent means 'parts per hundred', so p% is the fraction p/100 and the decimal p ÷ 100. One quantity as a percentage of another is first ÷ second × 100. Change is handled multiplicatively: a p% increase multiplies by 1 + p/100 and a p% decrease by 1 − p/100, percentages over 100% are allowed, and an original-value question is undone by dividing by that multiplier rather than by adding the percentage back. Simple interest pays the same amount each year, worked out on the starting amount only, so it is principal × rate ÷ 100 × years."},{"code":"R10","h":"Direct and inverse proportion","t":"In direct proportion two quantities scale together, so the ratio between them is fixed: find the value of one unit — one book, one pen, one litre — then multiply, and the unitary line is the method mark. In inverse proportion their product is fixed, so one rises as the other falls: six pumps taking 14 hours is 84 pump-hours of work however many pumps are used. The trap is assuming every relationship is direct, so ask whether doubling one quantity should double the other or halve it before you calculate."},{"code":"R11","h":"Compound units","t":"A compound unit is one quantity per unit of another: speed = distance ÷ time, density = mass ÷ volume, pressure = force ÷ area, pay = rate × hours, unit price = cost ÷ amount. None of these formulae is printed on the Exam Aid, so all must be remembered, together with the two rearrangements each one gives. The unit written on the answer states which division was done — g/cm³ is grams divided by cubic centimetres — so check the given quantities are in those units before dividing, and quote the unit with the answer or lose the accuracy mark."},{"code":"R12","h":"Comparing lengths, areas and volumes by ratio","t":"Corresponding lengths of similar figures are in a fixed ratio, and that ratio is exactly what a scale factor records. Because area is two lengths multiplied and volume is three, a length ratio a : b gives an area ratio a² : b² and a volume ratio a³ : b³: lengths 2 : 5 give volumes 8 : 125. The same fixed ratio of corresponding sides is what a trigonometric ratio measures in similar right-angled triangles. Questions ask for the area or volume ratio from a length ratio, or work back from an area ratio to the lengths."},{"code":"R13","h":"Equations of direct and inverse proportion","t":"Direct proportion is written y = kx and inverse proportion y = k/x, and saying X is inversely proportional to Y is the same as saying X is proportional to 1/Y. The number k is the constant of proportionality: substitute the one pair of values the question gives to find it, write the completed equation, then use that equation for any further value asked for. Interpreting the equation is the Foundation skill — reading off that doubling x halves y when y = k/x — and the substitution line earns the first method mark."},{"code":"R14","h":"Gradient as a rate of change","t":"The gradient of a straight-line graph is the change in the vertical quantity for each unit of the horizontal one, so it is a rate with units of y per unit of x: pounds per day on a cost graph, metres per second on a distance–time graph. A graph of direct proportion is a straight line through the origin, and its gradient is the rate; a graph of inverse proportion is a curve falling away from both axes and never touching them. Interpreting means writing the value with its units and saying what it means in the situation."},{"code":"R16","h":"Growth and decay problems","t":"Repeated percentage change is repeated multiplication: apply the multiplier once for each period, so n periods give the multiplier raised to the power n. £2000 growing at 3% a year for 5 years is 2000 × 1.03⁵, and a van losing 12% a year for two years is multiplied by 0.88². Compound interest is the growth case and depreciation the decay case, and where the rate changes the multipliers are simply multiplied together in order. Read whether the answer wanted is the final amount or the total change, and subtract if it is the change."}],"examples":[{"title":"Sharing a quantity in a given ratio","setup":"A box of 84 chocolates is shared between Ravi and Sam in the ratio 3 : 4. Work out how many chocolates Ravi gets and how many Sam gets.","steps":["The ratio 3 : 4 cuts the whole box into 3 + 4 = 7 equal parts.","One part is worth 84 ÷ 7 = 12 chocolates.","Ravi has 3 parts, so Ravi gets 3 × 12 = 36 chocolates.","Sam has 4 parts, so Sam gets 4 × 12 = 48 chocolates: Ravi gets 36 chocolates and Sam gets 48 chocolates, and 36 + 48 = 84 confirms the share."],"cues":["How many equal parts is the box cut into?","What is one part worth in chocolates?","How many of those parts belong to Ravi?","How many parts are left for Sam, and how can the total be checked?"]},{"title":"Finding the price before a reduction","setup":"In a sale, the price of a coat is reduced by 15%. The sale price of the coat is £102. Work out the price of the coat before the sale.","steps":["A reduction of 15% leaves 100% − 15% = 85% of the original price.","As a multiplier that is 0.85, so original price × 0.85 = 102.","Dividing undoes the multiplication: original price = 102 ÷ 0.85.","The coat cost £120 before the sale, and 120 × 0.85 = 102 checks it."],"cues":["What percentage of the original price does £102 represent?","What single number multiplies the old price to give the new one?","Which operation undoes multiplying by that number?","How can the answer be checked against the sale price?"]},{"title":"Compound interest over several years","setup":"Kemi invests £4500 in a savings account paying 2.5% compound interest per year. She leaves the money in the account for 6 years and takes nothing out. Work out the value of her investment at the end of the 6 years, giving your answer to the nearest penny.","steps":["Each year the amount in the account is multiplied by 1 + 2.5/100 = 1.025.","Six years means six multiplications, so value = 4500 × 1.025⁶ — the form the Exam Aid prints as Total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ.","1.025⁶ = 1.159693… so the value is 4500 × 1.159693… = 5218.6203…","To the nearest penny the investment is worth £5218.62."],"cues":["By what single number does one year's interest multiply the amount?","How many times is that multiplier applied?","What does that power come to on a calculator?","How should a money answer like this be rounded?"]},{"title":"Density from mass and volume","setup":"A solid block of copper has a mass of 1.79 kg and a volume of 200 cm³. Work out the density of the copper in g/cm³.","steps":["The answer is wanted in grams per cubic centimetre, so change the mass: 1.79 kg = 1.79 × 1000 = 1790 g.","Density = mass ÷ volume, which here is 1790 ÷ 200.","1790 ÷ 200 = 8.95, so the density is 8.95 g/cm³."],"cues":["Which unit must the mass be in before you divide?","Which quantity is divided by which?","What unit must be written on the answer?"]},{"title":"Inverse proportion with a fixed amount of work","setup":"Six identical pumps, all working at the same rate, empty a tank in 14 hours. Work out how long 4 of these pumps would take to empty the same tank.","steps":["Emptying the tank is a fixed amount of work: 6 pumps × 14 hours = 84 pump-hours.","One pump on its own would therefore need 84 hours — fewer pumps means more time, so this is inverse proportion.","Four pumps share that work: 84 ÷ 4 = 21 hours."],"cues":["What stays the same however many pumps are used?","How long would a single pump need?","How is the one-pump time shared between four pumps?"]},{"title":"A real distance from a map scale","setup":"A map is drawn to a scale of 1 : 50 000. On the map, two villages are 7.4 cm apart. Work out the real distance between the villages in kilometres.","steps":["Each 1 cm measured stands for 50 000 cm on the ground, so multiply the measurement by 50 000.","Real distance = 7.4 × 50 000 = 370 000 cm.","370 000 cm ÷ 100 = 3700 m.","3700 m ÷ 1000 = 3.7 km."],"cues":["What does one centimetre on this map stand for?","What is the real distance in centimetres?","How many centimetres make one metre?","How many metres make one kilometre?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Convert 3 m² into cm².","a":"30 000 cm² (the linear factor 100 is squared)"},{"q":"£45 is shared in the ratio 4 : 5. Work out the larger share.","a":"£25"},{"q":"X is inversely proportional to Y, and X = 12 when Y = 3. Work out X when Y = 9.","a":"X = 4 (since X = 36/Y)"},{"q":"A car travels 45 km in 30 minutes. Work out its average speed in km/h.","a":"90 km/h"}]},"room":{"facts":["One part of a share = quantity ÷ (total number of parts): to share in the ratio a : b, divide by a + b, then multiply each share up.","Simplest form: divide every part of the ratio by their highest common factor, with both parts in the same unit and no units written in the ratio.","Speed = distance ÷ time, density = mass ÷ volume, pressure = force ÷ area — none of these is on the Exam Aid, and each gives two rearrangements.","A p% increase multiplies by 1 + p/100 and a p% decrease by 1 − p/100; to find the original value, divide the new value by that multiplier.","Simple interest = principal × rate ÷ 100 × number of years; compound interest applies the multiplier once per year instead.","Direct proportion: y = kx, a straight line through the origin with gradient k. Inverse proportion: y = k/x, so xy is constant.","Similar shapes with lengths in the ratio a : b have areas in the ratio a² : b² and volumes in the ratio a³ : b³.","Unit conversions: 1 m = 100 cm, 1 m² = 10 000 cm², 1 m³ = 1 000 000 cm³, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 hour = 3600 s, and 1 m/s = 3.6 km/h.","A scale of 1 : n means map length × n = real length in the same unit, and real length ÷ n = map length."],"questions":[{"q":"Write the ratio 18 : 30 in its simplest form. (non-calculator)","a":"3 : 5","m":1,"d":1,"codes":["R4"],"sol":"The highest common factor of 18 and 30 is 6, and both parts are divided by it: 18 ÷ 6 = 3 and 30 ÷ 6 = 5, so 18 : 30 = 3 : 5. B1 for 3 : 5 (3 : 5 only; 9 : 15 is not simplest form).","hints":["Simplest form means the two parts share no factor above 1.","Divide both parts of the ratio by their highest common factor.","The first line divides each part by the same whole number."]},{"q":"Write 0.8 m : 50 cm as a ratio of two integers in its simplest form. (non-calculator)","a":"8 : 5","m":2,"d":1,"codes":["R4","R1"],"sol":"Both parts must be in the same unit first: 0.8 m = 0.8 × 100 = 80 cm, so the ratio is 80 : 50. M1 for 80 : 50 (or for 0.8 : 0.5 with both in metres). Both parts divide by 10: 80 ÷ 10 = 8 and 50 ÷ 10 = 5. A1 for 8 : 5.","hints":["A ratio compares two quantities measured in the same unit.","Convert one quantity so that both are in centimetres, then cancel.","The first line rewrites the metres as centimetres before any cancelling."]},{"q":"Express 45 minutes as a fraction of 2 hours. Give your answer in its lowest terms. (non-calculator)","a":"3/8","m":2,"d":1,"codes":["R3","R1"],"sol":"Put both times in the same unit: 2 hours = 2 × 60 = 120 minutes, so the fraction is 45/120. M1 for 45/120. Both parts divide by 15: 45 ÷ 15 = 3 and 120 ÷ 15 = 8. A1 for 3/8.","hints":["The two quantities must be measured in the same unit before one can be written over the other.","Change the hours into minutes, put the first quantity over the second, then cancel.","The first line converts the 2 hours into minutes."]},{"q":"Priya is paid £11.40 for each hour she works. Work out her pay for 7.5 hours of work. (a calculator may be used)","a":"£85.50","m":2,"d":1,"codes":["R11"],"sol":"A rate of pay is money per hour, so pay = rate × number of hours = 11.40 × 7.5. M1 for 11.40 × 7.5. This gives 85.5, written as money to the penny. A1 for £85.50.","hints":["A rate of pay tells you what one hour of work is worth.","Multiply the hourly rate by the number of hours worked.","The first line multiplies the rate per hour by the hours, and the answer is then written as money."]},{"q":"P is 2.5 times Q. Write the ratio P : Q in its simplest form, using integers. (non-calculator)","a":"5 : 2","m":2,"d":1,"codes":["R6","R4"],"sol":"'P is 2.5 times Q' means that for every 1 of Q there is 2.5 of P, so P : Q = 2.5 : 1. M1 for 2.5 : 1 (or for P/Q = 2.5). Multiplying both parts by 2 clears the decimal: 2.5 × 2 = 5 and 1 × 2 = 2. A1 for 5 : 2.","hints":["'So many times as big' can be written straight down as a comparison of two quantities.","Write the relationship against a single unit of Q, then clear the decimal from the ratio.","The first line writes the ratio with 1 as its second part."]},{"q":"Mortar is made by mixing cement and sand in the ratio 2 : 7 by mass. A builder makes 810 kg of mortar. Work out the mass of sand he uses. (a calculator may be used)","a":"630 kg","m":3,"d":2,"codes":["R5"],"sol":"The ratio 2 : 7 cuts the mortar into 2 + 7 = 9 equal parts. M1 for 9 parts seen. One part is 810 ÷ 9 = 90 kg. M1 for 810 ÷ 9 (= 90). Sand is 7 of those parts: 7 × 90 = 630. A1 for 630 kg, with the unit. (Check: cement is 2 × 90 = 180 kg and 180 + 630 = 810 kg.)","hints":["The two numbers in the ratio count equal parts of the whole mix.","Find how much one part weighs, then take as many parts as the sand needs.","The first line adds the parts of the ratio to find how many there are altogether."]},{"q":"Nadia invests £950 in an account that pays 2.4% simple interest per year. She leaves the money in the account for 5 years. Work out the total interest she earns. (a calculator may be used)","a":"£114","m":3,"d":2,"codes":["R9"],"sol":"Simple interest is worked out on the £950 only, so the same interest is paid each year. One year's interest is 950 × 2.4 ÷ 100 = £22.80. M1 for 950 × 0.024 (or 22.80 seen). Over 5 years the interest is 22.80 × 5. M1 for × 5. A1 for £114 (accept £114.00).","hints":["Simple interest is worked out on the starting amount every year, never on the interest already earned.","Find one year's interest, then account for all the years.","The first line finds the interest for a single year from the amount invested and the rate."]},{"q":"7 identical books have a total mass of 1218 g. Work out the total mass of 12 of these books. (a calculator may be used)","a":"2088 g","m":3,"d":2,"codes":["R10"],"sol":"The books are identical, so mass is directly proportional to the number of books. One book has mass 1218 ÷ 7 = 174 g. M1 for 1218 ÷ 7 (= 174). Twelve books have mass 174 × 12. M1 for × 12. A1 for 2088 g (accept 2.088 kg).","hints":["Identical books means the mass grows in step with the number of books.","Work out what one book weighs before scaling up.","The first line divides the total mass by the number of books it belongs to."]},{"q":"A car is travelling at a steady speed of 18 m/s. Change this speed into kilometres per hour. (non-calculator)","a":"64.8 km/h","m":3,"d":2,"codes":["R1","R11"],"sol":"There are 60 × 60 = 3600 seconds in an hour, so in one hour the car covers 18 × 3600 = 64 800 m. M1 for × 3600 (or for × 60 twice). There are 1000 m in a kilometre, so 64 800 m = 64 800 ÷ 1000 = 64.8 km. M1 for ÷ 1000. A1 for 64.8 km/h, with the unit.","hints":["A compound unit is changed one part at a time — the distance part and the time part.","Find how far the car goes in one hour, then rewrite that distance in kilometres.","The first line converts the distance travelled each second into the distance travelled each hour."]},{"q":"Two similar cylinders have heights in the ratio 3 : 4. Work out the ratio of their volumes, in its simplest form. (a calculator may be used)","a":"27 : 64","m":2,"d":2,"codes":["R12"],"sol":"Corresponding lengths of similar solids are in the ratio 3 : 4, and volume is three lengths multiplied together, so the volume ratio is the cube of the length ratio. M1 for cubing both parts (3³ : 4³ seen, or a scale factor of 4/3 cubed). 3³ = 27 and 4³ = 64, and 27 and 64 have no common factor. A1 for 27 : 64.","hints":["Volume is built from three lengths multiplied together.","Do to each part of the length ratio what volume does to a length.","The first line raises each part of the length ratio to the power that volume needs."]},{"q":"The cost £C of hiring a floor sander for d days is shown by a straight-line graph through the origin. The line passes through the point (6, 27). Interpret the gradient of the line by working out the cost of hiring the sander for one day. (a calculator may be used)","a":"£4.50 per day","m":2,"d":2,"codes":["R14","R8"],"sol":"The line passes through the origin, so C is directly proportional to d and C = kd, where the gradient k is the cost of one day. From the origin (0, 0) to the given point (6, 27) the change in C is 27 and the change in d is 6, so the gradient = 27 ÷ 6. M1 for 27 ÷ 6 (or for 27 = 6k). This gives 4.5, and the units of the gradient are pounds per day. A1 for £4.50 per day (the unit must be seen or implied by the interpretation).","hints":["On a straight-line graph through the origin, steepness measures how much one quantity changes for each unit of the other.","Divide the change in cost by the change in the number of days between the origin and the given point.","The first line is the change in C divided by the change in d for that point."]},{"q":"A garden path is 45 m long. It is drawn on a plan with a scale of 1 : 500. Work out the length of the path on the plan, in centimetres. (a calculator may be used)","a":"9 cm","m":3,"d":2,"codes":["R2","R1"],"sol":"A scale of 1 : 500 compares lengths in the same unit, and the answer is wanted in centimetres, so work in centimetres: 45 m = 45 × 100 = 4500 cm. M1 for 4500 cm. Going from real to plan divides by the scale: 4500 ÷ 500. M1 for ÷ 500. A1 for 9 cm.","hints":["A scale of 1 : 500 compares two lengths measured in the same unit.","Put the real length into the unit the answer is wanted in, then go from real length to plan length.","The first line rewrites the real length of the path in centimetres."]},{"q":"A van is bought for £18 000. Its value falls by 15% during the first year and by 12% during each of the next two years. Work out the value of the van at the end of the three years. (a calculator may be used)","a":"£11 848.32","m":3,"d":3,"codes":["R16","R9"],"sol":"A fall of 15% multiplies by 1 − 0.15 = 0.85 and a fall of 12% multiplies by 1 − 0.12 = 0.88. M1 for 0.85 and 0.88 both seen as multipliers. The 12% fall happens twice, so value = 18 000 × 0.85 × 0.88². M1 for the complete method. 18 000 × 0.85 = 15 300, 15 300 × 0.88 = 13 464 and 13 464 × 0.88 = 11 848.32. A1 for £11 848.32.","hints":["A percentage fall can be done in one multiplication instead of a subtraction.","Apply the multiplier for each year in turn, remembering that the later rate is used more than once.","The first line writes down the multiplier for each of the two different percentage falls."]},{"q":"Washing powder is sold in two sizes. A 1.5 kg box costs £4.20 and a 2.6 kg box costs £7.02. Work out which box is better value for money, showing the cost per kilogram of each box. (a calculator may be used)","a":"The 2.6 kg box (£2.70 per kg, against £2.80 per kg)","m":3,"d":3,"codes":["R10","R11"],"sol":"Compare the boxes by the cost of the same amount of powder — one kilogram. Small box: 4.20 ÷ 1.5 = 2.80. M1 for 4.20 ÷ 1.5 or for 7.02 ÷ 2.6. Large box: 7.02 ÷ 2.6 = 2.70. A1 for £2.80 per kg and £2.70 per kg. Since £2.70 is less than £2.80, each kilogram costs less from the larger box. B1 dep on the M1 and the A1, so both unit costs must be seen, for the conclusion naming the 2.6 kg box.","hints":["Two boxes can only be compared once they are measured against the same amount of powder.","Work out what one kilogram costs from each box, then compare the two costs.","The first line divides the cost of a box by the mass of powder in it."]},{"q":"In a table tennis club the ratio of boys to girls is 3 : 5. There are 24 more girls than boys. Work out the total number of members of the club. (non-calculator)","a":"96","m":3,"d":3,"codes":["R5","R7"],"sol":"Boys are 3 parts and girls are 5 parts, so the girls have 5 − 3 = 2 parts more than the boys, and those 2 parts are the 24 extra girls. M1 for 2 parts = 24. One part is 24 ÷ 2 = 12 members. M1 for 24 ÷ 2 (= 12). The club has 3 + 5 = 8 parts altogether, so the total is 8 × 12 = 96. A1 for 96. (Check: 36 boys and 60 girls differ by 24.)","hints":["The difference between the two groups is also measured in parts of the ratio.","Work out how many parts the 24 extra girls represent, then find the value of one part.","The first line subtracts the smaller part of the ratio from the larger one."]},{"q":"The pressure P atmospheres of a fixed mass of gas is inversely proportional to its volume V cm³, so P = k/V. At a volume of 250 cm³ the pressure is 1.8 atmospheres. The gas is squeezed until its pressure reaches 3 atmospheres. Work out the decrease in the volume of the gas. (a calculator may be used)","a":"100 cm³","m":4,"d":3,"codes":["R13","R10"],"sol":"Substituting the given pair into P = k/V gives 1.8 = k/250. M1 for 1.8 = k/250 (or k = 1.8 × 250). So k = 450 and the equation is P = 450/V. A1 for k = 450. At a pressure of 3 atmospheres the volume is V = 450 ÷ 3. M1 for 450 ÷ 3 (= 150). That gives 150 cm³, so the volume has fallen from 250 cm³ to 150 cm³ and the decrease is 250 − 150 = 100. A1 for 100 cm³, with the unit. (Check: 3 × 150 = 450 = 1.8 × 250.)","hints":["Inverse proportion means the pressure and the volume multiply to the same fixed number every time.","Use the pair of values given to complete the equation, then work out the new volume before comparing it with the old one.","The first line substitutes the given pressure and volume into P = k/V."]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write the ratio 24 : 36 in its simplest form.","a":"2 : 3"},{"q":"Express 12 as a percentage of 48.","a":"25%"},{"q":"Write down the single number an amount is multiplied by to decrease it by 6%.","a":"0.94"},{"q":"A cyclist travels 24 km in 1 hour 30 minutes. Work out the average speed in km/h.","a":"16 km/h"}]},"cards":[{"code":"R4","open":false,"front":"Ratio in its simplest form","back":"Put both parts in the same unit, then divide every part by their highest common factor: 20 : 35 = 4 : 7. The parts are whole numbers and no unit is written in the ratio."},{"code":"R5","open":false,"front":"Sharing a quantity in the ratio a : b","back":"The quantity is cut into a + b equal parts, so one part = quantity ÷ (a + b). The two shares are a × one part and b × one part, and they add back to the quantity."},{"code":"R3","open":false,"front":"One quantity as a fraction of another","back":"Put the first quantity over the second with both in the same unit, then cancel: 40 cm out of 3 m is 40/300 = 2/15. The fraction may be greater than 1."},{"code":"R1","open":false,"front":"Converting length, area, volume and compound units","back":"Length uses the conversion factor, area its square (1 m² = 10 000 cm²) and volume its cube (1 m³ = 1 000 000 cm³). A compound unit is converted one part at a time."},{"code":"R2","open":false,"front":"A scale written as 1 : n","back":"One unit on the drawing stands for n of the same units in real life: map length × n = real length, real length ÷ n = map length. 1 : 25 000 means 1 cm stands for 250 m."},{"code":"R6","open":false,"front":"A multiplicative relationship written as a ratio","back":"If A is k times B then A : B = k : 1 and A/B = k. Clear a decimal by multiplying both parts: 1.5 : 1 = 3 : 2. The order A : B matters."},{"code":"R7","open":false,"front":"Proportion as equality of ratios","back":"Two pairs are in proportion when their ratios are equal: 3 : 5 and 12 : 20 both simplify to 3 : 5. A missing value is found by scaling one ratio until a part matches the other."},{"code":"R8","open":false,"front":"A ratio written as a linear function","back":"If x : y = a : b then y = (b/a)x. The graph is a straight line through the origin whose gradient b/a is the ratio written as a fraction."},{"code":"R9","open":false,"front":"Percentage multiplier","back":"A p% increase multiplies by 1 + p/100 and a p% decrease by 1 − p/100, so +12% is × 1.12 and −12% is × 0.88. To find the original value, divide the new value by the multiplier."},{"code":"R10","open":false,"front":"Direct and inverse proportion compared","back":"Direct: y ÷ x is constant, so find the value of one unit then multiply. Inverse: x × y is constant, so as one quantity rises the other falls in the same proportion."},{"code":"R11","open":false,"front":"Compound units: speed, density and pressure","back":"Speed = distance ÷ time, density = mass ÷ volume, pressure = force ÷ area. The unit on the answer names the division: m/s, g/cm³, N/m². None is printed on the Exam Aid."},{"code":"R12","open":false,"front":"Length, area and volume ratios of similar shapes","back":"Lengths in the ratio a : b give areas in the ratio a² : b² and volumes in the ratio a³ : b³. A scale factor k multiplies area by k² and volume by k³."},{"code":"R13","open":false,"front":"Constant of proportionality k","back":"Direct proportion is y = kx; inverse proportion is y = k/x, which is y proportional to 1/x. Substitute one given pair of values to find k, then use the completed equation."},{"code":"R14","open":false,"front":"Gradient of a straight-line graph as a rate of change","back":"The gradient is the change in the vertical quantity for each unit of the horizontal one, with units of y per unit of x — pounds per day, metres per second."},{"code":"R16","open":false,"front":"Multiplier for growth and decay over n periods","back":"Apply the multiplier once per period, so after n periods the amount is P × mⁿ: 3% growth for 5 years is P × 1.03⁵ and 12% decay for 2 years is P × 0.88²."}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Compound interest — Total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, where P is the principal, r the rate per period and n the number of periods. PRINTED on the Foundation Exam Aid (W87990A) under 'Compound Interest', so it need not be recalled; the equivalent working, amount = P × multiplierⁿ, earns the same marks.","Speed = distance ÷ time, and its rearrangements distance = speed × time and time = distance ÷ speed. NOT on the Foundation sheet: remember it.","Density = mass ÷ volume, and its rearrangements mass = density × volume and volume = mass ÷ density. NOT on the Foundation sheet: remember it.","Pressure = force ÷ area, and its rearrangements force = pressure × area and area = force ÷ pressure. NOT on the Foundation sheet: remember it.","Simple interest = P × r/100 × n, the same interest each period on the principal only. NOT on the sheet — only the compound formula is printed, so the two must be told apart from the wording of the question.","One part of a share in the ratio a : b = quantity ÷ (a + b), and the shares are a × one part and b × one part. Not a printed formula: remember it.","Percentage multipliers — increase of p%: × (1 + p/100); decrease of p%: × (1 − p/100); original value = new value ÷ multiplier; one quantity as a percentage of another = first ÷ second × 100. NOT on the sheet: remember all four.","Direct proportion y = kx and inverse proportion y = k/x, with k found by substituting one given pair of values. NOT on the sheet: remember both forms.","Similar shapes — lengths a : b give areas a² : b² and volumes a³ : b³; a scale factor k scales area by k² and volume by k³. NOT on the sheet: remember it.","Unit conversions — 1 m = 100 cm, 1 m² = 10 000 cm², 1 m³ = 1 000 000 cm³, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 hour = 3600 s, 1 m/s = 3.6 km/h. NOT on the sheet: remember them.","Map and drawing scales — a scale of 1 : n gives real length = map length × n and map length = real length ÷ n, both in the same unit. Not a printed formula: remember it.","Gradient of a straight line graph = change in y ÷ change in x, and for a line through the origin that gradient is the rate of change — the amount of y for each 1 unit of x, with its units written as one unit per the other. NOT on the Foundation sheet (nor on the Higher sheet): remember it.","Nothing else in this room is printed. The rest of the Foundation Exam Aid — area of a trapezium, volume of a prism, circumference and area of a circle, a² + b² = c², sin A = a/c, cos A = b/c, tan A = a/b, and P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B) — belongs to other rooms. The Higher sheet prints the same compound interest formula and adds the quadratic formula, the sine and cosine rules, ½ab sin C and P(A and B) = P(A given B) P(B), none of which are needed here."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T12:01:01.258Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Every answer key, solution line, mark tally, worked example, card and formulae-sheet claim checks out — all 16 bank questions, the 4 exit questions and the 4 lesson check questions re-solved clean, six questions are non-calculator with hand-workable numbers, difficulties split 5/7/4, and the only gaps are a missing gradient entry on the formulae sheet and a sol line that refers to a graph the student is not given."}}},"geometry-mensuration":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"geometry-mensuration","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Geometry and measures is 12–18% of the Foundation tier, and mensuration — areas, perimeters, volumes, circles, sectors, similarity and right-angled trigonometry — is where nearly all of those marks are won. It is also the part of the paper that most often appears inside another question: a cost per square metre needs an area first, a capacity in litres needs a volume in cm³ first, and a reasoning question about whether something fits needs a length from Pythagoras' theorem. The Exam Aid printed with every paper gives you the trapezium, the prism, the circle, Pythagoras' theorem and the three trigonometric ratios, so the marks are lost not on recall but on choosing the right formula, keeping every length in the same unit, and writing the unit on the answer — cm for a length, cm² for an area, cm³ for a volume. On Paper 1 there is no calculator, so answers are left as multiples of π or as surds, and the exact trigonometric values have to be known by heart.","idea":[{"h":"Standard units and keeping them consistent","t":"Length is measured in mm, cm, m and km; area in mm², cm², m² and km²; volume in mm³, cm³ and m³; capacity in ml and litres; mass in g, kg and tonnes; time in seconds, minutes and hours; money in £ and pence. Every quantity in one calculation must be in the same unit before you start, so a cylinder with radius 40 cm and height 1.2 m is worked in centimetres by writing 1.2 m as 120 cm. The links you need are 1 cm = 10 mm, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 km = 1000 m, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 tonne = 1000 kg, 1 litre = 1000 ml and 1 ml = 1 cm³, so 1 litre = 1000 cm³. The unit belongs on the answer: an area given as '40' rather than '40 cm²' loses the accuracy mark, and examiners give the unit its own line in the mark scheme.","code":"G14"},{"h":"Measuring, scale drawings and bearings","t":"Lines are measured to the nearest millimetre with a ruler and angles to the nearest degree with a protractor, and answers are accepted inside a small tolerance either side. A scale tells you what one unit on the drawing stands for in real life: a scale of 1 : 25 000 means 1 cm on the map is 25 000 cm on the ground, so you multiply by 25 000 and then convert the answer into sensible units. A bearing is a direction measured clockwise from north and is always written with three figures, so a bearing of 70° is written 070°. North lines are parallel, so the bearing of A from B and the bearing of B from A differ by exactly 180°: add 180 when the first bearing is under 180°, and subtract 180 when it is 180° or more.","code":"G15"},{"h":"Areas of triangles and quadrilaterals, and the volume of a prism","t":"Area of a rectangle = length × width, area of a triangle = ½ × base × perpendicular height, and area of a parallelogram = base × perpendicular height; only the area of a trapezium, ½(a + b)h, is printed on the Exam Aid. The height in every one of these is the perpendicular height, not a sloping side, which is the commonest error in the question. A prism is a solid with the same cross section all the way along, and its volume = area of cross section × length — that result is on the Exam Aid too. A cuboid is a prism (volume = length × width × height) and so is a cylinder, whose cross section is a circle, giving volume = πr²h. Areas are in square units and volumes in cubic units.","code":"G16"},{"h":"Circles, composite shapes and curved solids","t":"For a circle of radius r and diameter d, circumference = 2πr = πd and area = πr²; both are on the Exam Aid, but you must know that the radius is half the diameter, because squaring the diameter by mistake is what loses the mark. A composite shape is split into parts whose areas you know — a rectangle plus a semicircle, or a large circle with a smaller one cut out — and the parts are then added or subtracted. Do the same with a composite solid. The sphere, cone and pyramid formulae are not on the Exam Aid: Pearson prints them inside any question that needs them, so read the question for the formula rather than trying to recall it. When the shape is cut out of another, the perimeter is the outside edge only, so an edge that has become internal is not counted.","code":"G17"},{"h":"Arcs and sectors","t":"A sector is the slice of a circle between two radii, and the angle θ at the centre says what fraction of the whole circle it is: that fraction is θ/360. So the arc length is θ/360 × 2πr and the sector area is θ/360 × πr², and neither formula is printed on the Exam Aid — both have to be remembered. Write the fraction down as a separate line, because that is where the first method mark falls. The perimeter of a sector is the arc plus the two radii, so it is θ/360 × 2πr + 2r, not just the arc. The relation can be reversed: if the arc length or the area is given, divide by the circumference or by the area of the whole circle to get θ/360, then multiply by 360 to find the angle.","code":"G18"},{"h":"Congruence and similarity","t":"Two figures are congruent if they are identical in every measurement — same side lengths, same angles — even if one has been turned or reflected. Two figures are similar if one is an enlargement of the other: the angles are equal and every pair of corresponding lengths is in the same ratio. That ratio is the scale factor, found by dividing a length on the new figure by the matching length on the original, and once you have it every other length follows by multiplying (or dividing, going the other way). The hardest part is pairing the sides correctly, so match them by the angles they lie between or by the order of the letters in the two names. A perimeter is a length, so it too is multiplied by the scale factor.","code":"G19"},{"h":"Pythagoras' theorem and the trigonometric ratios","t":"In a right-angled triangle whose hypotenuse is c, a² + b² = c². Add the squares to find the hypotenuse; subtract to find a shorter side; then take the square root. The trigonometric ratios are sin θ = opposite/hypotenuse, cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse and tan θ = opposite/adjacent — remembered as SOH CAH TOA. Both are printed on the Exam Aid, so the marks are for choosing correctly: label the hypotenuse first (always opposite the right angle), then the side opposite the angle you are using, and whatever is left is the adjacent side. Use Pythagoras' theorem when three sides are involved and no angle, and a trigonometric ratio whenever an angle appears. To find the angle itself, use the inverse: sin⁻¹, cos⁻¹ or tan⁻¹.","code":"G20"},{"h":"The exact trigonometric values","t":"Paper 1 has no calculator, so the values of sine, cosine and tangent for the special angles must be known by heart: sin 0° = 0, sin 30° = 1/2, sin 45° = √2/2, sin 60° = √3/2, sin 90° = 1; cos 0° = 1, cos 30° = √3/2, cos 45° = √2/2, cos 60° = 1/2, cos 90° = 0; tan 0° = 0, tan 30° = √3/3, tan 45° = 1, tan 60° = √3. They come from two triangles: a square of side 1 cut along its diagonal gives 45° with sides 1, 1 and √2, and an equilateral triangle of side 2 cut in half gives 30° and 60° with sides 1, √3 and 2. None of them is on the Exam Aid, and an answer is left in surd form rather than rounded.","code":"G21"}],"examples":[{"title":"Volume of a cylinder as a prism","setup":"A cylindrical tin of beans has radius 4 cm and height 11 cm. Work out its volume, correct to 3 significant figures. A calculator may be used.","steps":["A cylinder is a right prism, so its volume = area of cross section × length, and the cross section here is a circle.","Area of that circle = π × 4² = 16π cm².","Volume = area of cross section × height = π × 4² × 11, the substitution the method mark is awarded for.","π × 16 × 11 = 552.920… cm³, which is 553 cm³ correct to 3 significant figures."],"cues":["Which 2D shape is the cross section of a cylinder?","Which formula gives the area of that cross section?","What is the cross-section area multiplied by?","How many figures should the final answer be given to?"]},{"title":"Perimeter of a composite shape","setup":"A shape is made from a rectangle 12 cm long and 7 cm wide, with a semicircle of diameter 7 cm joined to one of its 7 cm sides. Work out the perimeter of the whole shape, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","steps":["The outside edge is three straight sides plus the curved edge; the fourth straight side has become internal where the semicircle joins, so it is not counted.","The three straight sides add to 12 + 7 + 12 = 31 cm.","The curved edge is half a circumference: ½ × π × 7 = 10.995… cm, the line the method mark is awarded for.","Perimeter = 31 + 10.995… = 41.995… cm, which is 42.0 cm correct to 1 decimal place."],"cues":["Which edges of the rectangle are still on the outside of the shape?","What do those straight edges add up to?","How do you find the length of half a circle's edge?","How are the straight part and the curved part combined?"]},{"title":"Arc length of a sector","setup":"A sector of a circle has radius 9 cm and an angle of 40° at the centre. Work out the length of its arc, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","steps":["The angle at the centre gives the fraction of the whole circle: 40/360, which cancels to 1/9.","The whole circumference is 2 × π × 9 = 18π cm.","Arc length = 40/360 × 18π = 2π cm, the line the method mark is awarded for.","2π = 6.283… , so the arc is 6.3 cm correct to 1 decimal place."],"cues":["What fraction of a full turn is the angle at the centre?","Which measurement of the whole circle is the arc a piece of?","How do the fraction and the circumference go together?","What does that exact answer come to as a decimal?"]},{"title":"Finding an angle with a trigonometric ratio","setup":"A ladder 6 m long leans against a vertical wall with its foot on horizontal ground, 2.1 m from the base of the wall. Work out the angle the ladder makes with the ground, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Call the angle at the ground θ. The ladder is the hypotenuse and the distance along the ground is the side adjacent to θ.","Adjacent with hypotenuse means cosine: cos θ = 2.1/6, the line the method mark is awarded for.","2.1 ÷ 6 = 0.35, so cos θ = 0.35.","θ = cos⁻¹(0.35) = 69.512… , so the ladder makes an angle of 69.5° with the ground."],"cues":["Which of the three sides is the hypotenuse here?","Which ratio uses the adjacent side and the hypotenuse?","What does that fraction come to as a decimal?","Which calculator key turns a cosine back into its angle?"]},{"title":"Similar triangles in a shadow problem","setup":"A vertical stick 1.5 m tall casts a shadow 2 m long on level ground. At the same moment a tree standing on the same ground casts a shadow 14 m long. Work out the height of the tree.","steps":["Both objects stand upright with the sun at the same angle, so the two right-angled triangles are similar and corresponding sides are in the same ratio.","The shadows are a pair of corresponding sides, so the scale factor from the stick to the tree is 14 ÷ 2 = 7.","The heights correspond in the same way, so the tree's height = 1.5 × 7, the line the method mark is awarded for.","1.5 × 7 = 10.5, so the tree is 10.5 m tall."],"cues":["Why must the two triangles be the same shape?","Which pair of sides has both lengths given?","What does the scale factor do to the stick's height?","What is that multiplication worth, and in what unit?"]},{"title":"An exact length from an exact trigonometric value","setup":"(non-calculator) A right-angled triangle has a hypotenuse of 12 cm and one of its acute angles is 60°. Work out the exact length of the side opposite the 60° angle, leaving your answer in surd form.","steps":["Opposite with hypotenuse means sine, so sin 60° = x/12, where x cm is the side wanted.","Multiplying both sides by 12 gives x = 12 × sin 60°.","The exact value is sin 60° = √3/2, so x = 12 × √3/2, the line the method mark is awarded for.","12 ÷ 2 = 6, so x = 6√3 cm."],"cues":["Which ratio links the opposite side to the hypotenuse?","How is x freed from the fraction?","What is the exact value of that sine, with no calculator?","How does the 2 underneath cancel down?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A prism has a cross section of area 14 cm² and a length of 6 cm. Work out its volume.","a":"84 cm³"},{"q":"A right-angled triangle has shorter sides 7 cm and 24 cm. Work out the length of the hypotenuse.","a":"25 cm"},{"q":"Write down the exact value of tan 45°.","a":"1"},{"q":"A shape is enlarged by scale factor 3. A side that was 5 cm long becomes how long?","a":"15 cm"}]},"room":{"facts":["Area of a triangle = ½ × base × perpendicular height; area of a parallelogram = base × perpendicular height; area of a rectangle = length × width. None of these three is printed on the Exam Aid.","Volume of a cuboid = length × width × height and volume of a cylinder = πr²h; both follow from volume of a prism = area of cross section × length, which is printed on the Exam Aid.","Arc length = θ/360 × 2πr and area of a sector = θ/360 × πr², where θ is the angle at the centre. Neither is on the Exam Aid; the perimeter of a sector is the arc plus 2r.","Exact values: sin 30° = 1/2, sin 45° = √2/2, sin 60° = √3/2; cos 30° = √3/2, cos 45° = √2/2, cos 60° = 1/2; tan 30° = √3/3, tan 45° = 1, tan 60° = √3; sin 0° = 0, cos 0° = 1, sin 90° = 1, cos 90° = 0, tan 0° = 0.","A bearing is written with three figures, measured clockwise from north, so 70° is written 070°; the bearing of A from B differs from the bearing of B from A by 180°.","Unit links: 1 cm = 10 mm, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 km = 1000 m, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 tonne = 1000 kg, 1 litre = 1000 ml, 1 ml = 1 cm³ and so 1 litre = 1000 cm³.","In similar figures every pair of corresponding lengths is in the same ratio, the scale factor; congruent figures are identical in every length and angle."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) A bottle holds 1.5 litres of juice. How many millilitres of juice does the bottle hold?","a":"1500 ml","sol":"1 litre = 1000 ml, so a number of litres is converted to millilitres by multiplying by 1000: 1.5 × 1000 = 1500 ml (B1, with the unit).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["A millilitre is much smaller than a litre, so the number of them must be much bigger.","Use the link between litres and millilitres, then multiply rather than divide.","Your first line is the conversion 1 litre = 1000 ml."],"codes":["G14"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The parallel sides of a trapezium are 6 cm and 10 cm long, and the perpendicular distance between them is 5 cm. Work out the area of the trapezium.","a":"40 cm²","sol":"Area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h, which is printed on the Exam Aid, with a = 6, b = 10 and h = 5: ½ × (6 + 10) × 5 (M1 for a correct substitution). 6 + 10 = 16, half of 16 is 8, and 8 × 5 = 40, so the area is 40 cm² (A1; the square unit is part of the answer).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The formula you need is one of the results the paper prints for you.","Add the two parallel sides, halve that total, then multiply by the perpendicular distance.","Your first line is ½(a + b)h with the two parallel sides inside the bracket."],"codes":["G16"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A circle has a radius of 6 cm. Work out the area of the circle, giving your answer as a multiple of π.","a":"36π cm²","sol":"Area of a circle = πr² with r = 6: π × 6² (M1). 6² = 36, so the area is 36π cm² (A1). Leaving the answer as a multiple of π keeps it exact, which is what a non-calculator paper asks for.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["There are two circle formulae — this one squares a length, so the answer is an area.","Substitute the radius into πr² and square the radius before anything else.","Your first line is π × r² with the radius in place of r."],"codes":["G17"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Write down the exact value of cos 60°.","a":"1/2","sol":"cos 60° = 1/2 (B1). It comes from halving an equilateral triangle of side 2, which leaves a right-angled triangle with sides 1, √3 and 2, so the side adjacent to the 60° angle is 1 and the hypotenuse is 2.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["This is one of the values you are expected to know by heart; it is not on the formulae sheet.","Cutting an equilateral triangle in half down its line of symmetry leaves a right-angled triangle that still has a 60° angle in it; that half-triangle is where the value comes from.","Your first line is cos 60° = adjacent ÷ hypotenuse, so decide which side of that half-triangle is adjacent to the 60° angle and which is the hypotenuse, then compare their lengths as an exact fraction."],"codes":["G21"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The two shorter sides of a right-angled triangle are 5 cm and 12 cm. Work out the length of the hypotenuse.","a":"13 cm","sol":"Pythagoras' theorem, a² + b² = c², is printed on the Exam Aid. The hypotenuse is the longest side, so c² = 5² + 12² = 25 + 144 = 169 (M1). c = √169 = 13, so the hypotenuse is 13 cm (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The hypotenuse is the side opposite the right angle, and it is the longest side.","Square both of the shorter sides and add them, then undo the squaring.","Your first line is c² = a² + b² with the two short sides in place of a and b."],"codes":["G20"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The cross section of a prism is a triangle with base 6 cm and perpendicular height 5 cm. The prism is 12 cm long. Work out the volume of the prism.","a":"180 cm³","sol":"Volume of a prism = area of cross section × length (printed on the Exam Aid). Area of the triangular cross section = ½ × 6 × 5 = 15 cm² (M1). Volume = 15 × 12 (M1) = 180 cm³ (A1, with the cubic unit).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A prism is the same shape all the way along, so start with the shape of the end face.","Find the area of the triangle first, then use the prism rule.","Your first line is the area of a triangle, ½ × base × perpendicular height."],"codes":["G16"]},{"q":"A sector of a circle has a radius of 15 cm and an angle of 72° at the centre. Work out the area of the sector, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"141.4 cm²","sol":"The sector is 72/360 of the whole circle, and 72/360 = 1/5 (M1 for the fraction). Area of the whole circle = π × 15² = 225π cm², so the sector area = 72/360 × 225π = 45π cm² (M1). 45π = 141.371… , so the area is 141.4 cm² correct to 1 decimal place (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The angle at the centre tells you what share of the circle the sector is.","Work out the area of the whole circle, then take that share of it.","Your first line is the fraction, angle at the centre over 360."],"codes":["G18"]},{"q":"A metal washer is made from a circle of radius 8 cm with a circular hole of radius 3 cm cut out of its centre. Work out the area of the washer, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"172.8 cm²","sol":"Area of the large circle = π × 8² = 64π cm² (M1). Area of the hole = π × 3² = 9π cm², and the washer is what is left: 64π − 9π = 55π cm² (M1 for subtracting the two circle areas). 55π = 172.787… , so the area is 172.8 cm² correct to 1 decimal place (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The washer is one circle with a smaller circle missing from it.","Find both circle areas separately, then deal with the part that has been removed.","Your first line is πr² for the larger circle."],"codes":["G17"]},{"q":"In a right-angled triangle the hypotenuse is 9.4 cm and one of the acute angles is 35°. Work out the length of the side opposite the 35° angle, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"5.4 cm","sol":"The side wanted is opposite the 35° angle and 9.4 cm is the hypotenuse, so the ratio is sine: sin 35° = x/9.4 (M1). Multiplying both sides by 9.4 gives x = 9.4 × sin 35° (M1). = 5.391… , so the side is 5.4 cm correct to 1 decimal place (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Label the sides relative to the 35° angle before choosing anything.","Opposite and hypotenuse together point to one of SOH CAH TOA.","Your first line is sin 35° = opposite ÷ hypotenuse."],"codes":["G20"]},{"q":"A photograph is 15 cm wide and 10 cm high. It is enlarged so that the enlargement is mathematically similar to the original and 24 cm wide. Work out the height of the enlargement. A calculator may be used.","a":"16 cm","sol":"The widths are corresponding lengths, so the scale factor = 24 ÷ 15 = 1.6 (M1). Every length is multiplied by the scale factor, so the height = 10 × 1.6 = 16 cm (A1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The enlargement is the same shape, so every length has grown in the same ratio.","Use the pair of lengths you know both of to find the scale factor.","Your first line is new width ÷ old width."],"codes":["G19"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The bearing of B from A is 070°. Work out the bearing of A from B.","a":"250°","sol":"North lines at A and at B are parallel, so the two bearings differ by 180°. The given bearing is less than 180°, so add: 070 + 180 (M1). = 250, so the bearing of A from B is 250° (A1). A bearing is always written with three figures.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The north lines at the two points are parallel, so the two directions are linked.","A back bearing differs from the bearing by half a turn — add 180 if the bearing is under 180°, otherwise subtract.","Your first line is the given bearing + 180."],"codes":["G15"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A map has a scale of 1 : 25 000. Two churches are 8 cm apart on the map. Work out the real distance between the churches, in kilometres.","a":"2 km","sol":"A scale of 1 : 25 000 means 1 cm on the map stands for 25 000 cm on the ground, so the real distance = 8 × 25 000 = 200 000 cm (M1). Convert: 200 000 ÷ 100 = 2000 m (M1 for a correct conversion to m or km), and 2000 ÷ 1000 = 2, so the distance is 2 km (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The scale says how many real centimetres one map centimetre stands for.","Multiply by the scale first and worry about the units afterwards.","Your first line is map distance × 25 000, giving an answer in centimetres."],"codes":["G15","G14"]},{"q":"A cylindrical water butt has a radius of 40 cm and a height of 1.2 m. Work out how many litres of water it holds when it is full, correct to the nearest litre. (1 litre = 1000 cm³.) A calculator may be used.","a":"603 litres","sol":"Work in one unit: 1.2 m = 120 cm (B1). A cylinder is a right prism, so volume = πr²h = π × 40² × 120 (M1) = 603 185.8 cm³ (A1). 1 litre = 1000 cm³, so 603 185.8 ÷ 1000 = 603.18… litres, which is 603 litres to the nearest litre (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The two measurements are not in the same unit yet, and a volume in cm³ is not a capacity in litres.","Convert the height, find the volume as area of cross section × length, then convert the volume.","Your first line is the height written in centimetres."],"codes":["G16","G17","G14"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A solid sphere of radius 3 cm is melted down and recast as a solid cone of base radius 3 cm and height h cm, with no metal lost. Work out the value of h. [Volume of a sphere = 4/3 πr³. Volume of a cone = 1/3 πr²h.]","a":"h = 12","sol":"Volume of the sphere = 4/3 × π × 3³ = 4/3 × π × 27 (M1) = 36π cm³ (A1). Volume of the cone = 1/3 × π × 3² × h = 3πh cm³ (M1). No metal is lost, so the volumes are equal: 3πh = 36π. Dividing both sides by 3π gives h = 12 (A1), so the cone is 12 cm tall.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Melting and recasting changes the shape but not the amount of metal.","Write each volume in terms of π using the formulae given, then set them equal.","Your first line is the sphere volume formula with the radius substituted."],"codes":["G17"]},{"q":"A ship sails 8 km due north from A to B, then 15 km due east from B to C. Work out the bearing of C from A, correct to the nearest degree. A calculator may be used.","a":"062°","sol":"North and east are perpendicular, so ABC is a right-angled triangle with the right angle at B; measured from the north line at A, the side opposite the bearing angle is the 15 km and the adjacent side is the 8 km (M1). Opposite with adjacent means tangent: tan θ = 15/8 (M1). θ = tan⁻¹(1.875) = 61.927… (A1). The bearing is measured clockwise from north at A and written with three figures, so the bearing of C from A is 062° (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Sketch the journey — the two legs meet at a right angle, and the bearing is the angle at A from north.","In that right-angled triangle you know the two shorter sides, so pick the ratio that uses both of them.","Your first line is tan θ = opposite ÷ adjacent, with θ the angle at A measured from north."],"codes":["G15","G20"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Triangle ABC is right-angled at B, with AB = 9 cm and BC = 12 cm. Triangle XYZ is mathematically similar to triangle ABC, and XY corresponds to AB. XY = 21 cm. Work out the perimeter of triangle XYZ.","a":"84 cm","sol":"First find AC by Pythagoras' theorem: AC² = 9² + 12² = 81 + 144 = 225 (M1), so AC = √225 = 15 cm (A1). Perimeter of ABC = 9 + 12 + 15 = 36 cm. The scale factor from ABC to XYZ is XY ÷ AB = 21 ÷ 9 = 7/3, and a perimeter is a length, so perimeter of XYZ = 36 × 7/3 (M1) = 84 cm (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["You cannot find the perimeter of either triangle until you know all three sides of the first one.","Use Pythagoras' theorem on ABC, add its three sides, then enlarge the total.","Your first line is AC² = AB² + BC² with the two given lengths substituted."],"codes":["G19","G20"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Work out the area of a triangle with base 8 cm and perpendicular height 5 cm.","a":"20 cm²"},{"q":"A circle has a diameter of 10 cm. Write down its circumference as a multiple of π.","a":"10π cm"},{"q":"A sector of a circle has radius 8 cm and an angle of 90° at the centre. Work out its area as a multiple of π.","a":"16π cm²"},{"q":"The bearing of Q from P is 115°. Work out the bearing of P from Q.","a":"295°"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Metric unit conversions","back":"1 cm = 10 mm, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 km = 1000 m, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 litre = 1000 ml and 1 ml = 1 cm³, so 1 litre = 1000 cm³.","code":"G14","open":false},{"front":"Three-figure bearing","back":"A direction measured clockwise from north, always written with three figures, so 70° is written 070°. The bearing of A from B and of B from A differ by 180°.","code":"G15","open":false},{"front":"Scale of a map or scale drawing","back":"A scale of 1 : n means one unit on the drawing stands for n real units. Multiply a drawing length by n for the real length; divide to go the other way.","code":"G15","open":false},{"front":"Area of a triangle","back":"Area = ½ × base × perpendicular height. The height must be perpendicular to the base, not a sloping side. Not printed on the Exam Aid, so it must be remembered.","code":"G16","open":false},{"front":"Area of a parallelogram and of a trapezium","back":"Parallelogram: area = base × perpendicular height, and it is not on the Exam Aid. Trapezium: area = ½(a + b)h with a and b the parallel sides and h their perpendicular separation, and it is on the Exam Aid.","code":"G16","open":false},{"front":"Volume of a prism","back":"Volume = area of cross section × length, for any solid with the same cross section all the way along. Printed on the Exam Aid. A cuboid is a prism: volume = length × width × height.","code":"G16","open":false},{"front":"Volume of a cylinder","back":"A cylinder is a prism with a circular cross section, so volume = πr²h. Not printed on the Exam Aid — it comes from combining the prism rule with the area of a circle.","code":"G16","open":false},{"front":"Circumference of a circle","back":"Circumference = 2πr = πd, where r is the radius and d the diameter. Printed on the Exam Aid. The radius is half the diameter, so check which one the question gives.","code":"G17","open":false},{"front":"Area of a circle","back":"Area = πr², where r is the radius. Printed on the Exam Aid. Square the radius, never the diameter, and give the answer in square units or as a multiple of π.","code":"G17","open":false},{"front":"The sphere and cone formulae","back":"Volume of a sphere = 4/3 πr³, surface area of a sphere = 4πr², volume of a cone = 1/3 πr²h, curved surface area of a cone = πrl. Not on the Exam Aid; printed inside the question.","code":"G17","open":false},{"front":"Arc length of a sector","back":"Arc length = θ/360 × 2πr, where θ is the angle at the centre. The perimeter of the sector is the arc plus the two radii: θ/360 × 2πr + 2r. Not on the Exam Aid.","code":"G18","open":false},{"front":"Area of a sector","back":"Area of a sector = θ/360 × πr², where θ is the angle at the centre. Reverse it to find θ: divide the sector area by πr², then multiply by 360. Not on the Exam Aid.","code":"G18","open":false},{"front":"Congruent and similar figures","back":"Congruent figures are identical in every length and angle, however they are turned or reflected. Similar figures have equal angles and all corresponding lengths in the same ratio, the scale factor = new length ÷ original length.","code":"G19","open":false},{"front":"Pythagoras' theorem and the trigonometric ratios","back":"In a right-angled triangle with hypotenuse c: a² + b² = c². For an angle θ: sin θ = opposite/hypotenuse, cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse, tan θ = opposite/adjacent. All four are on the Exam Aid.","code":"G20","open":false},{"front":"The exact trigonometric values","back":"sin: 0, 1/2, √2/2, √3/2, 1 for 0°, 30°, 45°, 60°, 90°. cos: 1, √3/2, √2/2, 1/2, 0 for the same angles. tan: 0, √3/3, 1, √3 for 0°, 30°, 45°, 60°. Not on the Exam Aid.","code":"G21","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["The Exam Aid formulae sheet is printed as an insert in every Foundation tier 1MA1 paper. Six of the results it prints belong to this room and are listed first; everything marked Remember is not on the sheet and must be written from memory. (The sheet also prints compound interest and P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B), which belong to other rooms.)","Given on the Exam Aid: area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h, where a and b are the parallel sides and h their perpendicular separation.","Given on the Exam Aid: volume of a prism = area of cross section × length.","Given on the Exam Aid: circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd, where r is the radius and d the diameter.","Given on the Exam Aid: area of a circle = πr².","Given on the Exam Aid: Pythagoras' theorem a² + b² = c², in a right-angled triangle whose hypotenuse is c.","Given on the Exam Aid: sin A = a/c, cos A = b/c, tan A = a/b, in a right-angled triangle ABC whose hypotenuse is c — that is, sin θ = opposite/hypotenuse, cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse and tan θ = opposite/adjacent.","Printed inside the question, not on the Exam Aid: volume of a sphere = 4/3 πr³ and surface area of a sphere = 4πr².","Printed inside the question, not on the Exam Aid: volume of a cone = 1/3 πr²h and curved surface area of a cone = πrl.","Remember: area of a rectangle = length × width.","Remember: area of a triangle = ½ × base × perpendicular height.","Remember: area of a parallelogram = base × perpendicular height.","Remember: volume of a cuboid = length × width × height.","Remember: volume of a cylinder = πr²h.","Remember: arc length of a sector = θ/360 × 2πr, where θ is the angle at the centre.","Remember: area of a sector = θ/360 × πr², where θ is the angle at the centre.","Remember: perimeter of a sector = θ/360 × 2πr + 2r — the arc plus the two radii.","Remember: in similar figures, scale factor = corresponding length on the new figure ÷ corresponding length on the original.","Remember: sin 0° = 0, sin 30° = 1/2, sin 45° = √2/2, sin 60° = √3/2, sin 90° = 1.","Remember: cos 0° = 1, cos 30° = √3/2, cos 45° = √2/2, cos 60° = 1/2, cos 90° = 0.","Remember: tan 0° = 0, tan 30° = √3/3, tan 45° = 1, tan 60° = √3.","Remember: a bearing is measured clockwise from north and written with three figures; the bearing of A from B differs from the bearing of B from A by 180°.","Remember: 1 cm = 10 mm, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 km = 1000 m, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 tonne = 1000 kg, 1 litre = 1000 ml and 1 ml = 1 cm³, so 1 litre = 1000 cm³."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T11:57:26Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Answer keys, solutions, formulae-sheet claims and Foundation-tier scope are all sound — every one of the 16 bank questions, 4 exit tickets and 4 lesson checks re-solved to exactly the stated answer, form and unit, and the six \"Given on the Exam Aid\" results match the 2026 Foundation Exam Aid (W87990A) exactly, with nothing Higher-only anywhere in the room; only the shadow worked example's missing method-mark line and one redundant example step need a teacher's pen."}}},"geometry-vectors":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"geometry-vectors","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Vectors are the one part of Geometry and measures where the answer is a pair of numbers rather than a single value, and both statements in this room are assessed at Foundation tier as well as Higher. A transformation question that asks you to describe a mapping fully is only complete when the word translation is written alongside the column vector, so students who give one without the other throw away half of an easy tariff. The same column vectors turn up inside coordinate questions, inside questions about a shape on a grid, and inside problems where two moves have to be combined into one. The arithmetic itself is directed-number arithmetic, which is where nearly all of these marks are lost: a sign the wrong way round turns a correct method into a wrong answer. Paper 1 is a non-calculator paper, so every addition, subtraction and scalar multiplication in this room has to be done and shown by hand.","idea":[{"h":"Translations written as column vectors","t":"A translation slides every point of a shape the same distance in the same direction; the shape keeps its size, its shape and the way round it is, and only its position changes. One column vector fixes the translation completely: the top number is the movement across the page, positive for right and negative for left, and the bottom number is the movement up the page, positive for up and negative for down. In these notes a column vector is typed on one line as (across, up), so (−5, 2) means 5 squares left and 2 squares up. To find the vector that maps an object onto its image, pick a vertex and the matching vertex of the image and subtract: across = x of the image − x of the object, up = y of the image − y of the object. Checking on a second pair of matching vertices catches a sign error. Describe fully means name the transformation and give its vector; naming two transformations for one mapping scores nothing.","code":"G24"},{"h":"Adding, subtracting and scaling vectors","t":"Column vectors are combined one row at a time. To add, add the two across-components and, separately, the two up-components: (a, b) + (c, d) = (a + c, b + d). To subtract, subtract the matching components, remembering that taking away a negative adds: 5 − (−3) = 8. To multiply by a scalar k, multiply both components: k(a, b) = (ka, kb). On a diagram a vector is a directed line segment — an arrow that goes across by the top number and up by the bottom number, with the arrowhead giving the direction of travel. Drawn this way, a + b is the journey along a followed by the journey along b, ka is an arrow k times as long in the same direction, and −a is the same arrow reversed. The method mark is for the line that shows the components being combined, so write that line out even when the answer is short.","code":"G25"}],"examples":[{"title":"Describing the translation that maps one triangle onto another","setup":"On a coordinate grid, triangle A has vertices (1, 2), (3, 2) and (1, 5). Triangle B has vertices (−2, 4), (0, 4) and (−2, 7). Triangle A is mapped onto triangle B by a single transformation. Describe that transformation fully.","steps":["The two triangles are the same size, the same shape and the same way round, so every point has slid by the same amount.","Vertex (1, 2) of A and vertex (−2, 4) of B are matching corners, because each is the bottom left corner of its triangle.","Across: x of the image − x of the object = −2 − 1 = −3, so the slide is 3 squares to the left.","Up: y of the image − y of the object = 4 − 2 = 2, so the slide is 2 squares up.","Testing the same movement on another vertex: (1, 5) moves to (1 − 3, 5 + 2) = (−2, 7), which is a vertex of B.","So the transformation is a translation by the column vector (−3, 2), with −3 on top and 2 beneath."],"cues":["What has changed about the triangle, and what has not?","Which corner of B matches the corner (1, 2) of A?","Is the shape moving to the left or to the right?","Is the shape moving up the grid or down it?","How can the movement be tested on a second corner?","What two things must a full description give here?"]},{"title":"Carrying out a translation given by a column vector","setup":"Triangle T has vertices (2, 3), (5, 3) and (2, 7). Triangle T is translated by the column vector (3, −2), which has 3 on top and −2 beneath. Work out the coordinates of the three vertices of the image of T.","steps":["The top number adds 3 to every x-coordinate; the bottom number adds −2 to every y-coordinate, which takes 2 away.","Vertex (2, 3) lands on (2 + 3, 3 − 2) = (5, 1).","Vertex (5, 3) lands on (5 + 3, 3 − 2) = (8, 1).","Vertex (2, 7) lands on (2 + 3, 7 − 2) = (5, 5).","So the image has vertices (5, 1), (8, 1) and (5, 5), and it is the same size and shape as T."],"cues":["What does each number in the vector change?","Where does the corner (2, 3) land?","Where does the corner (5, 3) land?","Where does the corner (2, 7) land?","Has the triangle changed size at all?"]},{"title":"Adding and subtracting two column vectors","setup":"a is the column vector (5, −2) and b is the column vector (−3, 6), each written with the across-component on top and the up-component beneath. Work out a + b and a − b, giving each answer as a column vector.","steps":["Adding works one row at a time: the across-components give 5 + (−3), and the up-components give −2 + 6.","5 + (−3) = 2 and −2 + 6 = 4, so a + b = (2, 4).","For a − b the across-components give 5 − (−3), and taking away a negative adds: 5 + 3 = 8.","The up-components give −2 − 6, and taking 6 away from −2 gives −8.","So a − b = (8, −8)."],"cues":["Which component pairs with which?","What does each pair of components come to?","What does taking away a negative do?","What happens to the up-components here?","How is the second answer written down?"]},{"title":"Multiplying by a scalar and then subtracting","setup":"a is the column vector (4, −1) and b is the column vector (2, −5). Work out 3a − b, giving the answer as a single column vector.","steps":["The scalar 3 multiplies both components of a: 3 × 4 across and 3 × (−1) up.","That gives 3a = (12, −3).","Now take b away row by row: 12 − 2 across, and −3 − (−5) up.","12 − 2 = 10 and −3 + 5 = 2, so 3a − b = (10, 2)."],"cues":["What does the 3 in front of a do?","What is 3a as a column vector?","Which numbers are taken from which?","What does taking away −5 become?"]},{"title":"Two translations combined into one","setup":"A counter on a grid is translated by the column vector (3, −4) and is then translated again by the column vector (−5, 1). Work out the single column vector that would move the counter straight from its starting square to its finishing square.","steps":["One slide followed by another moves the counter across by both top numbers and up by both bottom numbers, so the single vector is the sum.","Across: 3 + (−5) = −2, so the counter finishes 2 squares to the left of where it started.","Up: −4 + 1 = −3, so it finishes 3 squares below where it started.","So one translation by the column vector (−2, −3) does the job of both."],"cues":["Why can the two vectors be combined at all?","What is the total movement across the page?","What is the total movement up or down?","How is that written as one vector?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Write the translation '2 squares right and 5 squares down' as a column vector.","a":"(2, −5), with 2 on top and −5 beneath."},{"q":"p = (−4, 3) and q = (6, −1). Work out p + q.","a":"(2, 2)"},{"q":"r = (−2, 5). Work out 3r.","a":"(−6, 15)"},{"q":"A shape is translated by the column vector (7, −2). Which single column vector translates it back to where it started?","a":"(−7, 2)"}]},"room":{"facts":["A column vector has the movement across the page on top and the movement up the page beneath; right and up are positive, left and down are negative. Written on one line here as (across, up).","To describe a translation fully, write the word translation and the column vector. Either one on its own is not a complete description.","The vector that maps an object onto its image is (x of the image − x of the object, y of the image − y of the object); check it on a second pair of matching vertices.","A point (x, y) translated by the column vector (p, q) lands on (x + p, y + q).","Vectors are added and subtracted component by component: (a, b) + (c, d) = (a + c, b + d) and (a, b) − (c, d) = (a − c, b − d).","A scalar multiplies both components: k(a, b) = (ka, kb). The arrow is k times as long, in the same direction unless k is negative, which reverses it.","Two translations one after the other are equivalent to the single translation given by the sum of the two vectors; if that single translation is (p, q), the translation back is (−p, −q), and (p, q) + (−p, −q) = (0, 0).","A translation changes only position: the image is congruent to the object, the same size, the same shape and the same way round, so lengths, angles and area are unchanged."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Write the translation '7 squares left and 3 squares down' as a column vector.","a":"(−7, −3)","sol":"The top number records the movement across the page and left is the negative direction, so the top number is −7. The bottom number records the movement up the page and down is the negative direction, so the bottom number is −3. The column vector is (−7, −3), with −7 on top (B1).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Only one of the two numbers records movement across the page.","Put the across movement on top and the up movement beneath, then decide the sign of each.","Your first line is an empty column vector with the across movement written in the top place."],"codes":["G24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) a = (3, 5) and b = (−4, 2). Work out a + b as a column vector.","a":"(−1, 7)","sol":"Add the across-components: 3 + (−4) = −1. Add the up-components: 5 + 2 = 7. So a + b = (−1, 7), with −1 on top (B1).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The two vectors are combined one row at a time.","Add the two top numbers together, then add the two bottom numbers together.","Your first line is the sum of the two across-components written out as an addition."],"codes":["G25"]},{"q":"p = (6, −2). Work out 3p as a column vector. A calculator may be used.","a":"(18, −6)","sol":"A scalar multiplies every component of the vector: 3 × 6 = 18 across and 3 × (−2) = −6 up. So 3p = (18, −6) (B1).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The 3 in front of p acts on the whole vector, not just the top number.","Multiply the across-component by 3, then multiply the up-component by 3.","Your first line is 3 multiplied by the across-component of p, written out."],"codes":["G25"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) a = (8, 3) and b = (5, −1). Work out a − b as a column vector.","a":"(3, 4)","sol":"Subtract one row at a time. Across: 8 − 5 = 3. Up: 3 − (−1) = 3 + 1 = 4, because taking away a negative adds. So a − b = (3, 4) (B1).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Subtraction of vectors works the same way round as addition does.","Take the top number of b from the top number of a, then do the same with the bottom numbers.","Your first line is the top number of b taken from the top number of a, written out."],"codes":["G25"]},{"q":"The point A has coordinates (−2, 6). A is translated by the column vector (5, −4). Work out the coordinates of the image of A. A calculator may be used.","a":"(3, 2)","sol":"Add the top number to the x-coordinate and the bottom number to the y-coordinate: (−2 + 5, 6 + (−4)) (M1). This gives the image (3, 2) (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The vector tells the point how far to move across and how far to move up.","Add the across-component to the x-coordinate and the up-component to the y-coordinate.","Your first line is the x-coordinate of A with the across-component added to it."],"codes":["G24"]},{"q":"Rectangle A has vertices (1, 1), (4, 1), (4, 3) and (1, 3). Rectangle B has vertices (−3, 4), (0, 4), (0, 6) and (−3, 6). Describe fully the single transformation that maps rectangle A onto rectangle B. A calculator may be used.","a":"A translation by the column vector (−4, 3)","sol":"The two rectangles are the same size, the same shape and the same way round, so the transformation is a translation (B1). Matching the vertex (1, 1) with the vertex (−3, 4): across = −3 − 1 = −4 and up = 4 − 1 = 3; the same movement takes (4, 1) to (0, 4), (4, 3) to (0, 6) and (1, 3) to (−3, 6), so the column vector is (−4, 3) with −4 on top (B1). The word translation on its own, or the vector on its own, is not a full description, and naming two transformations scores nothing.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Compare the size, the shape and the way round the two rectangles are before naming anything.","Pair up matching vertices and work out how far the shape has moved across and up.","Your first line pairs the vertex (1, 1) of A with the matching vertex of B."],"codes":["G24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) a = (−2, 5) and b = (4, −3). Work out 2a + b as a column vector.","a":"(0, 7)","sol":"First scale a: 2a = (2 × (−2), 2 × 5) = (−4, 10) (M1). Then add b one row at a time: across −4 + 4 = 0, up 10 + (−3) = 7. So 2a + b = (0, 7) (A1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Deal with the scalar before anything is added.","Work out 2a as a column vector first, then add b component by component.","Your first line is the scalar multiplying each component of a, written out."],"codes":["G25"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) a = (7, −4) and b = (−2, 6). Work out 3a − 2b as a column vector.","a":"(25, −24)","sol":"3a = (3 × 7, 3 × (−4)) = (21, −12) (M1). 2b = (2 × (−2), 2 × 6) = (−4, 12) (M1). Subtracting row by row: across 21 − (−4) = 21 + 4 = 25, up −12 − 12 = −24. So 3a − 2b = (25, −24) (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Both vectors have to be scaled before they can be combined.","Work out 3a and 2b separately as column vectors, then subtract the second from the first.","Your first line is each component of a multiplied by the scalar in front of it."],"codes":["G25"]},{"q":"Shape P is translated by the column vector (5, −3) onto shape Q. Shape Q is then translated by the column vector (−2, 6) onto shape R. Work out the column vector of the single translation that maps shape R back onto shape P. A calculator may be used.","a":"(−3, −3)","sol":"One translation followed by another moves the shape across by both top numbers and up by both bottom numbers, so P is mapped onto R by the sum (5 + (−2), −3 + 6) = (3, 3) (M1). The journey back is the negative of that vector, with the sign of each component reversed, so R is mapped onto P by (−3, −3) (A1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The shape only ends up in one place, however many slides took it there.","Combine the two movements into one vector, then think about what undoes it.","Your first line adds the two across-components of the given vectors."],"codes":["G24"]},{"q":"The point M has coordinates (−1, 4) and the point N has coordinates (5, 1). The translation given by the vector v maps M onto N. Write v as a column vector. A calculator may be used.","a":"(6, −3)","sol":"The vector is the image take away the object in each direction. Across: 5 − (−1) = 5 + 1 = 6. Up: 1 − 4 = −3 (M1). So v = (6, −3), with 6 on top (A1). A check: (−1 + 6, 4 + (−3)) = (5, 1), which is N.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Think about how far the point has to travel across the grid and how far up or down.","Take the coordinates of M from the coordinates of N, one direction at a time.","Your first line takes the x-coordinate of M from the x-coordinate of N."],"codes":["G24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) a = (2, −1) and b = (−3, 4). The vector c is such that a + b + c = (0, 0). Work out c as a column vector.","a":"(1, −3)","sol":"First combine the two known vectors: a + b = (2 + (−3), −1 + 4) = (−1, 3) (M1). For the total to be the zero vector, c must undo this movement, so c is a + b with the sign of each component reversed (M1). That gives c = (1, −3) (A1). A check: (−1 + 1, 3 + (−3)) = (0, 0).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["A total of (0, 0) means the journey finishes exactly where it started.","Add a and b first, then decide what must be added to that result to get back to the start.","Your first line adds the across-components of a and b."],"codes":["G25"]},{"q":"Triangle P is drawn on a centimetre grid with vertices (1, 2), (6, 2) and (1, 6). P is translated by the column vector (2, −5) onto triangle Q. Work out the area of triangle Q. A calculator may be used.","a":"10 cm²","sol":"A translation changes only the position of a shape, so triangle Q is congruent to triangle P and has the same area (B1). In P the base runs from (1, 2) to (6, 2), a length of 5 cm, and the perpendicular height runs from (1, 2) to (1, 6), a length of 4 cm, so the area is ½ × 5 × 4 (M1). This gives an area of 10 cm² (A1). Working the other way round, Q has vertices (3, −3), (8, −3) and (3, 1), with the same base of 5 cm and height of 4 cm.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what a translation does to the size of a shape before doing any arithmetic.","Find the base and the perpendicular height of the triangle from the coordinates, then use the area formula for a triangle.","Your first line reads the base and the perpendicular height off the coordinates of the two sides that meet at a right angle."],"codes":["G24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) a = (3, −2) and b = (k, n), where k and n are numbers. Given that a + b = (−5, 4), work out the value of k + n.","a":"−2","sol":"The across-components must agree, which gives the equation 3 + k = −5, so k = −5 − 3 = −8 (M1). The up-components must agree, which gives −2 + n = 4, so n = 4 + 2 = 6 (M1). Therefore k + n = −8 + 6 = −2 (A1).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Two column vectors are equal only when both rows match.","Write an equation from the top row and another from the bottom row, then solve each one.","Your first line sets the across-component of a + b equal to the across-component of the given answer."],"codes":["G25"]},{"q":"A robot starts at the point (−3, 2) on a coordinate grid. It moves by the column vector (5, −1), then by the column vector (−4, −3), and finally by the column vector v. The robot finishes at the point (1, 0). Work out v as a column vector. A calculator may be used.","a":"(3, 2)","sol":"After the first move the robot is at (−3 + 5, 2 + (−1)) = (2, 1) (M1). After the second move it is at (2 + (−4), 1 + (−3)) = (−2, −2) (M1). The last move must take it from (−2, −2) to (1, 0), so v = (1 − (−2), 0 − (−2)) (M1), giving v = (3, 2) (A1). Adding the three vectors to the starting point is an equally valid route and scores the same.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Track where the robot actually is after each move before worrying about the last one.","Translate the starting point by the first vector, then by the second, then compare that position with the finish.","Your first line adds the components of the first vector to the coordinates of the starting point."],"codes":["G24","G25"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write the translation '4 squares right and 6 squares down' as a column vector.","a":"(4, −6)"},{"q":"a = (2, 7) and b = (−5, −2). Work out a + b as a column vector.","a":"(−3, 5)"},{"q":"p = (−1, 3). Work out 4p as a column vector.","a":"(−4, 12)"},{"q":"The point (5, −2) is translated by the column vector (−3, 4). Write down the coordinates of the image.","a":"(2, 2)"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Vector","back":"A quantity with a size and a direction. On a grid it is drawn as an arrow; in writing it is given as a column vector. Where the arrow is drawn does not matter, only its size and direction.","code":"G25","open":false},{"front":"Column vector","back":"Two numbers written one above the other: the top number is the movement across the page, the bottom number the movement up the page. Typed on one line here as (across, up).","code":"G24","open":false},{"front":"Translation","back":"A transformation that slides every point of a shape the same distance in the same direction. Size, shape and the way round the shape is all stay the same; only its position changes.","code":"G24","open":false},{"front":"Describing a translation fully","back":"Write the word translation and the column vector that gives it. The word on its own, or the vector on its own, is not a full description, and naming two transformations scores nothing.","code":"G24","open":false},{"front":"Signs in a column vector","back":"Top number: positive for right, negative for left. Bottom number: positive for up, negative for down. So (−5, −2) means 5 squares left and 2 squares down.","code":"G24","open":false},{"front":"Vector that maps an object onto its image","back":"Subtract in each direction: across = x of the image − x of the object; up = y of the image − y of the object. Use matching vertices, and test the vector on a second pair.","code":"G24","open":false},{"front":"Translating a point by a column vector","back":"The point (x, y) translated by (p, q) lands on (x + p, y + q): add the top number to the x-coordinate and the bottom number to the y-coordinate.","code":"G24","open":false},{"front":"Adding two column vectors","back":"Add the matching components: (a, b) + (c, d) = (a + c, b + d). On a diagram this is the journey along the first arrow followed by the journey along the second.","code":"G25","open":false},{"front":"Subtracting two column vectors","back":"Subtract the matching components: (a, b) − (c, d) = (a − c, b − d). Taking away a negative component adds, so 5 − (−3) = 8.","code":"G25","open":false},{"front":"Multiplying a vector by a scalar","back":"Multiply both components by the scalar: k(a, b) = (ka, kb). The arrow becomes k times as long and keeps its direction, unless k is negative, which reverses it.","code":"G25","open":false},{"front":"Directed line segment","back":"A vector drawn as an arrow on a grid: it goes across by the top number and up by the bottom number, and the arrowhead shows the direction of travel.","code":"G25","open":false},{"front":"The negative of a vector, −a","back":"The same length and line as a but pointing the opposite way: every component changes sign. It undoes a, so a + (−a) = (0, 0), the zero vector.","code":"G25","open":false},{"front":"Two translations one after the other","back":"The same as one translation by the sum of the two column vectors, and the order in which the two are carried out makes no difference to where the shape ends up.","code":"G24","open":false},{"front":"Name one way to check a column vector written for a translation","back":"Apply it to a second pair of matching vertices and see that the image vertex is reached; count the squares across and up between one vertex and its image; translate an image vertex by the negative vector and land back on the object.","code":"G24","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Preamble: the Exam Aid formulae sheet printed as an insert with every Foundation tier 1MA1 paper prints eight results, and not one of them concerns vectors. (The Higher tier Exam Aid adds the quadratic formula, the sine and cosine rules, the area of a triangle as ½ab sin C and P(A and B) = P(A given B) P(B); it prints nothing about vectors either.) So every result this room uses, listed next, must be written from memory.","Remember: a column vector is written (across, up) with the across-component on top; right and up are positive, left and down are negative.","Remember: a translation is described fully by the word translation together with its column vector.","Remember: the vector mapping an object onto its image is (x of the image − x of the object, y of the image − y of the object).","Remember: the point (x, y) translated by the column vector (p, q) lands on (x + p, y + q).","Remember: (a, b) + (c, d) = (a + c, b + d) — vectors are added component by component.","Remember: (a, b) − (c, d) = (a − c, b − d) — vectors are subtracted component by component, and taking away a negative adds.","Remember: k(a, b) = (ka, kb) — a scalar multiplies both components, multiplying the length of the arrow by k and reversing it when k is negative.","Remember: −(a, b) = (−a, −b) and (a, b) + (−a, −b) = (0, 0), the zero vector, so the negative vector is the translation that undoes the original.","Remember: two translations one after the other are equivalent to the single translation given by the sum of the two vectors.","Remember: a translation produces a congruent image, so lengths, angles and area are unchanged — and the area of a triangle, ½ × base × perpendicular height, is itself not on the Exam Aid."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T12:00:39.598715Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"Mathematically clean throughout - all 14 bank questions, 4 exit questions and 4 lesson checks re-solved independently in node and every answer key, unit and solution line is right, both statements stay inside Foundation G25 (no vector proofs), and seven questions are non-calculator with hand-sized numbers; fix the \"nine results\" miscount in the Formulae sheet preamble before publishing."}}},"probability":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"probability","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Probability is the one part of this course where the answer is a claim about what will happen, not a measurement of what has. It runs through all three papers and every question is built from the same four moves: list the equally likely outcomes, count the ones you want, use the fact that a complete set of probabilities adds to 1, and multiply along the branches of a tree when two things happen together. Examiners set it in ordinary contexts — a frequency tree of survey answers, a biased dice tested 500 times, counters taken from a bag without replacement — and the marks sit in the working, not the final fraction. On Paper 1 you have no calculator, so the fractions must be cancelled by hand and left exact; on Papers 2 and 3 the same method is keyed in, and the decimal must be written to the accuracy asked. Get the counting and the sum-to-1 rule secure here and the Higher room's conditional probability is just the same idea with the total restricted.","idea":[{"h":"Recording outcomes in tables and frequency trees","t":"An experiment is recorded by writing the count of each outcome against that outcome in a frequency table; the counts must add to the number of trials, which is the first thing to check. A frequency tree does the same job for two attributes: the total splits down the first pair of branches (walk or do not walk), and each of those splits again down the second pair (Year 11 or not). Every pair of branches adds back to the number it came from, and the four end frequencies add to the original total, so one missing figure is always found by subtraction. Examiners give you three of the four end frequencies, or a total and two splits, and ask you to complete the tree and then read a frequency or a probability off it. Fill every box, including the ones you were not asked for, because the branch totals are where the method marks are.","code":"P1"},{"h":"Expected outcomes of future experiments","t":"If an experiment is repeated n times and one outcome has probability p, the expected number of times that outcome happens is p × n. The probability comes first: from fairness and equally likely sections for a spinner or dice, from a relative frequency for a biased one, or from a share of a group. Then multiply. The wording matters as much as the arithmetic — an expectation is not a promise, so 1/4 × 200 = 50 means about 50 in the long run, and getting 46 reds does not make the spinner unfair. Questions ask this straight ('how many times would you expect it to land on red in 200 spins') or in reverse, giving the expected number and the number of trials and asking for the probability, which is the same equation rearranged as p = expected ÷ n.","code":"P2"},{"h":"Relative frequency and the 0–1 probability scale","t":"The relative frequency of an outcome is the number of times it happened divided by the number of trials: 180 sixes in 400 rolls of a biased dice gives 180 ÷ 400 = 0.45. It is an estimate of the probability, and it is the only estimate available when the object is biased or the situation is not made of equally likely outcomes. Every probability, theoretical or experimental, is a number between 0 and 1, and may be written as a fraction, a decimal or a percentage — never as a ratio or as '1 in 4' when a value is asked for. The scale carries the words too: impossible at 0, unlikely below 1/2, even chance at 1/2, likely above it, certain at 1. Examiners ask you to work out a relative frequency, to place it on a marked scale, and to compare it with the theoretical value and say what the difference suggests.","code":"P3"},{"h":"Exhaustive and mutually exclusive outcomes","t":"Mutually exclusive outcomes cannot both happen in one trial; a set of outcomes is exhaustive if one of them must happen. The probabilities of an exhaustive set of mutually exclusive outcomes add to exactly 1, and that single fact answers two whole families of question. A missing probability in a table of four outcomes is 1 minus the sum of the other three. The probability of an event not happening is P(not A) = 1 − P(A), so P(the bus is late) = 0.12 gives P(the bus is not late) = 0.88. It also gives you a check: if your probabilities do not add to 1, something is wrong before you go any further. The Exam Aid prints P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B); when A and B are mutually exclusive, P(A and B) = 0 and it reduces to simple addition.","code":"P4"},{"h":"Why more trials give a better estimate","t":"A relative frequency from a small unbiased sample can sit a long way from the theoretical probability by pure chance: 13 heads in 20 throws of a fair coin gives 0.65, and proves nothing. As the number of trials grows, the relative frequency of an unbiased experiment settles towards the theoretical probability, so 5120 heads in 10 000 throws (0.512) is much stronger evidence than any short run. Examiners ask this as a written reason — 'explain why Jo cannot be sure the dice is biased' — and the mark is for saying that the sample is too small for the relative frequency to have settled, or that more trials would give a more reliable estimate. Saying only 'it is close to 1/6' or 'she was unlucky' does not score; the reason must mention the number of trials.","code":"P5"},{"h":"Enumerating sets and combinations systematically","t":"Listing everything without repeating or missing anything needs a fixed order and the right diagram. A two-way table or grid suits two experiments (four sandwiches down, three drinks across); a tree diagram suits two or three stages, one branch per outcome; a Venn diagram suits overlapping sets, where the overlap holds the members in both and the space outside the circles holds those in neither. With a Venn diagram, fill the overlap first and subtract it from each subject total, then check that all four regions add to the group size. Where the overlap is not given, the members in at least one set are (first set) + (second set) − (both), because adding the two totals counts the overlap twice. An ordered list works too, as long as the order is visibly systematic: HH, HT, TH, TT.","code":"P6"},{"h":"Possibility spaces and theoretical probability","t":"When every outcome is equally likely, P(event) = (number of outcomes in the event) ÷ (total number of equally likely outcomes). The work is the counting, and a possibility space diagram does it reliably: for two experiments, a grid with the first experiment's outcomes down the side and the second's across the top, with the total or product written in each cell. Two fair six-sided dice give a 6 × 6 grid of 36 equally likely cells, so a single cell has probability 1/36. In general, the number of combined outcomes is (outcomes of the first) × (outcomes of the second). Answers are given as a fraction in its lowest terms unless a decimal or percentage is asked for, and on Paper 1 the cancelling has to be shown. The grid also earns a method mark on its own, even if the final count slips.","code":"P7"},{"h":"Combined events: independent and dependent","t":"On a tree diagram you multiply along a path and add between paths. Each pair of branches from one point adds to 1, which is the quickest check that the tree is right. Events are independent when the first does not change the second — a coin thrown twice, or a counter replaced before the next is taken — and then P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B), with the same probabilities on both sets of branches. They are dependent when the first changes what is left: taking a red counter from 5 red and 3 blue without replacement leaves 4 red out of 7, so the second set of branches changes. State the assumption when asked — that each counter is equally likely to be taken, or that the events are independent. For 'at least one', it is almost always quicker to work out P(none) and subtract from 1.","code":"P8"}],"examples":[{"title":"A possibility space for the total of two dice","setup":"Two fair six-sided dice, one red and one blue, are rolled and the two scores are added. Work out the probability that the total is 9. Give your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms.","steps":["A possibility space grid for the red score 1 to 6 against the blue score 1 to 6 has 6 × 6 = 36 equally likely outcomes.","Reading the grid, the cells with a total of 9 are (3, 6), (4, 5), (5, 4) and (6, 3) — 4 cells out of the 36.","P(total of 9) = cells in the event ÷ all equally likely cells = 4/36.","4/36 cancels by 4, so the probability is 1/9."],"cues":["How many equally likely outcomes are there altogether?","Which pairs of scores add to 9?","What is a theoretical probability divided by?","What is the highest common factor of 4 and 36?"]},{"title":"Completing a frequency tree","setup":"60 students were asked whether they walk to school. 35 of the 60 walk to school. 14 of the students who walk to school are in Year 11, and 9 of the students who do not walk to school are in Year 11. Complete a frequency tree for this information and work out how many of the 60 students are not in Year 11.","steps":["The first pair of branches splits by walking: 35 walk, so 60 − 35 = 25 do not walk.","The walking branch splits again: 14 are in Year 11, so 35 − 14 = 21 are not.","The non-walking branch splits: 9 are in Year 11, so 25 − 9 = 16 are not.","Check the four end frequencies against the total: 14 + 21 + 9 + 16 = 60.","Students not in Year 11 are the two end branches 21 + 16 = 37."],"cues":["How many of the students do not walk to school?","How many of the walkers are not in Year 11?","How many of the non-walkers are not in Year 11?","What should the four end frequencies add up to?","Which end branches hold students who are not in Year 11?"]},{"title":"Relative frequency used to estimate a future count","setup":"A spinner has sections coloured red, green and blue. In a test of 200 spins it lands on red 82 times, on green 64 times and on blue 54 times. Work out the relative frequency of red as a decimal, and use it to estimate the number of reds in 500 spins.","steps":["A relative frequency divides the count of the outcome by how many trials were carried out, giving 82 ÷ 200.","82 ÷ 200 = 0.41, so the relative frequency of red is 0.41.","This estimate is used as the probability, so the estimated number of reds is 0.41 × 500 spins.","0.41 × 500 = 205, so about 205 of the 500 spins would be expected to land on red."],"cues":["What two numbers does a relative frequency compare?","What is that division as a decimal?","How is an expected number worked out from a probability?","Should the result be reported as an exact count or as an expectation?"]},{"title":"A Venn diagram with an unknown overlap","setup":"In a class of 30 students, 18 study French, 14 study Spanish and 5 study neither language. One of the 30 students is chosen at random. Work out the probability that the student studies both French and Spanish. Give your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms.","steps":["Members of at least one set = 30 − 5 = 25.","Adding the two subject totals counts anyone in both of them twice: 18 + 14 = 32.","The excess is the overlap, so 32 − 25 = 7 study both, and the diagram reads 11 French only, 7 both, 7 Spanish only and 5 outside.","P(both) = 7/30, which will not cancel."],"cues":["How many of the class study at least one language?","What happens to anyone in the overlap when the two subject totals are added?","The two totals come to more than the number in at least one set — what does that excess count?","Which region is wanted, and out of how many students?"]},{"title":"A tree diagram without replacement","setup":"A bag contains 5 red counters and 3 blue counters. Two counters are taken at random, one after the other, and the first is not put back. Assume every counter left in the bag is equally likely to be taken. Work out the probability that both counters are red, as a fraction in its lowest terms.","steps":["There are 5 + 3 = 8 counters to start, so the first pair of branches gives P(red) = 5/8.","After a red is removed, 4 of the 7 counters left are red, so the second branch gives 4/7.","Multiply along the path: P(red, red) = 5/8 × 4/7 = 20/56.","20/56 cancels by 4, so P(both red) = 5/14."],"cues":["How many counters are in the bag before anything is taken?","What is left in the bag if the first counter taken is red?","On a tree diagram, do you multiply along a path or add?","What is the highest common factor of 20 and 56?"]}],"check":[{"q":"The probability that a train is late is 0.3. What is the probability that it is not late?","a":"0.7"},{"q":"A fair coin is thrown 80 times. How many heads would you expect?","a":"40"},{"q":"Two fair coins are thrown. List all the equally likely outcomes.","a":"HH, HT, TH, TT — four equally likely outcomes."},{"q":"Ali throws a fair coin 20 times and Beth throws it 2000 times. Whose relative frequency of heads is likely to be closer to 0.5?","a":"Beth's, because the relative frequency settles towards the theoretical probability as the number of trials increases."}]},"room":{"facts":["P(event) = number of equally likely outcomes in the event ÷ total number of equally likely outcomes.","The probabilities of an exhaustive set of mutually exclusive outcomes add to 1, so P(not A) = 1 − P(A).","Relative frequency = number of times the outcome happened ÷ number of trials.","Expected number of successes = probability × number of trials (an expectation, not a guarantee).","On a tree diagram multiply along a path and add between paths; each pair of branches from one point adds to 1.","Independent events: P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B). Without replacement the total falls by 1 for the second stage, and the favourable count falls by 1 only if the item taken first was itself a success.","Two combined experiments have (outcomes of the first) × (outcomes of the second) equally likely outcomes.","Members of at least one of two sets = (first set) + (second set) − (members of both)."],"questions":[{"q":"The probability that it rains in Leeds tomorrow is 0.35. Work out the probability that it does not rain in Leeds tomorrow. (non-calculator)","a":"0.65","sol":"Rain and no rain are mutually exclusive and one of them must happen, so their probabilities add to 1. P(no rain) = 1 − 0.35. M1 for 1 − 0.35. = 0.65. A1 for 0.65 (65% or 13/20 also accepted).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Rain and no rain between them cover everything that can happen tomorrow.","The probabilities of an exhaustive set of outcomes add to 1, so subtract.","The first line is P(no rain) = 1 − P(rain)."],"codes":["P4"]},{"q":"A fair eight-sided dice is numbered 1 to 8. It is rolled 120 times. Work out the expected number of times it lands on a number greater than 6. (non-calculator)","a":"30","sol":"Two of the eight equally likely numbers (7 and 8) are greater than 6, so P(greater than 6) = 2/8 = 1/4. Expected number = 1/4 × 120. M1 for 1/4 × 120 or 120 ÷ 4. = 30. A1 for 30.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Each of the eight numbers is equally likely on a fair dice.","An expected number is the probability of the outcome multiplied by the number of trials.","The first line is P(greater than 6) = (how many numbers qualify) ÷ 8."],"codes":["P2","P7"]},{"q":"A biased coin is thrown 500 times. It lands on heads 320 times. Work out the relative frequency of heads. Give your answer as a decimal. (non-calculator)","a":"0.64","sol":"Relative frequency = heads ÷ throws = 320/500. M1 for 320/500 or 64/100. Dividing both parts by 5 gives 64/100 = 0.64. A1 for 0.64.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A biased coin has no equally likely outcomes to count, so its probability can only be estimated from what the 500 throws actually did.","An estimate like that compares how often the outcome happened with how many trials there were, and is then written as a decimal.","The first line is relative frequency = number of heads ÷ number of throws."],"codes":["P3"]},{"q":"A fair six-sided dice numbered 1 to 6 is rolled once. Work out the probability of rolling a number less than 3. Give your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms. (non-calculator)","a":"1/3","sol":"The equally likely outcomes are 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6, and two of them (1 and 2) are less than 3. P = 2/6. M1 for 2/6. Cancelling by 2 gives 1/3. A1 for 1/3.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Write out the scores the dice can show; each one is equally likely.","With equally likely outcomes a probability compares how many outcomes are in the event with how many there are in total, and the fraction is then cancelled.","The first line is P(less than 3) = (number of scores below 3) ÷ (number of scores on the dice), then cancel."],"codes":["P7"]},{"q":"A four-sided spinner can land on A, B, C or D. P(A) = 0.2, P(B) = 0.15 and P(C) = 0.4. Work out P(D). (non-calculator)","a":"0.25","sol":"The spinner must land on one of the four letters, so the four probabilities add to 1. 0.2 + 0.15 + 0.4 = 0.75. M1 for 0.75. P(D) = 1 − 0.75 = 0.25. A1 for 0.25.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The spinner has to land on one of the four letters.","An exhaustive set of mutually exclusive probabilities adds to 1, so the missing one is 1 minus the rest.","The first line adds the three probabilities you are given, before subtracting the total from 1."],"codes":["P4"]},{"q":"A dice is rolled 50 times. The scores are recorded as: 1 seven times, 2 nine times, 3 six times, 4 eleven times, 5 nine times and 6 eight times. Write down the relative frequency of a score of 4. Give your answer as a fraction. (non-calculator)","a":"11/50","sol":"Relative frequency = frequency of that score ÷ total rolls = 11/50. B1 for 11/50. It does not cancel, as 11 is prime and is not a factor of 50.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The frequency you need is already in the list; nothing has to be worked out from the other scores.","A relative frequency compares one recorded frequency with the total number of trials.","For a parallel, a score that came up 3 times in 20 rolls would have a relative frequency of 3/20."],"codes":["P1","P3"]},{"q":"80 people took a driving test. 45 of them were women. 33 of the women passed. 21 of the men failed. One of the 80 people is chosen at random. Work out the probability that this person passed the test. Give your answer as a fraction. A calculator may be used.","a":"47/80","sol":"Draw a frequency tree splitting 80 by sex then by pass or fail. Men = 80 − 45 = 35. M1 for 35. Men who passed = 35 − 21 = 14. M1 for 14. Total who passed = 33 + 14 = 47. M1 for 47. P(passed) = 47/80. A1 for 47/80. Check: 33 + 12 + 14 + 21 = 80.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["A frequency tree splits the 80 by sex first, then each branch by pass or fail.","Find the number of men, then how many of the men passed, then add the two groups of passes.","The first line is number of men = total − number of women."],"codes":["P1","P7"]},{"q":"A box contains 4 green pens and 6 black pens. A pen is taken at random, its colour is recorded and it is put back in the box. A second pen is then taken at random. Work out the probability that both pens are green. Give your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms. (non-calculator)","a":"4/25","sol":"There are 4 + 6 = 10 pens, so P(green) = 4/10. M1 for 4/10. The pen is replaced, so the picks are independent and the second probability is also 4/10: P(green, green) = 4/10 × 4/10. M1 for 4/10 × 4/10. = 16/100 = 4/25. A1 for 4/25.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The first pen goes back, so the box is the same for the second pick.","Multiply the probability of a green first by the probability of a green second.","The first line is P(green) = number of green pens ÷ total pens, and the same value is used twice."],"codes":["P8"]},{"q":"Spinner A is fair and has four equal sections numbered 1, 2, 3 and 4. Spinner B is fair and has three equal sections numbered 1, 2 and 3. Both spinners are spun and the two scores are multiplied. Work out the probability that the product is an even number. Give your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms. A calculator may be used.","a":"2/3","sol":"A possibility space grid of A against B holds 4 × 3 = 12 equally likely products. M1 for 12 outcomes or a complete grid. The products are 1, 2, 3 / 2, 4, 6 / 3, 6, 9 / 4, 8, 12, of which the even ones are 2, 2, 4, 6, 6, 4, 8, 12 — 8 in total. M1 for 8 even products. P(even) = 8/12 = 2/3. A1 for 2/3.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Every pair of scores is equally likely, so a grid of all the products shows them all at once.","Count the even products in the grid, then divide by how many products there are.","The first line is the number of equally likely outcomes = sections on A × sections on B."],"codes":["P6","P7"]},{"q":"Jo rolls a dice 60 times. It lands on a six 14 times. (a) Work out the relative frequency of a six, as a fraction in its lowest terms. (b) Jo says the dice must be biased. Explain why 60 rolls is not enough to be sure. A calculator may be used.","a":"(a) 7/30 (b) 60 rolls is too small a sample for the relative frequency to have settled, so it can differ from 1/6 by chance.","sol":"(a) Relative frequency = 14/60. M1 for 14/60. Cancelling by 2 gives 7/30 (about 0.233, against a theoretical 1/6 ≈ 0.167). A1 for 7/30. (b) B1 for saying the sample is too small / more rolls are needed, because the relative frequency of an unbiased experiment only settles towards the theoretical probability as the number of trials increases. 'She was unlucky' on its own scores nothing.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["One of these values is what happened; 1/6 is what theory predicts for a fair dice.","Divide the number of sixes by the number of rolls and cancel, then think about how large 60 is as a sample.","The first line is relative frequency = sixes ÷ number of rolls."],"codes":["P3","P5"]},{"q":"A gym has 40 members. 22 of them use the pool, 18 of them go to the classes and 7 of them do both. One member is chosen at random. Work out the probability that this member uses neither the pool nor the classes. Give your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms. A calculator may be used.","a":"7/40","sol":"Adding 22 and 18 counts the 7 who do both twice, so members using at least one = 22 + 18 − 7 = 33. M1 for 22 + 18 − 7 (or a Venn diagram showing 15, 7 and 11). Members using neither = 40 − 33 = 7. M1 for 40 − 33. P(neither) = 7/40. A1 for 7/40. Check: 15 + 7 + 11 + 7 = 40.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Some members are counted in both of the two totals you are given.","Find how many use at least one facility, then take that from the membership.","The first line is (pool) + (classes) − (both) = number using at least one."],"codes":["P6","P7"]},{"q":"A drawer contains 7 white socks and 5 grey socks. Two socks are taken at random, one after the other, and the first is not put back. Work out the probability that the two socks are different colours. Give your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms. A calculator may be used.","a":"35/66","sol":"There are 7 + 5 = 12 socks. P(white then grey) = 7/12 × 5/11 = 35/132. M1 for 7/12 × 5/11. P(grey then white) = 5/12 × 7/11 = 35/132. M1 for 5/12 × 7/11. Different colours happens either way round, so add the two paths: 35/132 + 35/132 = 70/132. M1 for adding the two paths. Cancelling by 2 gives 35/66. A1 for 35/66. Equivalent method: 1 − (7/12 × 6/11) − (5/12 × 4/11) = 1 − 42/132 − 20/132 = 70/132.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Two different colours can be drawn in two different orders.","Work out the probability of white then grey and of grey then white, then combine them.","The first line is P(white first) = whites ÷ total, with the second sock taken from one fewer sock."],"codes":["P8","P4"]},{"q":"A bag contains 240 beads. The ratio of yellow beads to purple beads is 3 : 5. A bead is taken at random, its colour is recorded and it is put back. This is done 600 times. Work out how many times a yellow bead would be expected. A calculator may be used.","a":"225","sol":"The ratio has 3 + 5 = 8 parts and 240 ÷ 8 = 30 beads per part, so there are 3 × 30 = 90 yellow beads. M1 for 90 (or for going straight to 3/8). P(yellow) = 90/240 = 3/8 = 0.375. A1 for 3/8. Expected number = 3/8 × 600. M1 for 3/8 × 600. = 225. A1 for 225.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The ratio tells you what fraction of the beads are yellow.","Turn the ratio into a probability, then multiply that probability by the number of picks.","The first line is total parts = 3 + 5, then P(yellow) = yellow parts ÷ total parts."],"codes":["P2","P7"]},{"q":"160 people were each asked which one of tea, coffee and juice they preferred. 45% of them preferred tea and 1/4 of them preferred coffee. The rest preferred juice. Work out how many of the 160 people preferred juice. A calculator may be used.","a":"48","sol":"1/4 = 25%, so tea and coffee together take 45% + 25% = 70%. B1 for 70% (or 0.7). The three preferences are mutually exclusive and exhaustive, so juice takes 100% − 70% = 30%, giving P(juice) = 0.3. M1 for 30% or 0.3. Number preferring juice = 0.3 × 160. M1 for 0.3 × 160. = 48. A1 for 48.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Tea, coffee and juice are the only choices, so between them they take the whole group.","Put both known shares in the same form, add them and subtract from the whole.","The first line writes 1/4 as a percentage, then takes (tea% + coffee%) from 100%."],"codes":["P4","P3"]},{"q":"A fair four-sided dice is numbered 1, 2, 3 and 4. It is rolled twice. Work out the probability that at least one of the two rolls is a 4. Give your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms. (non-calculator)","a":"7/16","sol":"Three of the four numbers are not a 4, so P(not a 4) = 3/4 on each roll. M1 for 3/4. The rolls are independent, so P(no 4 at all) = 3/4 × 3/4 = 9/16. M1 for 3/4 × 3/4. 'At least one 4' is the only other possibility, so P(at least one 4) = 1 − 9/16. M1 for 1 − 9/16. = 7/16. A1 for 7/16. Listing the 16 equally likely ordered pairs and counting the 7 containing a 4 scores full marks too.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["'At least one' is far easier to handle through the outcome it rules out.","Find the probability that neither roll is a 4, then take it from 1.","The first line is P(not a 4) on one roll = (numbers that are not 4) ÷ 4."],"codes":["P8","P4","P7"]}],"exit":[{"q":"A bag holds only red and yellow balls. P(red) = 3/7. Work out P(yellow).","a":"4/7"},{"q":"A fair coin is thrown 90 times. How many tails would you expect?","a":"45"},{"q":"Two fair coins are thrown. Work out the probability of getting two tails.","a":"1/4"},{"q":"A spinner lands on green 36 times in 90 spins. Work out the relative frequency of green, as a decimal.","a":"0.4"}]},"cards":[{"front":"The probability scale","back":"Every probability is a number from 0 to 1: 0 impossible, 1 certain, 1/2 an even chance, below 1/2 unlikely, above it likely. Write it as a fraction, decimal or percentage, never as a ratio.","code":"P3","open":false},{"front":"Theoretical probability with equally likely outcomes","back":"P(event) = number of outcomes in the event ÷ total number of equally likely outcomes. On a fair six-sided dice, P(even) = 3/6 = 1/2.","code":"P7","open":false},{"front":"Relative frequency (experimental probability)","back":"Relative frequency = number of times the outcome happened ÷ number of trials. It estimates a probability that cannot be found from equally likely outcomes, such as for a biased dice: 180 sixes in 400 rolls gives 0.45.","code":"P3","open":false},{"front":"Expected number of successes in n trials","back":"Expected number = probability × number of trials: 1/4 × 200 = 50. It is an expectation over the long run, not a guarantee, so the actual count usually differs.","code":"P2","open":false},{"front":"The sum of an exhaustive set of mutually exclusive probabilities","back":"They add to exactly 1, so a missing probability is 1 minus the sum of the others: 0.3 + 0.1 + 0.45 = 0.85, leaving 0.15.","code":"P4","open":false},{"front":"P(not A)","back":"P(not A) = 1 − P(A), because A and 'not A' are mutually exclusive and one of them must happen. P(late) = 0.12 gives P(not late) = 0.88.","code":"P4","open":false},{"front":"Mutually exclusive events","back":"Events that cannot both happen in the same trial, such as rolling a 2 and rolling a 5 on one roll. Then P(A and B) = 0 and P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B).","code":"P4","open":false},{"front":"The effect of sample size on relative frequency","back":"A relative frequency from a small unbiased sample can sit far from the theoretical probability by chance; as the number of trials grows it settles towards that value, so a long run is stronger evidence of bias.","code":"P5","open":false},{"front":"Frequency tree","back":"A diagram splitting a total by one attribute and then by a second, with a frequency at every branch end. Each pair of branches adds back to the figure it came from, and the end frequencies add to the total.","code":"P1","open":false},{"front":"Possibility space diagram","back":"A list, table or grid of every equally likely outcome of one or two experiments. Two fair dice give a 6 × 6 grid of 36 cells, so one cell has probability 1/36.","code":"P7","open":false},{"front":"Reading a Venn diagram","back":"The overlap holds members in both sets, the outer region those in neither, and all regions add to the group size. Members of at least one set = (first) + (second) − (both).","code":"P6","open":false},{"front":"Tree diagram: along and between branches","back":"Multiply the probabilities along a path to get that path's probability; add the probabilities of the different paths that give the event. Each pair of branches from one point adds to 1.","code":"P8","open":false},{"front":"Independent events","back":"Events where the first does not change the probability of the second, as when a counter is replaced. Then P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B), and both sets of branches carry the same probabilities.","code":"P8","open":false},{"front":"Dependent events (without replacement)","back":"The first outcome changes what is left, so the second set of branch probabilities changes. From 6 red and 4 blue, P(red then red) = 6/10 × 5/9 = 1/3.","code":"P8","open":false},{"front":"Give one way of listing every outcome of a combined experiment","back":"A two-way table or possibility space grid; a tree diagram with one branch per outcome; an ordered list written systematically, such as HH, HT, TH, TT.","code":"P6","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B) — printed under Probability on the Foundation Exam Aid (W87990A), so it does not have to be remembered. For mutually exclusive events P(A and B) = 0 and it reduces to P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B).","The probabilities of an exhaustive set of mutually exclusive outcomes add to 1 — not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","P(not A) = 1 − P(A) — not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","P(event) = number of equally likely outcomes in the event ÷ total number of equally likely outcomes — not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","Relative frequency = number of times the outcome happened ÷ number of trials — not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","Expected number of successes = probability × number of trials — not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","Independent events: P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B), which is multiplying along the branches of a tree — not on the Foundation Exam Aid: remember it.","Dependent events: no formula is printed. After the first item is taken and not replaced, drop the total by 1 and drop the favourable count by 1 as well if that item was a success, then multiply along the path.","Combined experiments: total number of equally likely outcomes = (outcomes of the first) × (outcomes of the second) — not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","Members of at least one of two sets = (first set) + (second set) − (members of both) — not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","The Higher Exam Aid (W87991A) prints one extra probability line, P(A and B) = P(A given B) P(B). The Foundation sheet (W87990A) does not print it, and conditional probability is Higher-tier content, so nothing in this room relies on it.","Nothing else on the Foundation Exam Aid belongs here: the rest of W87990A is the area of a trapezium, the volume of a prism, the circumference and area of a circle, a² + b² = c², sin A = a/c, cos A = b/c, tan A = a/b and Total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T11:58:32Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.84,"notes":"Every answer key checks out — all 15 bank questions, the 4 exit questions, the 4 lesson checks and all 5 worked examples were re-solved independently in Python (exact fractions, with the two-dice, two-spinner and two-roll spaces enumerated by brute force), every sol's M/A/B marks add to its tariff, P1–P8 are covered in order with nothing beyond Foundation, and every Formulae sheet claim matches Exam Aid W87990A and W87991A in exam-aid.md exactly; the repairs needed are the decimal acceptances in five solutions, the false without-replacement fact, and the three questions whose answers are already printed on the cards."}}},"number-measures-higher":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"number-measures-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Bounds are where Higher tier turns a rounded measurement back into the range of values it really stands for, and the marks are worth protecting: every measured value in a question has been rounded, so any length, mass, time or rate worked out from it is itself only known within limits. Papers 2 and 3 set bound calculations inside area, speed, density and percentage questions, and Paper 1 sets them as short non-calculator writing-down questions or as a reasoning question — will the load be safe, will the piece fit, can the claim be true. The command words are 'write down the upper bound', 'calculate the upper and lower bounds' and 'give your answer to a suitable degree of accuracy', and each has a fixed method. Choosing the wrong end of one interval loses every accuracy mark in the question, so the habit of asking which value makes the answer largest and which makes it smallest is what this room builds.","idea":[{"h":"Upper and lower bounds of a measurement","t":"A measurement is never exact: a value given as a, correct to the nearest u, was rounded, so the true value lies within half of u either side. The lower bound is a − u/2 and the upper bound is a + u/2, and the limits of accuracy are written (a − u/2) ≤ x < (a + u/2) — the lower bound is included because it rounds up to the reading, the upper bound is not because it rounds up to the next reading instead. First find u, the accuracy the measurement is given to: 'to the nearest metre' gives u = 1 m, 'to the nearest 0.1 s' gives u = 0.1 s, 'to the nearest 10 g' gives u = 10 g, and a value written to a number of decimal places or significant figures is rounded to the place value of its last figure, so 5.83 to 3 significant figures has u = 0.01 and bounds 5.825 and 5.835. The exam asks for these as 'write down' answers carrying B marks, so no working can rescue the wrong half-unit.","code":"N16 (i)"},{"h":"Calculating with upper and lower bounds","t":"Once each measurement has been replaced by its interval, the bounds are carried through the calculation, and the only question at each step is which end makes the answer as large as possible and which makes it as small as possible. A sum is largest from the two upper bounds and smallest from the two lower bounds. A product behaves the same way. A difference a − b is largest from the upper bound of a with the lower bound of b, and smallest from the lower bound of a with the upper bound of b. A quotient a ÷ b is largest from the upper bound of a over the lower bound of b, and smallest from the lower bound of a over the upper bound of b — dividing by the smallest possible divisor gives the biggest answer. An exact number in the calculation, such as the 4 in the perimeter of a square, has no bounds of its own. Finally the two answers are interpreted: 'give your answer to a suitable degree of accuracy' means round the upper and lower bounds to the same accuracy and quote the value they agree on, and in a decision question the bound that works against you is the one that settles it.","code":"N16 (ii)"}],"examples":[{"title":"Bounds of a length measured to the nearest centimetre","setup":"A piece of wood has a length of 24 cm, measured to the nearest centimetre. Write down the lower bound and the upper bound of the length L of the wood, and then write the limits of accuracy as an inequality in cm.","steps":["Measuring to the nearest centimetre means the true value lies within half of 1 cm of the reading, and half of 1 cm is 0.5 cm.","Lower bound = 24 − 0.5 = 23.5 cm, and a length of exactly 23.5 cm would round up to the reading, so it is included.","Upper bound = 24 + 0.5 = 24.5 cm, but a length of exactly 24.5 cm would round up to 25 cm instead, so the true length stays below it.","The limits of accuracy are therefore 23.5 ≤ L < 24.5, in cm."],"cues":["How far either side of a reading can the true value be?","What is the smallest length that still rounds to this reading?","Does a value at the top of the range round to this reading or to the next one?","Which end of the interval takes ≤ and which takes <?"]},{"title":"Bounds of an area, and a suitable degree of accuracy","setup":"A rectangle measures 8.2 cm by 5.6 cm, each length correct to the nearest 0.1 cm. Calculate the upper bound and the lower bound of the area of the rectangle, and then give the area to a suitable degree of accuracy, explaining why that accuracy is the right one.","steps":["Half of 0.1 cm is 0.05 cm, so the length lies in 8.15 ≤ l < 8.25 and the width in 5.55 ≤ w < 5.65, in cm.","A product is largest when both factors are largest, so the upper bound of the area = 8.25 × 5.65 = 46.6125 cm².","A product is smallest when both factors are smallest, so the lower bound of the area = 8.15 × 5.55 = 45.2325 cm².","Rounding both bounds to 2 significant figures gives 47 cm² and 45 cm²; they disagree, so 2 significant figures cannot be justified.","Rounding both bounds to 1 significant figure gives 50 cm² each time, so the area is 50 cm² to 1 significant figure."],"cues":["How wide is the interval for each side of the rectangle?","Which end of each interval makes the area as large as it can be?","Which end of each interval makes the area as small as it can be?","Do the two bounds agree once they are rounded to two significant figures?","How accurate can the answer be if the two bounds are to agree?"]},{"title":"Upper and lower bounds of a speed","setup":"A runner covers a distance measured as 96 m, correct to the nearest metre, in a time measured as 7.5 seconds, correct to the nearest 0.1 second. Calculate the upper bound and the lower bound of the runner's average speed in m/s, each correct to 2 decimal places. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Distance: half of 1 m is 0.5 m, so 95.5 ≤ d < 96.5 in m. Time: half of 0.1 s is 0.05 s, so 7.45 ≤ t < 7.55 in s.","Average speed = distance ÷ time, and a quotient is largest when the number being divided is largest and the divider is smallest.","Upper bound of the speed = 96.5 ÷ 7.45 = 12.9530… = 12.95 m/s (2 d.p.).","The quotient is smallest the other way round, so the lower bound = 95.5 ÷ 7.55 = 12.6490… = 12.65 m/s (2 d.p.)."],"cues":["What is the interval for each of the two measurements?","Which quantity is divided by which, and what makes a quotient big?","Which pair of values goes into the division for the largest speed?","Which pair of values is left for the smallest speed?"]},{"title":"Deciding with bounds: will it certainly fit?","setup":"A doorway is 76 cm wide, correct to the nearest centimetre. A wardrobe is 75.8 cm wide, correct to the nearest 0.1 cm. Decide whether the wardrobe is certain to fit through the doorway without being turned, and justify the decision using bounds.","steps":["To be certain, the narrowest the doorway could be must still be wider than the widest the wardrobe could be.","Doorway: half of 1 cm is 0.5 cm, so its lower bound is 76 − 0.5 = 75.5 cm.","Wardrobe: half of 0.1 cm is 0.05 cm, so its upper bound is 75.8 + 0.05 = 75.85 cm.","75.85 cm is greater than 75.5 cm, so the wardrobe could be wider than the doorway and it is not certain to fit."],"cues":["Which extreme of each measurement is the worst case here?","What is the least the doorway could measure?","What is the most the wardrobe could measure?","Which of those two values is the larger, and what does that mean?"]},{"title":"Bounds of a difference","setup":"A plank of wood is 240 cm long, correct to the nearest centimetre. A piece of length 86.5 cm, correct to the nearest 0.5 cm, is cut from the plank. Calculate the upper bound and the lower bound of the length of plank that is left. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Plank: half of 1 cm is 0.5 cm, so its length p satisfies 239.5 ≤ p < 240.5, in cm.","Piece cut off: half of 0.5 cm is 0.25 cm, so its length c satisfies 86.25 ≤ c < 86.75, in cm.","A difference is largest when the starting value is largest and the amount taken away is smallest: upper bound = 240.5 − 86.25 = 154.25 cm.","A difference is smallest the other way round: lower bound = 239.5 − 86.75 = 152.75 cm."],"cues":["What is half of the accuracy the plank is given to?","What is half of the accuracy the cut piece is given to?","Which two values leave as much plank as possible?","Which two values leave as little plank as possible?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A length is 45 cm, correct to the nearest centimetre. Write down its lower bound.","a":"44.5 cm"},{"q":"A mass is 3.20 kg, correct to the nearest 0.01 kg. Write down its upper bound.","a":"3.205 kg"},{"q":"u = 9.6 and v = 4.1, each correct to 1 decimal place. Work out the upper bound of u ÷ v, correct to 3 significant figures.","a":"2.38 (from 9.65 ÷ 4.05)"},{"q":"Which bounds of p and q give the smallest possible value of p − q?","a":"The lower bound of p minus the upper bound of q"}]},"room":{"facts":["A value given as a, correct to the nearest u, has lower bound a − u/2 and upper bound a + u/2, and limits of accuracy (a − u/2) ≤ x < (a + u/2).","The accuracy u is the place value of the last figure given: 1 dp means u = 0.1, 2 dp means u = 0.01, and 5.83 to 3 significant figures means u = 0.01.","Sums and products: largest from the two upper bounds, smallest from the two lower bounds.","Differences: a − b is largest from UB(a) − LB(b) and smallest from LB(a) − UB(b).","Quotients: a ÷ b is largest from UB(a) ÷ LB(b) and smallest from LB(a) ÷ UB(b).","A suitable degree of accuracy: round the upper and lower bounds to the same accuracy and give the most accurate value they both round to."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) A steel rod has a length of 36 cm, correct to the nearest centimetre. Write down the lower bound of the length of the rod.","a":"35.5 cm","sol":"Correct to the nearest centimetre means the true length lies within half of 1 cm, that is 0.5 cm, of the reading. Lower bound = 36 − 0.5 = 35.5 cm (B1, unit needed). A rod of exactly 35.5 cm rounds up to 36 cm, so this value is possible.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The figure given is a rounded length, not the exact length.","For a value rounded to the nearest centimetre the true length can be up to half a centimetre either side.","Your first line is half of the unit the length was rounded to."],"codes":["N16 (i)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The mass of a parcel is 4.6 kg, correct to the nearest 0.1 kg. Write down the lower bound and the upper bound of the mass of the parcel.","a":"Lower bound 4.55 kg, upper bound 4.65 kg","sol":"Half of the accuracy is 0.1 ÷ 2 = 0.05 kg. Lower bound = 4.6 − 0.05 = 4.55 kg (B1). Upper bound = 4.6 + 0.05 = 4.65 kg (B1). The true mass is at least 4.55 kg and below 4.65 kg.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The mass on the label stands for a whole range of masses.","Halve the accuracy the mass is given to, then go that far below and above the reading.","Your first line is 0.1 ÷ 2."],"codes":["N16 (i)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The length of a tunnel is 8400 m, correct to the nearest 100 m. Write down the limits of accuracy of the length d metres, as an inequality.","a":"8350 ≤ d < 8450","sol":"Half of 100 m is 50 m, so the bounds are 8400 − 50 = 8350 m and 8400 + 50 = 8450 m (B1 for both values). A length of 8350 m rounds up to 8400 m so it is included, while 8450 m rounds up to 8500 m so it is not: 8350 ≤ d < 8450 (B1 for the fully correct inequality, including the two signs).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The limits of accuracy are the two ends of the range the true length lies in.","Halve the unit the length was rounded to, then take it from and add it to the reading.","Your first line is half of the rounding unit, in metres."],"codes":["N16 (i)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A number x is 5.83, correct to 3 significant figures. Write down the limits of accuracy of x as an inequality.","a":"5.825 ≤ x < 5.835","sol":"The third significant figure of 5.83 is in the hundredths place, so x was rounded to the nearest 0.01 (B1 for using 0.01, or for either bound). Half of 0.01 is 0.005, giving 5.83 − 0.005 = 5.825 and 5.83 + 0.005 = 5.835, so 5.825 ≤ x < 5.835 (B1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Decide first what place value the last figure given sits in.","Three significant figures here is the same as rounding to the nearest hundredth.","Your first line is half of the place value of the last figure."],"codes":["N16 (i)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) a = 6.4 and b = 2.5, each correct to 1 decimal place. Work out the upper bound of a + b.","a":"9","sol":"Each value is rounded to the nearest 0.1, so half of that is 0.05: the upper bounds are 6.45 and 2.55. A sum is largest when both values are largest, so the calculation is 6.45 + 2.55 (M1). 6.45 + 2.55 = 9, so the upper bound of a + b is 9 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Replace each letter by the largest value it could stand for.","Find the upper bound of a and the upper bound of b, then add them.","Your first line writes the two upper bounds as an addition."],"codes":["N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) x = 8.5 and y = 2.4, each correct to 1 decimal place. Work out the lower bound of x − y.","a":"6","sol":"Each value is rounded to the nearest 0.1, so the bounds are 8.45 ≤ x < 8.55 and 2.35 ≤ y < 2.45. A difference is smallest when the first value is as small as possible and the value taken away is as large as possible, so the calculation is 8.45 − 2.45 (M1). 8.45 − 2.45 = 6, so the lower bound of x − y is 6 (A1). Pairing the two lower bounds, 8.45 − 2.35 = 6.1, is the wrong choice of ends and scores nothing.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The two letters do not use the same end of their intervals here.","To make a subtraction as small as possible, start small and take away as much as possible.","Your first line writes the lower bound of x and the upper bound of y as a subtraction."],"codes":["N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"A triangle has a base of 12.4 cm and a perpendicular height of 7.8 cm, each correct to the nearest 0.1 cm. Work out the upper bound of the area of the triangle. Give your answer exactly. A calculator may be used.","a":"48.86625 cm²","sol":"Half of 0.1 cm is 0.05 cm, so the upper bounds are 12.45 cm for the base and 7.85 cm for the height (M1). Area of a triangle = ½ × base × height, and the product is largest when both lengths are largest, so the upper bound = ½ × 12.45 × 7.85 (M1). This gives 48.86625, so the upper bound of the area is 48.86625 cm² (A1, unit needed).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Each length stands for a range, and only one end of each range is wanted here.","Write down the largest possible base and the largest possible height, then use the area formula.","Your first line is the two upper bounds, 12.45 cm and 7.85 cm."],"codes":["N16 (i)","N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"A force of 480 N, correct to the nearest 10 N, acts on an area of 62 cm², correct to the nearest 1 cm². Pressure = force ÷ area. Work out the upper bound of the pressure, in N/cm², correct to 3 significant figures. A calculator may be used.","a":"7.89 N/cm²","sol":"Force: half of 10 N is 5 N, so its upper bound is 485 N. Area: half of 1 cm² is 0.5 cm², so its lower bound is 61.5 cm² (M1 for both of these values). A quotient is largest when the number divided is largest and the divider is smallest, so the upper bound of the pressure = 485 ÷ 61.5 (M1). This gives 7.8861…, which is 7.89 N/cm² to 3 significant figures (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The two measurements do not both take their upper bound here.","For the biggest possible pressure, make the force as large as possible and the area as small as possible.","Your first line is the upper bound of the force and the lower bound of the area."],"codes":["N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"Water flows into a tank. The volume collected is 245 litres, correct to the nearest 5 litres, and the time taken is 31.2 seconds, correct to the nearest 0.1 second. Work out the lower bound of the rate of flow, in litres per second. A calculator may be used.","a":"7.76 litres per second","sol":"Volume: half of 5 litres is 2.5 litres, so its lower bound is 242.5 litres. Time: half of 0.1 s is 0.05 s, so its upper bound is 31.25 s (M1 for both values). Rate of flow = volume ÷ time, and a quotient is smallest when the number divided is smallest and the divider is largest, so the lower bound = 242.5 ÷ 31.25 (M1). This gives exactly 7.76, so the lower bound of the rate of flow is 7.76 litres per second (A1, unit needed).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A rate of flow is one quantity divided by another, so each has its own interval.","For the smallest possible rate, collect as little water as possible in as long a time as possible.","Your first line is the lower bound of the volume and the upper bound of the time."],"codes":["N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Each of 40 identical crates has a mass of 12 kg, correct to the nearest kilogram. A lift has a maximum safe load of 495 kg. Can the 40 crates be carried in the lift in one go without any risk of passing the safe load? You must show your working.","a":"No — the total mass could be up to 500 kg, which is more than 495 kg","sol":"The worst case is the heaviest the crates could be: half of 1 kg is 0.5 kg, so the upper bound of one crate is 12 + 0.5 = 12.5 kg (M1). Upper bound of the total mass = 40 × 12.5 = 500 kg (M1). 500 kg is greater than the safe load of 495 kg, so the load might not be safe and the crates cannot be carried in one go with certainty (A1 for the comparison and the conclusion).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Only one end of the crates' interval can cause a problem here.","Work out the heaviest one crate could be, then the heaviest all of them could be together.","Your first line is the upper bound of the mass of one crate."],"codes":["N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A square tile has sides of length 7.0 cm, correct to the nearest 0.1 cm. Work out the upper bound of the perimeter of the tile.","a":"28.2 cm","sol":"Half of 0.1 cm is 0.05 cm, so the upper bound of the side is 7.0 + 0.05 = 7.05 cm (M1). The perimeter is 4 sides, and 4 is an exact number with no bounds of its own, so the upper bound of the perimeter = 4 × 7.05 = 28.2 cm (A1, unit needed).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The number of sides is exact; only the length of a side has been rounded.","Find the longest a side could be, then use it in the perimeter of the square.","Your first line is the upper bound of the length of one side."],"codes":["N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"A car travels at a constant speed of 12.53 m/s, correct to 2 decimal places, for 4.13 seconds, correct to 2 decimal places. Work out the distance travelled, in metres, giving your answer to a suitable degree of accuracy. You must show your working and explain why your answer is given to that accuracy. A calculator may be used.","a":"52 m (correct to 2 significant figures)","sol":"Correct to 2 decimal places means rounded to the nearest 0.01, and half of that is 0.005, so 12.525 ≤ speed < 12.535 and 4.125 ≤ time < 4.135 (M1). Distance = speed × time, so the upper bound = 12.535 × 4.135 = 51.832225 m (M1) and the lower bound = 12.525 × 4.125 = 51.665625 m (M1). Both bounds round to 52 to 2 significant figures, while to 3 significant figures they give 51.8 and 51.7 and disagree, so the distance is 52 m to 2 significant figures (A1 for 52 m with the comparison of the rounded bounds).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A calculation on rounded values cannot give an answer more accurate than the values allow.","Work out the upper and lower bounds of the distance, then round both to the same accuracy.","Your first line is half of 0.01, the amount each measurement can be out by."],"codes":["N16 (i)","N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"A metal block is a cuboid with edges of length 6.2 cm, 4.5 cm and 3.8 cm, each correct to the nearest 0.1 cm. The mass of the block is 640 g, correct to the nearest 10 g. Density = mass ÷ volume. Work out the upper bound of the density of the metal, in g/cm³, correct to 3 significant figures. A calculator may be used.","a":"6.28 g/cm³","sol":"Mass: half of 10 g is 5 g, so its upper bound is 645 g (M1). For the largest density the volume must be as small as possible, so use the lower bound of each edge, 6.15 cm, 4.45 cm and 3.75 cm: lower bound of the volume = 6.15 × 4.45 × 3.75 = 102.628125 cm³ (M1). Upper bound of the density = 645 ÷ 102.628125 (M1) = 6.2848…, which is 6.28 g/cm³ to 3 significant figures (A1, unit needed).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The mass and the volume pull in opposite directions when the density is to be as large as possible.","Take the heaviest possible mass and the smallest possible volume, which needs the shortest possible edges.","Your first line is the upper bound of the mass, in grams."],"codes":["N16 (i)","N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A strip of ribbon is 50 cm long, correct to the nearest centimetre. The strip is cut into 6 equal lengths. Show that each of these lengths must be more than 8.2 cm.","a":"Each length is at least 49.5 ÷ 6 = 8.25 cm, and 8.25 > 8.2","sol":"The shortest each piece could be comes from the shortest the ribbon could be: half of 1 cm is 0.5 cm, so the lower bound of the ribbon is 50 − 0.5 = 49.5 cm (M1). The 6 pieces are equal and 6 is exact, so the lower bound of one piece = 49.5 ÷ 6 = 8.25 cm (M1). Every possible length is at least 8.25 cm, and 8.25 cm is greater than 8.2 cm, so each piece must be more than 8.2 cm (A1 for the comparison and the conclusion).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["'Show that' means the worst case has to be tested, not a typical case.","Find the shortest the whole ribbon could be, then share that length between the pieces.","Your first line is the lower bound of the length of the ribbon."],"codes":["N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"The price of a share rises from 240 pence, correct to the nearest 10 pence, to 315 pence, correct to the nearest 5 pence. Work out the upper bound of the percentage increase in the price, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"35.1%","sol":"Old price: half of 10 p is 5 p, so 235 ≤ old < 245. New price: half of 5 p is 2.5 p, so 312.5 ≤ new < 317.5 (M1 for both intervals). Percentage increase = (new − old) ÷ old × 100, which is largest when the new price is largest and the old price is smallest, so use 317.5 p and 235 p: increase = 317.5 − 235 = 82.5 p (M1). Percentage increase = 82.5 ÷ 235 × 100 (M1) = 35.106…, so the upper bound is 35.1% (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A percentage increase compares the rise with the price it started from, and both prices are rounded.","The smallest possible starting price makes the percentage increase as large as possible; pair it with the largest possible new price.","Your first line is the lower bound of the old price and the upper bound of the new price."],"codes":["N16 (i)","N16 (ii)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"A width is 18 cm, correct to the nearest centimetre. Write down the upper bound of the width.","a":"18.5 cm"},{"q":"A mass is 250 g, correct to the nearest 10 g. Write down the limits of accuracy of the mass m grams, as an inequality.","a":"245 ≤ m < 255"},{"q":"p = 3.6 and q = 1.2, each correct to 1 decimal place. Work out the upper bound of p + q.","a":"4.9 (from 3.65 + 1.25)"},{"q":"x = 7.4 and y = 2.5, each correct to 1 decimal place. Work out the upper bound of xy.","a":"18.9975 (from 7.45 × 2.55)"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Upper bound of a measurement","back":"The value the true measurement must stay below: for a value a given to the nearest u, the upper bound is a + u/2. A measurement exactly there would round up to the next reading.","code":"N16 (i)","open":false},{"front":"Lower bound of a measurement","back":"The smallest value the true measurement could take. For a value a given to the nearest u, the lower bound is a − u/2, and the true value can equal it, because that value rounds up to the reading.","code":"N16 (i)","open":false},{"front":"Limits of accuracy of a rounded measurement","back":"The two bounds the true value lies between, written (a − u/2) ≤ x < (a + u/2) for a value a given to the nearest u. Example: 24 cm to the nearest cm gives 23.5 ≤ L < 24.5.","code":"N16 (i)","open":false},{"front":"The rounding unit u of a measurement","back":"The accuracy the value is given to: 'nearest metre' means u = 1 m, 'nearest 10 g' means u = 10 g, 1 decimal place means u = 0.1. Halving u gives the distance to each bound.","code":"N16 (i)","open":false},{"front":"Bounds of a value given to a number of significant figures","back":"The rounding unit is the place value of the last significant figure. So 5.83 to 3 significant figures was rounded to the nearest 0.01, giving bounds 5.825 and 5.835.","code":"N16 (i)","open":false},{"front":"The inequality signs in the limits of accuracy","back":"≤ at the lower bound and < at the upper bound. The lower bound rounds up to the stated value so it is included; the upper bound rounds up to the next value so it is excluded.","code":"N16 (i)","open":false},{"front":"Bounds of a sum a + b","back":"Upper bound = UB(a) + UB(b); lower bound = LB(a) + LB(b). Both measurements use the same end of their intervals.","code":"N16 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Bounds of a difference a − b","back":"Upper bound = UB(a) − LB(b); lower bound = LB(a) − UB(b). The two measurements use opposite ends, because taking away less leaves more.","code":"N16 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Bounds of a product ab","back":"Upper bound = UB(a) × UB(b); lower bound = LB(a) × LB(b). Used for areas and volumes, where every length takes the same end of its interval.","code":"N16 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Bounds of a quotient a ÷ b","back":"Upper bound = UB(a) ÷ LB(b); lower bound = LB(a) ÷ UB(b). Dividing by the smallest possible divider gives the largest answer, so the two measurements use opposite ends.","code":"N16 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"An exact number inside a bounds calculation","back":"A counted or defined number, such as the 4 sides of a square or the 6 equal pieces, has not been rounded, so it has no bounds: use it unchanged in both the upper and lower bound calculations.","code":"N16 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Giving an answer to a suitable degree of accuracy","back":"Round the upper and lower bounds of the answer to the same accuracy; the answer is the most accurate value both bounds round to. If they disagree, round both less accurately and compare again.","code":"N16 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"The worst case in a 'will it fit?' question","back":"The pair of bounds that makes the outcome least favourable: the smallest gap with the largest object, or the largest load against a safe limit. If the worst case passes, the outcome is certain.","code":"N16 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Name one situation where the lower bound is the value that decides the answer","back":"Checking a shelf or gap is long enough for something to fit; checking a shared quantity gives everyone at least a stated amount; finding the smallest possible speed from a rounded distance and time, where the lower bound of the distance is divided by the upper bound of the time.","code":"N16 (ii)","open":true},{"front":"Bounds of a percentage change from rounded values","back":"Percentage increase = (new − old) ÷ old × 100 is largest when the new value takes its upper bound and the old value its lower bound, and smallest the other way round.","code":"N16 (ii)","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Preamble: the Exam Aid formulae sheet printed as an insert with every Higher tier 1MA1 paper prints nothing this room uses — no bound, no limit of accuracy and no compound measure — so every result below must be written from memory.","Remember: a value a given correct to the nearest u has lower bound a − u/2 and upper bound a + u/2 — no bound formula appears on the Exam Aid.","Remember: the limits of accuracy are written (a − u/2) ≤ x < (a + u/2), with ≤ at the lower bound and < at the upper bound.","Remember: the rounding unit u is the place value of the last figure given, so 1 decimal place gives u = 0.1 and 3 significant figures in 5.83 gives u = 0.01.","Remember: upper bound of a + b = UB(a) + UB(b); lower bound of a + b = LB(a) + LB(b).","Remember: upper bound of a − b = UB(a) − LB(b); lower bound of a − b = LB(a) − UB(b).","Remember: upper bound of ab = UB(a) × UB(b); lower bound of ab = LB(a) × LB(b).","Remember: upper bound of a ÷ b = UB(a) ÷ LB(b); lower bound of a ÷ b = LB(a) ÷ UB(b).","Remember: average speed = distance ÷ time, density = mass ÷ volume and pressure = force ÷ area — the Exam Aid prints no compound measure, and each of these is a quotient, so its bounds use opposite ends of the two intervals.","Remember: area of a triangle = ½ × base × perpendicular height, which the Exam Aid gives only in the form ½ab sin C, for two sides and the angle between them.","Remember: a suitable degree of accuracy is the most accurate value that both the upper bound and the lower bound of the answer round to.","Remember: an exact number in a calculation, such as the 4 in the perimeter of a square, has no bounds and is used unchanged."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T12:01:36Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Every answer key, solution line and worked-example calculation checks out exactly (bounds, the 2 s.f. 'suitable accuracy' judgement in Q11 and example 2, and all quotient/difference end-pairings), and the Formulae sheet matches the Higher Exam Aid line for line; fix the missing accuracy instruction on the triangle-area question and the self-contradicting upper-bound card and this is ready to publish."}}},"algebra-notation-higher":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"algebra-notation-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"At Higher tier the Algebra topic area carries 27–33% of the marks, and this room holds the manipulation Foundation never meets: cancelling an algebraic fraction, expanding a product of three binomials, factorising ax² + bx + c, setting an argument out as an algebraic proof, and using formal function notation for composite and inverse functions. Each of these is asked for in its own right, and each is also a step buried inside harder work on quadratics, graphs and circle proof, so a weakness here quietly costs marks across the whole of the algebra you meet. Much of it is examined without a calculator, where the written method is what earns the method mark, and a proof scores nothing at all unless every line of the reasoning is set down.","idea":[{"h":"Algebraic fractions, three brackets and ax² + bx + c","t":"The Higher list widens A4 to expressions 'including those involving surds and algebraic fractions', to 'expanding products of two or more binomials' and to 'factorising quadratic expressions of the form ax² + bx + c'. To cancel an algebraic fraction, factorise the numerator and the denominator fully and cancel a whole bracket that appears in both — never a single term. To expand three brackets, multiply two of them first, then multiply that quadratic by the third bracket term by term. To factorise ax² + bx + c, find two numbers with product a × c and sum b, split the middle term into those two x terms, and factorise the four terms in pairs. Surds behave like any other terms once you know √a × √a = a.","code":"A4"},{"h":"Algebraic proof","t":"A6 at Higher ends 'use algebra to support and construct arguments and proofs'. Testing a few numbers is not a proof: it only shows the statement works for the values you tried. A proof represents the general case with a letter — n for any integer, 2n for an even number, 2n + 1 for an odd number, n, n + 1, n + 2 for consecutive integers — then expands and collects until the required form is visible, usually by taking out a common factor such as 3(…) or 4(…). It ends with a conclusion in words saying that this holds for every value of n. Because the result is given, every line of the argument has to be written down: marks are for the chain of reasoning, not the answer.","code":"A6"},{"h":"Inverse and composite functions","t":"A7 at Higher adds 'interpret the reverse process as the inverse function; interpret the succession of two functions as a composite function (the use of formal function notation is expected)'. f(x) is the output of the machine f for the input x, so f(3) means substitute 3 into the rule. gf(x) means g(f(x)): apply f first and feed its output into g, so the order matters and fg(x) is usually different from gf(x). The inverse f⁻¹ undoes f — write y = f(x), rearrange to make x the subject, then rewrite the result using x as the input, and answer in f⁻¹(x) = … form. Examiners expect that notation, not words.","code":"A7"}],"examples":[{"title":"Simplifying an algebraic fraction","setup":"Simplify fully the algebraic fraction (x² + 2x − 15)/(x² − 25). You may assume that x ≠ 5 and x ≠ −5.","steps":["Factorise the top: two numbers with product −15 and sum +2 are +5 and −3, so x² + 2x − 15 = (x + 5)(x − 3)","Factorise the bottom: x² − 25 is a difference of two squares, so x² − 25 = (x + 5)(x − 5)","Rewrite the fraction with both factorisations in place: ((x + 5)(x − 3))/((x + 5)(x − 5))","The bracket (x + 5) appears above and below, so it cancels, leaving (x − 3)/(x − 5)"],"cues":["Which pair of numbers multiplies to the constant and adds to the coefficient of x?","What kind of quadratic has no x term and a subtracted square?","What does the fraction look like once neither part is a single quadratic?","Which bracket appears in both parts of the fraction?"]},{"title":"Expanding a product of three binomials","setup":"Expand and simplify the product of three binomials (x − 3)(x + 2)(x − 1), giving your answer as a cubic expression with the terms written in descending powers of x.","steps":["Multiply the first two brackets: (x − 3)(x + 2) = x² + 2x − 3x − 6 = x² − x − 6","Now multiply every term of x² − x − 6 by x: x³ − x² − 6x","Multiply every term of x² − x − 6 by −1: −x² + x + 6","Add the two lines and collect like terms: x³ − 2x² − 5x + 6"],"cues":["Which two brackets are easiest to deal with first?","What does each term of the quadratic give when multiplied by the x in the last bracket?","What does the same quadratic give when multiplied by the number in the last bracket?","Which terms in the two lines are like terms?"]},{"title":"Factorising when the coefficient of x² is not 1","setup":"Factorise fully the quadratic expression 4x² − 4x − 15, in which the coefficient of x² is not 1. Give your answer as a product of two brackets.","steps":["Multiply the coefficient of x² by the constant term: 4 × (−15) = −60, while the middle coefficient is −4","Look for two numbers with product −60 and sum −4: they are −10 and +6","Split the middle term using them: 4x² − 10x + 6x − 15","Factorise the first pair and the second pair separately: 2x(2x − 5) + 3(2x − 5)","Both parts now share the bracket (2x − 5), so take it out: (2x − 5)(2x + 3)"],"cues":["Which two numbers in the expression have to be multiplied together first?","What must the pair of numbers do to that product and to the middle coefficient?","How is the −4x rewritten so there are four terms?","What is the highest common factor of each pair of terms?","What do the two parts have in common now?"]},{"title":"Multiplying two brackets that contain a surd","setup":"Expand and simplify (2 + √3)(5 − √3), giving your answer in the form a + b√3, where a and b are integers.","steps":["Multiply each term in the first bracket by each term in the second: 2 × 5, 2 × (−√3), √3 × 5 and √3 × (−√3)","That gives 10 − 2√3 + 5√3 − (√3)²","A square root multiplied by itself gives the number back, so (√3)² = 3","Collect the whole numbers and the surd terms separately: 10 − 3 = 7 and −2√3 + 5√3 = 3√3","So the expansion simplifies to 7 + 3√3"],"cues":["How many products are there when two two-term brackets are multiplied?","What is the product of the two numbers at the front of each bracket?","What happens to a square root multiplied by itself?","Which of the four terms are like terms?","How is the result written in the form the question asks for?"]},{"title":"Proving a statement about consecutive even numbers","setup":"Let n stand for any integer. Prove that the sum of any three consecutive even numbers is always a multiple of 6.","steps":["Write the three numbers algebraically: the first even number is 2n, the next is 2n + 2 and the one after is 2n + 4","Add them together: 2n + (2n + 2) + (2n + 4)","Collect like terms: 6n + 6","Take out the common factor: 6(n + 1)","6(n + 1) is 6 times an integer, so the sum of any three consecutive even numbers is a multiple of 6"],"cues":["How is an even number written using n, and how do you step to the next even number?","What does the statement 'the sum of' tell you to do with the three expressions?","How many n terms and how many units are there altogether?","What number divides both terms of that expression?","Why does this form prove the statement for every integer, not just one case?"]},{"title":"Composite and inverse functions in formal notation","setup":"Two functions are defined by f(x) = 2x + 7 and g(x) = x − 4. Work out gf(3), work out fg(3), and find an expression for f⁻¹(x).","steps":["gf means f acts first, so start with f(3) = 2 × 3 + 7 = 13","Feed that output into g: g(13) = 13 − 4 = 9, so gf(3) = 9","fg means g acts first: g(3) = 3 − 4 = −1, and then f(−1) = 2 × (−1) + 7 = 5, so fg(3) = 5","For the inverse, write y = 2x + 7, undo the + 7 to get y − 7 = 2x, then divide by 2 to get x = (y − 7)/2","Rewrite with x as the input: f⁻¹(x) = (x − 7)/2"],"cues":["In gf(3), which function is applied to the 3?","What is done with the number the first function produced?","Which function acts first in fg(3), and why is that the other way round?","Which operations have to be undone, and in what order?","How is the rearranged rule written in function notation?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Factorise 2x² + 11x + 5.","a":"(2x + 1)(x + 5)"},{"q":"Simplify fully (x² − 4)/(x² + 5x + 6).","a":"(x − 2)/(x + 3)"},{"q":"f(x) = 4x + 1 and g(x) = x². Work out fg(2).","a":"17"},{"q":"Prove that the sum of any two consecutive odd numbers is a multiple of 4.","a":"(2n + 1) + (2n + 3) = 4n + 4 = 4(n + 1), which is 4 times an integer, so the sum is always a multiple of 4"}]},"room":{"facts":["To factorise ax² + bx + c, find two numbers with product a × c and sum b, split the middle term into those two x terms, then factorise the four terms in pairs.","To simplify an algebraic fraction, factorise the numerator and the denominator fully and cancel a whole bracket that appears in both; a single term never cancels on its own.","The difference of two squares: x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a).","To expand three binomials, multiply two brackets first, then multiply that quadratic by each term of the remaining bracket and collect like terms.","√a × √a = a, so brackets containing surds expand in the ordinary way and the surd terms are collected at the end.","In an algebraic proof: n is any integer, 2n is even, 2n + 1 is odd, and consecutive integers are n, n + 1, n + 2; finish with a conclusion in words covering every value of n.","gf(x) means g(f(x)), so f is applied first; f⁻¹ undoes f and is found by writing y = f(x), making x the subject and rewriting the rule as f⁻¹(x) = …"],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Factorise 3x² + 10x + 8.","a":"(3x + 4)(x + 2)","sol":"Multiply the coefficient of x² by the constant: 3 × 8 = 24, and the middle coefficient is 10. Two numbers with product 24 and sum 10 are 6 and 4, so split the middle term: 3x² + 6x + 4x + 8 (M1). Factorise in pairs: 3x(x + 2) + 4(x + 2) = (3x + 4)(x + 2) (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The coefficient of x² is not 1, so the pair of numbers you look for is not simply a pair of factors of the constant term.","Find two numbers whose product is the coefficient of x² times the constant term and whose sum is the coefficient of x, then use them to split the middle term into two.","Work out the coefficient of x² multiplied by the constant term, and list the factor pairs of that product before splitting 10x."],"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The function f is defined by f(x) = 5x + 2. Find f⁻¹(x).","a":"f⁻¹(x) = (x − 2)/5","sol":"Write y = 5x + 2 and rearrange to make x the subject: y − 2 = 5x, so x = (y − 2)/5 (M1). Rewrite with x as the input, in function notation: f⁻¹(x) = (x − 2)/5 (A1). Total 2 marks.","hints":["The inverse is the function that undoes f, so think about what f does to an input and in what order.","Write y = f(x), then rearrange the equation so that x stands alone on one side.","Set the rule equal to y, then undo the addition first and the multiplication second, keeping both sides balanced."],"m":2,"d":1,"codes":["A7"]},{"q":"(calculator allowed) f(x) = 4x − 3 and g(x) = x + 5. Work out the value of gf(2).","a":"10","sol":"gf means f is applied first: f(2) = 4 × 2 − 3 = 5 (M1). Apply g to that output: g(5) = 5 + 5 = 10 (A1). Total 2 marks.","hints":["In a composite function written gf, the two functions are not used in the order they are read.","Work out the output of the function nearer the bracket first, then put that number into the other function.","Substitute the given input into f to get a single number before you go anywhere near g."],"m":2,"d":1,"codes":["A7"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Simplify fully (x² − 49)/(x² + 7x).","a":"(x − 7)/x","sol":"Factorise both parts: x² − 49 is a difference of two squares, so it is (x − 7)(x + 7), and x² + 7x = x(x + 7) (M1). The bracket (x + 7) appears above and below, so it cancels, leaving (x − 7)/x (A1). Total 2 marks.","hints":["An algebraic fraction only simplifies once both parts are written as products.","Factorise the numerator and the denominator, then look for a bracket common to both.","The numerator is a square subtract a square, and the denominator has a common factor in every term — factorise each before doing anything else."],"m":2,"d":1,"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Expand and simplify (2x + 1)(x + 3)(x − 2).","a":"2x³ + 3x² − 11x − 6","sol":"Multiply the first two brackets: (2x + 1)(x + 3) = 2x² + 6x + x + 3 = 2x² + 7x + 3 (M1). Multiply that by (x − 2): 2x³ + 7x² + 3x − 4x² − 14x − 6 (M1). Collect like terms: 2x³ + 3x² − 11x − 6 (A1). Total 3 marks.","hints":["Three brackets cannot be multiplied all at once; deal with them in stages.","Expand any two of the brackets and simplify, then multiply the quadratic you get by the bracket that is left.","Start by expanding the first two brackets and collecting the two x terms they produce."],"m":3,"d":2,"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Factorise 6x² + x − 12.","a":"(3x − 4)(2x + 3)","sol":"6 × (−12) = −72 and the middle coefficient is 1. Two numbers with product −72 and sum 1 are 9 and −8, so split the middle term: 6x² + 9x − 8x − 12 (M1). Factorise in pairs: 3x(2x + 3) − 4(2x + 3) = (3x − 4)(2x + 3) (A1). Total 2 marks.","hints":["The coefficient of x² is not 1, so use the splitting method rather than guessing a pair of brackets.","Find two numbers with product equal to the coefficient of x² times the constant term and sum equal to the coefficient of x.","Multiply 6 by −12 first, then hunt through the factor pairs of that product for the pair with the right sum."],"m":2,"d":2,"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Simplify fully (2x² + 5x − 3)/(x² + 6x + 9).","a":"(2x − 1)/(x + 3)","sol":"Factorise the numerator by splitting the middle term: 2 × (−3) = −6, and 6 and −1 have product −6 and sum 5, so 2x² + 6x − x − 3 = 2x(x + 3) − 1(x + 3) = (2x − 1)(x + 3) (M1). Factorise the denominator: x² + 6x + 9 = (x + 3)(x + 3) (M1). Cancel the common bracket (x + 3): (2x − 1)/(x + 3) (A1). Total 3 marks.","hints":["Neither part of this fraction can be cancelled until both are written as products of brackets.","Factorise the numerator with the splitting method, and look at the denominator for a repeated bracket.","Multiply the coefficient of x² by the constant term in the numerator, then find the pair of numbers that splits its middle term."],"m":3,"d":2,"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Expand and simplify (3 + √5)(4 − 2√5), giving your answer in the form a + b√5 where a and b are integers.","a":"2 − 2√5","sol":"Multiply each term in the first bracket by each term in the second: 12 − 6√5 + 4√5 − 2(√5)² (M1). Use √5 × √5 = 5, so the last term is −2 × 5 = −10 (M1). Collect: 12 − 10 = 2 and −6√5 + 4√5 = −2√5, giving 2 − 2√5 (A1). Total 3 marks.","hints":["Brackets containing surds expand exactly like any other pair of brackets.","Write out all four products first, then deal with the term in which a root is multiplied by itself.","Multiply each term of the first bracket by each term of the second and set the four products down before simplifying any of them."],"m":3,"d":2,"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"(calculator allowed) f(x) = 3x + 2 and g(x) = x² − 1. Express fg(x) in its simplest form.","a":"fg(x) = 3x² − 1","sol":"fg means g is applied first, so the input to f is x² − 1: fg(x) = 3(x² − 1) + 2 (M1). Expand and collect: 3x² − 3 + 2 = 3x² − 1 (A1). Total 2 marks.","hints":["In fg(x) the two functions are not applied in the order in which the letters are written.","Work out which function acts first, then substitute its whole rule in place of x in the other function.","Replace the x in the rule of f by the entire expression that g produces, keeping it inside brackets."],"m":2,"d":2,"codes":["A7"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Prove that the sum of the squares of any two consecutive integers is always an odd number.","a":"n² + (n + 1)² = 2n² + 2n + 1 = 2(n² + n) + 1, which is one more than an even number, so the sum is always odd","sol":"Let the two consecutive integers be n and n + 1, where n is any integer (B1). Sum of the squares: n² + (n + 1)² = n² + n² + 2n + 1 = 2n² + 2n + 1 (M1). Write this as 2(n² + n) + 1, which is twice an integer plus 1, so the sum is one more than an even number and is therefore odd for every integer n (A1). Total 3 marks.","hints":["Trying a few pairs of numbers shows the statement works for those pairs only, so start from a letter that stands for any integer.","Write the two integers in terms of n, square each of them and add, then expand and collect the terms.","Give the smaller of the two integers a letter, write the larger one in terms of that same letter, then set down the sum of their two squares before expanding anything."],"m":3,"d":2,"codes":["A6"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Prove that (n + 3)² − (n − 3)² is a multiple of 12 for all integer values of n.","a":"(n + 3)² − (n − 3)² = 12n, which is 12 times an integer, so it is always a multiple of 12","sol":"Expand both squares: (n + 3)² = n² + 6n + 9 and (n − 3)² = n² − 6n + 9 (M1). Subtract, taking care with the signs: n² + 6n + 9 − n² + 6n − 9 = 12n (A1). 12n is 12 times an integer, so the expression is a multiple of 12 for every integer n (A1). Total 3 marks.","hints":["The two brackets are squares, so nothing can be said about the difference until each one is multiplied out in full.","Expand each square separately, then subtract the second expansion from the first, remembering that every term of it changes sign.","Write out (n + 3)(n + 3) and (n − 3)(n − 3) as expansions before attempting the subtraction."],"m":3,"d":2,"codes":["A6"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Simplify fully (2x² − 8)/(x² + x − 6).","a":"2(x + 2)/(x + 3)","sol":"Take the common factor out of the numerator: 2x² − 8 = 2(x² − 4) (M1). x² − 4 is a difference of two squares, so the numerator is 2(x − 2)(x + 2) (A1). Factorise the denominator: two numbers with product −6 and sum 1 are 3 and −2, so x² + x − 6 = (x + 3)(x − 2) (M1). Cancel the common bracket (x − 2): 2(x + 2)/(x + 3) (A1). Total 4 marks.","hints":["Fully means the numerator may need more than one stage of factorising before anything cancels.","Take out the numerical common factor of the numerator first, then factorise what is left inside the bracket and factorise the denominator.","Look at the numerator for a factor common to both terms, and take it outside a bracket before going any further."],"m":4,"d":3,"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"(calculator allowed) f(x) = 2x − 3 and g(x) = x². Solve gf(x) = 25.","a":"x = 4 or x = −1","sol":"gf means f first, so gf(x) = (2x − 3)², giving the equation (2x − 3)² = 25 (M1). Take the square root of both sides, keeping both signs: 2x − 3 = 5 or 2x − 3 = −5 (M1). From the first, 2x = 8 so x = 4 (A1). From the second, 2x = −2 so x = −1 (A1). Total 4 marks. (Expanding to 4x² − 12x − 16 = 0 and factorising scores the same marks.)","hints":["Build the composite rule as an expression in x before trying to solve anything.","Apply f to x, square the result because that is what g does, then set the squared bracket equal to the given value.","Write down gf(x) as a squared bracket and form the equation; remember a square can come from a positive or a negative quantity."],"m":4,"d":3,"codes":["A7"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Prove that the product of any two consecutive odd numbers is always one less than a multiple of 4.","a":"(2n + 1)(2n + 3) = 4n² + 8n + 3 = 4(n + 1)² − 1, which is one less than a multiple of 4","sol":"Let the two consecutive odd numbers be 2n + 1 and 2n + 3, where n is any integer (B1). Their product is (2n + 1)(2n + 3) = 4n² + 6n + 2n + 3 = 4n² + 8n + 3 (M1). Rewrite this with 4 as a factor: 4n² + 8n + 3 = 4(n² + 2n + 1) − 1 = 4(n + 1)² − 1 (M1). Since (n + 1)² is an integer, 4(n + 1)² is a multiple of 4, so the product is always one less than a multiple of 4 (A1). Total 4 marks.","hints":["Odd numbers have a standard algebraic form, and the next odd number after one of them is two more.","Write both numbers in terms of n, expand the product, then rearrange the result so that 4 is a factor of all but a constant.","Give the smaller odd number its general algebraic form, write the next odd number in terms of the same letter, then set down their product before expanding."],"m":4,"d":3,"codes":["A6"]},{"q":"(calculator allowed) A rectangle has area (6x² + 11x − 10) cm² and length (3x − 2) cm. Find an expression, in terms of x, for the width of the rectangle, and hence work out the perimeter of the rectangle when x = 3.","a":"Width = (2x + 5) cm; perimeter = 36 cm","sol":"The length is one factor of the area, so factorise the area: 6 × (−10) = −60 and 15 and −4 have product −60 and sum 11, giving 6x² + 15x − 4x − 10 = 3x(2x + 5) − 2(2x + 5) (M1) = (3x − 2)(2x + 5), so the width is (2x + 5) cm (A1). Perimeter = 2[(3x − 2) + (2x + 5)] = 2(5x + 3) = (10x + 6) cm (M1). Substitute x = 3: 10 × 3 + 6 (M1) = 36 cm (A1). Total 5 marks.","hints":["Area of a rectangle is length × width, so the given length must divide the area exactly.","Factorise the area into two brackets, identify which one is the width, then build the perimeter expression before substituting.","Multiply the coefficient of x² by the constant term of the area and find the pair of numbers that splits the middle term."],"m":5,"d":3,"codes":["A4"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Factorise 2x² + 9x + 4.","a":"(2x + 1)(x + 4)"},{"q":"Simplify fully (x² − 16)/(x² − 3x − 4).","a":"(x + 4)/(x + 1)"},{"q":"f(x) = 6x − 5. Find f⁻¹(x).","a":"f⁻¹(x) = (x + 5)/6"},{"q":"Expand and simplify (x + 1)(x + 2)(x + 3).","a":"x³ + 6x² + 11x + 6"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Factorising ax² + bx + c","back":"Find two numbers with product a × c and sum b, split the middle term into those two x terms, factorise the four terms in pairs and take out the common bracket.","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Simplifying an algebraic fraction","back":"Factorise the numerator and the denominator fully, then cancel a bracket that appears in both. Whole factors cancel; single terms never do.","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Difference of two squares","back":"x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a). It is often the factorisation hiding in one part of an algebraic fraction, for example x² − 25 = (x + 5)(x − 5).","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Expanding a product of three binomials","back":"Multiply two brackets and simplify, then multiply that quadratic by each term of the third bracket and collect like terms. The result is a cubic.","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Multiplying brackets that contain surds","back":"Expand as usual and use √a × √a = a. For example (1 + √2)(3 + √2) = 3 + √2 + 3√2 + 2 = 5 + 4√2.","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Checking a factorisation","back":"Expand the brackets again: the expansion must reproduce every term of the original expression, including the sign of the constant.","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Function notation f(x)","back":"f(x) is the output of the function f for the input x. f(3) means substitute x = 3 into the rule; the f names the function and is not a multiplier.","code":"A7","open":false},{"front":"Composite function gf(x)","back":"gf(x) = g(f(x)): apply f first and put its output into g. Order matters, so fg(x) is usually not the same as gf(x).","code":"A7","open":false},{"front":"Inverse function f⁻¹(x)","back":"The function that undoes f: if f(a) = b then f⁻¹(b) = a. Find it by writing y = f(x), making x the subject, then rewriting the rule as f⁻¹(x) = …","code":"A7","open":false},{"front":"Representing numbers algebraically in a proof","back":"n is any integer; 2n is even; 2n + 1 is odd; consecutive integers are n, n + 1, n + 2; consecutive even numbers are 2n, 2n + 2, 2n + 4.","code":"A6","open":false},{"front":"The conclusion line of an algebraic proof","back":"After simplifying, state in words that the expression has the required form — a multiple of 3, one less than a multiple of 4 — and that this holds for every value of n.","code":"A6","open":false},{"front":"Testing numbers is not a proof","back":"Checking examples shows only that the statement works for the values tried. A proof uses a letter for the general case, so the conclusion covers all values.","code":"A6","open":false},{"front":"Showing an expression is a multiple of k","back":"Expand and collect, then take k out as a common factor, leaving an integer expression inside the bracket: 6n + 6 = 6(n + 1) is a multiple of 6.","code":"A6","open":false},{"front":"Name one way to write an even number and one way to write an odd number in an algebraic proof","back":"2n is even; 2n + 2 is the next even number; 2n + 1 or 2n − 1 is odd, where n stands for any integer.","code":"A6","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Preamble: none of this room's own results — the factorising rules, the rule for cancelling an algebraic fraction, the proof forms and the function notation — is printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid (W87991A), the formulae insert in every Higher paper, so everything marked Remember below must be written from memory. The items marked Printed are the whole of that sheet; the quadratic formula it prints solves ax² + bx + c = 0, it does not factorise the expression, so it is a check on a factorisation rather than a substitute for one.","Remember: to factorise ax² + bx + c, find two numbers with product a × c and sum b, split the middle term and factorise the four terms in pairs. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: x² + bx + c = (x + p)(x + q), where p × q = c and p + q = b. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: the difference of two squares, x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a). Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: to simplify an algebraic fraction, factorise the numerator and the denominator fully and cancel a bracket common to both. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: to expand three binomials, multiply two brackets first and then multiply that quadratic by each term of the third bracket. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: √a × √a = a, the step that closes a bracket expansion containing surds. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: an even number is written 2n, for any integer n. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: an odd number is written 2n + 1 (or 2n − 1). Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: consecutive integers are written n, n + 1, n + 2, and consecutive even numbers 2n, 2n + 2, 2n + 4. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: an algebraic proof ends with a conclusion in words stating that the result holds for every value of n. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: gf(x) = g(f(x)) — the function nearer the bracket acts first. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: to find f⁻¹, write y = f(x), make x the subject, then write the answer as f⁻¹(x) = … Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: the quadratic formula, x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/2a, for ax² + bx + c = 0 where a ≠ 0.","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h, where a and b are the parallel sides and h their perpendicular separation.","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: volume of a prism = area of cross section × length.","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd, where r is the radius and d the diameter.","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: area of a circle = πr², where r is the radius.","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: Pythagoras' theorem, a² + b² = c², in a right-angled triangle whose hypotenuse is c.","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: sin A = a/c, in a right-angled triangle ABC whose hypotenuse is c and whose side a is opposite angle A.","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: cos A = b/c, where b is the side adjacent to angle A and c the hypotenuse.","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: tan A = a/b, where a is opposite angle A and b adjacent to it.","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: the sine rule, a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C, in any triangle ABC.","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: the cosine rule, a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A, in any triangle ABC.","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: area of a triangle = ½ab sin C.","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, where P is the principal, r the interest rate per period and n the number of periods.","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B).","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: P(A and B) = P(A given B) P(B)."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T12:07:11.826177+00:00","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.93,"notes":"All 15 bank questions, the 4 exit-ticket items, the 4 lesson check items and all 6 worked examples re-solved in sympy and every answer key, solution line and mark tally (M/A/B) is correct, with 11 of 15 questions non-calculator and the Higher Exam Aid claims exact — publish as is, optionally splitting the rectangle question's two-part answer key."}}}} };
